const documents=[{"url":"checked-exercise.html","title":"跑通销售报表，再核对每一列","text":"跑通销售报表，再核对每一列 本练习已通过严格编译、源键验证和实际执行，两行六列与标准结果一致。 使用教程同一组八条销售数据；独立练习模型明确指定 DuckDB 方言，不改写原 SQL Server 项目和历史记录。 下载与前提 下载可复现练习 。你需要已有的 dmlc.exe 、Python 和 duckdb==1.5.5 。压缩包不含编译器或第三方运行时。解压到一个新的可写目录，脚本只在该目录创建练习库及配置，不连接业务数据库。 py -m pip install duckdb==1.5.5 py verify.py C:/path/to/dmlc.exe 已有 practice.duckdb 时脚本停止；再次复现请重新解压到新目录。脚本保存版本、编译器 SHA-256、退出码、诊断及执行结果。 应看到什么？ compile、validate、execute 退出码均为 0，最后 result_matches 为 true。脚本独立检查行数、唯一地区键、列集合、NULL 和未格式化数值。 地区 销售额 已完成 上月 客户平均 全地区占比 North 30 10 20 15 0.3 South 70 70 50 70 0.7 验证范围 本次记录：dmlc 0.3.16，构建 2026-11-06 02:59:12 Z，Windows/amd64；指纹见下载包 verification.json。实际使用的 Python DuckDB 版本见同一记录。结果与 expected.csv 逐键逐列比较，销售额和已完成销售额另外用独立 SQL 核对。 这里只确认该数据、模型、查询和 DuckDB 路径；不代表其他数据库都已验证。原 互动项目 的编译观察保持原样。遇到差异保留诊断，按 排错步骤 处理。"},{"url":"docs.html","title":"DML and DMX documentation","text":"DML and DMX documentation Define and compose reusable business metrics with DML . Write formulas and adjust a measure&#39;s calculation with DMX . Ask questions of the model with Model Query . A measure can reference other measures, including measures from different datasets at compatible grains. Applying a time rule to a ratio recalculates its components in that period; it does not average displayed percentages. The profitability example follows one definition through these changes. New to DML? Open the overview for the idea, then follow the interactive journey : twelve illustrated lessons with exercises in English, 中文, 日本語 and Deutsch. Core reference pages are in English; the problem guides below are available in English and Chinese. Continue from the journey When you want to… Look up… Choose a lookup, prepared source relation or cross-dataset metric composition Source relations and composition Reuse a calculation rule across metrics Custom modifiers and database functions Define a dataset and its source-row key (chapters 1–2) Core concepts and dimensions Reuse a calculation (chapters 3–4) Measure declarations and Model Query SQL Change filters, grain or time (chapters 6–9) Measure modifiers Check lookups, missing values and results (chapters 5, 10–12) Compile and validate For a second worked example, follow profitability from definitions to cumulative comparison. Find a task 中文文档导航 · Checked sales exercise · Materialization workflow · Troubleshooting and feedback Concepts Core concepts — how the model, query and expression layers fit together. Dimensions — source-row identity, report grouping and evaluation grain. Measure-as-dimension — calculate a label per entity, then group by it. Window placement — choose the stage at which a window calculation runs. Text introduction — a short companion to the interactive journey. Language reference The reference index covers model declarations, measures, DMX expressions, modifiers, SQL and YAML queries, materialization and model composition. Examples Runnable task examples · 中文 include source data, complete models, queries, expected results and replay instructions for lookups, time context, running totals and fixed denominators. Browse the example catalog for models, queries, aggregation, measure formulas and advanced calculations. Each example states the definitions it needs. Your question Start here Which price was valid when a transaction happened? Effective-date lookup Can an id repeat in different regions? Compound keys Can a running total be a reusable metric? Named running sales Does a February KPI include January? Time context and sibling measures Should a denominator retain the report filter? Fixed totals Which database dialect can I target, and what was executed? Dialect and execution scope Can my application bind query parameters? Physical and semantic query binding What does checking a model prove about column types? Declaration schemas and SQL value types Can I explore a metric before saving its definition? Inline measures How do I investigate a change or reuse existing SQL? Contribution analysis and SQL recovery How should a business day handle daylight saving? DuckDB timezone recipe Usage guides Compile your first query . Use the interactive journey and its downloadable project . Build an analysis and design reusable measures . Extend DML with custom modifiers and database functions and design materializations . Write model metadata and review a model . Compile and validate , troubleshoot a query , or look up an error code . A successful compile produces SQL to inspect. Validate source keys on the intended connection and compare result values separately. Solve an analytical question Why you cannot average monthly gross margins · 中文 . Why a region filter changes the share denominator · 中文 . Why duplicate dimension keys can double sales · 中文 ."},{"url":"docs.zh.html","title":"DML 文档：按问题找答案","text":"DML 文档：按问题找答案 先选要完成的事，再查语法。 标注“英文”的链接通向完整参考，不代表已有中文翻译。 DML 是可独立使用的语义层编译器，保存可复用指标并生成 SQL，由数据库执行。它检查模型结构、粒度与上下文；业务口径、结果正确性和访问权限仍需使用者、数据库与应用负责。 按业务问题运行完整案例 关联、累计与分母案例集 提供数据、完整模型、查询、预期输出和可重跑校验。 开始与验证 十二章互动教程 ：浏览器内学习。 运行销售练习并核对结果 ：已有编译器的用户从固定数据开始。 编译第一个查询（英文） 。 验证键声明并执行（英文） 。 排错与反馈准备 。 我想解决什么问题？ 问题 文档 交易发生时哪条价格或等级有效？ 有效期查找 不同地区重复编号怎样关联？ 复合键 累计指标怎样定义一次再复用？ 有序累计 单个累计 KPI 用哪个截止点？ 时间上下文 只显示一月，分母要包含其他月份吗？ 分母范围 查找属性、准备源关系、组合独立指标有什么区别？ 源关系与指标组合（英文） 能面向哪些数据库，是否都执行验证过？ 方言与验证范围（英文） 应用能绑定查询参数吗？ 物理 SQL 与语义查询的参数边界（英文） 模型检查能证明列值类型正确吗？ 声明结构与 SQL 值类型（英文） 怎样调查指标变化，或从已有 SQL 开始建模？ 贡献分析与 SQL 恢复（英文） 业务日如何处理夏令时？ DuckDB 配方 同比、上月、累计怎么计算？ 修饰符（英文） 筛选地区后分母怎样保持全体？ 占比分母 先按客户汇总再平均？ 中间粒度（英文） 怎样计算累计毛利率？ 累计毛利率 维度重复导致多行怎么办？ 重复键验证 NULL、零分母怎样处理？ 表达式与函数（英文） 怎样声明临时指标？ 查询及内联指标（英文） 怎样确认预计算命中和刷新？ 物化运行与验证 先确认集成需要的能力 需要应用传入筛选参数时，先确认语义查询的绑定方式。已测物理 SQL 的 JDBC ? 可绑定，但 FROM MODEL 查询的占位符解析失败。物化参数和内部字面量参数化不能替代这个接口，也不能用不可信文本拼接补齐。 按目标数据库显式设置 dialect: ；遗漏时已测默认是 mssql ，连接配置不会代替该声明。识别方言不等于各引擎所有功能都执行验证过。列值类型由数据库定义，已测列声明不接受 type: ，编译通过仍可能出现数据库类型错误。 累计指标要区分时间范围与筛选后行窗口。二月的窗口累计不会自动包含一月；日历累计则要明确截止点和输出期间。键验证也不保证与后续执行使用同一数据快照。根据上表的对应指南验证边界，再对照完整结果。 扩展自己的计算规则 可以扩展修饰符，也可以调用数据库中的标量函数。 自定义 fn-macro 包装指标公式， dt-macro 调整时间上下文，并按同一语法组合。数据库函数的实现、部署和权限由数据库负责；编译通过不代表函数存在，应在目标连接上验证。 完整定义、实测结果与边界（英文） 术语与完整参考 中文 参考中的名称 数据集、源行键 dataset、dimension 原子指标、复合指标 atomic/composite measure 查询分组、计算粒度 grouping、evaluation grain 筛选、修饰符 filter、modifier 物化、预先存储的汇总 materialization 完整参考（英文） · 所有示例（英文） · 英文导航"},{"url":"examples--compound-key-en.html","title":"Look up an id that repeats across regions","text":"Look up an id that repeats across regions Declare the complete business key and supply lookup arguments in declaration order. id=1 can exist in both east and west; the (id, region) combination must be unique. Inspect the input and result The target maps (1,east) to East and (1,west) to West. Sales are 10 and 20 respectively. The query returns: Label Sales East 10 West 20 Joining on id alone cannot distinguish these target rows. This small fixture isolates that boundary; the unit of currency does not change the result. Define the lookup once The target declares dimension: [id, region] . The fact&#39;s label is pk({id}, {region}).{D}.label , with arguments in the same order as the target dimension. 完整文件 / Complete file: compound/model.dml dialect: duckdb D: type: dataset table: DIM dimension: [id, region] columns: id: region: label: F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [fid] columns: fid: id: region: amount: label: &#39;pk({id}, {region}).{D}.label&#39; measures: sales: SUM({amount}) 完整文件 / Complete file: compound/setup.sql CREATE TABLE DIM(id INTEGER, region VARCHAR, label VARCHAR); INSERT INTO DIM VALUES (1,&#39;east&#39;,&#39;East&#39;),(1,&#39;west&#39;,&#39;West&#39;); CREATE TABLE FACT(fid INTEGER,id INTEGER,region VARCHAR,amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO FACT VALUES (1,1,&#39;east&#39;,10),(2,1,&#39;west&#39;,20); 完整文件 / Complete file: compound/normal.sql SELECT label, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY label ORDER BY label The query reuses this modeled lookup when grouping by label. You do not repeat the join condition in every consumer. Identify your actual complete business key before adapting id, region . Check the key after a source change The verifier compiles, validates and executes the normal data. It then inserts a duplicate (1,east) and expects --validate-only to report DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE . After removing the duplicate, validation and complete result comparison pass again. Validation detects a mismatch between the declared key and current data before you trust lookup results. It does not guarantee uniqueness of future writes. Repair the source, complete key or data; adding DISTINCT to the final output cannot reliably undo an inflated aggregate. 中文 · Task examples · Compile and validate Run and interpret the checks Download the complete project and run verify.py as described in its README. It needs your existing dmlc, Python and DuckDB. The script creates a practice database only in the new output directory you specify. The shared verifier records results and diagnostics there. This example was checked on DuckDB with dmlc 0.3.16, build 2026-11-13. The executable fingerprint and case statuses are in verification-summary.json in the download. Database validation and subsequent execution are independent operations; they do not guarantee a shared snapshot. Verify other engines separately."},{"url":"examples--compound-key-zh.html","title":"同一编号在不同地区重复，怎样正确关联？","text":"同一编号在不同地区重复，怎样正确关联？ 将完整业务键声明为复合键，并按声明顺序查找。 id=1 可以同时出现在 east 和 west；必须唯一的是 (id, region) 组合。 先看输入和结果 目标表中 (1,east) 对应 East， (1,west) 对应 West。两条销售分别为10和20。查询应得到： 标签 销售额 East 10 West 20 只按 id 关联不足以区分这两条目标记录。示例使用最小夹具突出这个边界，金额单位不影响结果。 声明一次，在查询中复用 模型的目标数据集声明 dimension: [id, region] 。事实表中的标签定义为 pk({id}, {region}).{D}.label ；参数顺序与目标 dimension 一致。 完整文件 / Complete file: compound/model.dml dialect: duckdb D: type: dataset table: DIM dimension: [id, region] columns: id: region: label: F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [fid] columns: fid: id: region: amount: label: &#39;pk({id}, {region}).{D}.label&#39; measures: sales: SUM({amount}) 完整文件 / Complete file: compound/setup.sql CREATE TABLE DIM(id INTEGER, region VARCHAR, label VARCHAR); INSERT INTO DIM VALUES (1,&#39;east&#39;,&#39;East&#39;),(1,&#39;west&#39;,&#39;West&#39;); CREATE TABLE FACT(fid INTEGER,id INTEGER,region VARCHAR,amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO FACT VALUES (1,1,&#39;east&#39;,10),(2,1,&#39;west&#39;,20); 完整文件 / Complete file: compound/normal.sql SELECT label, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY label ORDER BY label 业务标签查找定义在模型中。查询按标签分组时复用它，不必再次手写关联条件。换成自己的数据时，应确认实际复合键，而不是机械复制 id, region 。 数据变更后检查键声明 校验脚本按顺序执行：正常数据编译、验证、查询通过；插入重复 (1,east) 后， --validate-only 检出 DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE ；删除重复数据并恢复唯一组合后，重新验证和结果比对通过。 这说明验证能够发现键声明与当前数据的不一致，帮助在信任结果前避免关联放大。它不证明以后写入的数据仍唯一。实际业务应修正来源、完整键或数据，在最终结果加 DISTINCT 不能可靠修复已经放大的聚合。 运行与验证范围 下载完整项目 ，按包内 README.md 运行 verify.py 。需要已有的 dmlc、Python 和 DuckDB；脚本只在你指定的全新输出目录创建练习库。所有案例共用一个入口，结果与诊断保存在输出目录。 本例在 dmlc 0.3.16、2026-11-13 构建的 DuckDB 连接上验证。编译器指纹、用例列表和状态见包内 verification-summary.json。数据库验证和随后执行是独立操作，不保证同一快照。其他引擎需单独验证。 返回案例目录 · 编译与验证（英文） English"},{"url":"examples--duckdb-timezone-en.html","title":"Define a business day in a fixed DuckDB timezone","text":"Define a business day in a fixed DuckDB timezone Map instants to the business timezone before deriving a date or day boundary. This recipe uses America/New_York and tests DuckDB physical SQL. It does not establish automatic per-user or cross-database timezone support in DML. Identify what the input represents Input values are TIMESTAMPTZ instants with explicit UTC offsets. The connection&#39;s session zone is UTC; the business zone is America/New_York. A timestamp without an offset is not automatically UTC. Establish how the source records it first. SET TimeZone = &#39;UTC&#39;; SELECT timezone(&#39;America/New_York&#39;, TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-01-01 02:00:00+00&#39;); The local time is 2023-12-31 21:00:00, so the business date is the previous day. Convert to the business zone before extracting a grouping date. Bound a day with two local midnights SELECT count(*) FROM (VALUES (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-10 04:59:59+00&#39;), (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-10 05:00:00+00&#39;), (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-11 03:59:59+00&#39;), (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-11 04:00:00+00&#39;) ) events(ts) WHERE ts &gt;= timezone(&#39;America/New_York&#39;, TIMESTAMP &#39;2024-03-10 00:00:00&#39;) AND ts &lt; timezone(&#39;America/New_York&#39;, TIMESTAMP &#39;2024-03-11 00:00:00&#39;); The result is 2. Include the start and exclude the end. The end is not always the start plus 24 hours: the verifier checks a 23-hour spring transition day and a 25-hour autumn transition day. Preserve distinct instants during a repeated local hour 2024-11-03 05:30 UTC and 06:30 UTC both map to local 01:30. They are distinct instants. Retain the event key and original instant rather than deduplicating by local timestamp alone. Replay and adapt the recipe Download the project and follow its README&#39;s JDBC steps to run jdbc/VerifyJdbc.java . With the existing JDK and matching installed jars, run from the extracted project directory: java -cp &quot;C:/path/to/dmlc-assembly.jar;C:/path/to/duckdb-jdbc.jar&quot; jdbc/VerifyJdbc.java &quot;$PWD/jdbc&quot; C:/path/to/driver-directory Replace the paths; use a colon instead of a semicolon in the classpath on other systems. The program also tests the parameter-binding boundary, so expected semantic-placeholder parsing errors appear in the output. Check the final pass messages and process exit rather than treating every diagnostic as a failed replay. It does not download third-party runtimes. The verifier checks midnight, repeated hours, 23/25-hour days and half-open interval filtering. Failures exit with a nonzero code. The checked JDBC distribution uses the DuckDB 1.5.4 driver. To reuse a business date in DML, a database view can normalize timestamps first and expose the business date as a model column. Verify that integration on your model and connection; this physical SQL recipe does not establish arbitrary timezone-aware models. 中文 · Task examples · Compile and validate"},{"url":"examples--duckdb-timezone-zh.html","title":"DuckDB：按固定业务时区划分一天","text":"DuckDB：按固定业务时区划分一天 先把时刻映射到业务时区，再定义日期和一天的边界。 本配方固定使用 America/New_York，只验证 DuckDB 的物理 SQL，不宣称 DML 提供跨数据库或每个用户可切换的时区能力。 明确输入的含义 输入使用带明确 UTC 偏移的 TIMESTAMPTZ ，表示实际时刻；连接会话设为 UTC。业务时区是 America/New_York。没有偏移的时间戳不能直接当作 UTC；需要先确认源系统如何记录它。 SET TimeZone = &#39;UTC&#39;; SELECT timezone(&#39;America/New_York&#39;, TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-01-01 02:00:00+00&#39;); 得到当地时间2023-12-31 21:00:00，业务日期属于前一天。按业务日期分组时，先做同样的转换，再取日期。 用两个当地零点界定一天 SELECT count(*) FROM (VALUES (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-10 04:59:59+00&#39;), (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-10 05:00:00+00&#39;), (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-11 03:59:59+00&#39;), (TIMESTAMPTZ &#39;2024-03-11 04:00:00+00&#39;) ) events(ts) WHERE ts &gt;= timezone(&#39;America/New_York&#39;, TIMESTAMP &#39;2024-03-10 00:00:00&#39;) AND ts &lt; timezone(&#39;America/New_York&#39;, TIMESTAMP &#39;2024-03-11 00:00:00&#39;); 结果为2。起点包含、终点不包含；不要假定结束时间总是起点加24小时。验证中，春季切换日为23小时，秋季切换日为25小时。 重复的当地时间不是重复的时刻 2024-11-03 05:30 UTC 和06:30 UTC 均映射为当地01:30。这两个时刻不同，不能只凭当地日期时间去重；保留事件主键和原始时刻。 复现和复用 下载项目 ，按 README 的 JDBC 步骤运行 jdbc/VerifyJdbc.java 。验证器检查跨午夜、重复小时、23/25小时日和半开区间筛选的实际结果；失败会以非零退出。 本次使用 JDBC 分发中的 DuckDB 1.5.4 驱动。若要在 DML 数据集中复用业务日期，可先用经过验证的数据库视图完成时区归一化，再把业务日期作为模型列；这个接入步骤仍需在你的模型和连接中验证。不要将此配方描述为已验证的任意时区模型。 返回案例目录 English"},{"url":"examples--fixed-total-en.html","title":"Choose which months belong in a denominator","text":"Choose which months belong in a denominator -d retains the month filter; -df removes it for that measure. Both can display January only. Check the denominator&#39;s population separately from the output row population. Compare the same data Region a has January sales 10 and February sales 20. Region b has January sales 30. After selecting January: Region Month Sales -d region total -df region total a 2024-01-01 10 10 30 b 2024-01-01 30 30 30 Region a&#39;s share can therefore be 10/10 or 10/30, depending on the business definition. Without a month filter both region totals are 30; a&#39;s January and February rows retain sales 10 and 20 respectively. Save both definitions, then change the query The model defines region_selected: &#39;{sales: {-d: month}}&#39; and region_all_months: &#39;{sales: {-df: month}}&#39; . Here month is an ordinary ISO-formatted string, not the automatic time context dim_dt . 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/model.dml dialect: duckdb F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [id] columns: id: region: month: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) region_selected: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-d: month}}&#39; region_all_months: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-df: month}}&#39; running_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({sales}) OVER (ORDER BY {month} ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)&#39; dimensions: - [month] 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/setup.sql CREATE TABLE FACT(id INTEGER,region VARCHAR, month VARCHAR,amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO FACT VALUES (1,&#39;a&#39;,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,10),(2,&#39;a&#39;,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,20),(3,&#39;b&#39;,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,30); 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/january.sql SELECT region, month, AGG(sales), AGG(region_selected), AGG(region_all_months) FROM MODEL WHERE month=&#39;2024-01-01&#39; GROUP BY region,month ORDER BY region,month 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/unfiltered.sql SELECT region, month, AGG(sales), AGG(region_selected), AGG(region_all_months) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region, month ORDER BY region,month The query chooses which months to display without changing the model. The verifier compares every column and the full row set, checking that -df does not restore February output rows. Keep the demonstrated boundary This example uses known region/month groupings, not dynamic pivot axes or an automatic subtotal API. Ordinary-field behavior does not verify removal of a dim_dt time grain. For time queries, read the cumulative KPI context before substituting fields. 中文 · Task examples · Compile and validate Run and interpret the checks Download the complete project and run verify.py as described in its README. It needs your existing dmlc, Python and DuckDB. The script creates a practice database only in the new output directory you specify. The shared verifier records results and diagnostics there. This example was checked on DuckDB with dmlc 0.3.16, build 2026-11-13. The executable fingerprint and case statuses are in verification-summary.json in the download. Database validation and subsequent execution are independent operations; they do not guarantee a shared snapshot. Verify other engines separately."},{"url":"examples--fixed-total-zh.html","title":"只显示一月时，分母应保留哪些月份？","text":"只显示一月时，分母应保留哪些月份？ -d 保留月份筛选， -df 在该指标计算中移除它。 两者都可以只输出一月，分母计算范围与报表输出行范围需要分别检查。 用同一份数据比较 地区 a 一月销售10、二月20；地区 b 一月销售30。筛选一月后： 地区 月份 销售额 -d 地区总额 -df 地区总额 a 2024-01-01 10 10 30 b 2024-01-01 30 30 30 因此 a 的占比可以是10/10，也可以是10/30；需要由业务口径决定。未筛选月份时，两种地区总额都是30，a 的一月、二月各占自己的销售额。 保存两种口径，再改变查询 模型定义 region_selected: &#39;{sales: {-d: month}}&#39; 和 region_all_months: &#39;{sales: {-df: month}}&#39; 。本例 month 是普通字符串字段，值按 ISO 日期排列，不是自动时间上下文 dim_dt 。 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/model.dml dialect: duckdb F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [id] columns: id: region: month: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) region_selected: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-d: month}}&#39; region_all_months: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-df: month}}&#39; running_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({sales}) OVER (ORDER BY {month} ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)&#39; dimensions: - [month] 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/setup.sql CREATE TABLE FACT(id INTEGER,region VARCHAR, month VARCHAR,amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO FACT VALUES (1,&#39;a&#39;,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,10),(2,&#39;a&#39;,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,20),(3,&#39;b&#39;,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,30); 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/january.sql SELECT region, month, AGG(sales), AGG(region_selected), AGG(region_all_months) FROM MODEL WHERE month=&#39;2024-01-01&#39; GROUP BY region,month ORDER BY region,month 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/unfiltered.sql SELECT region, month, AGG(sales), AGG(region_selected), AGG(region_all_months) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region, month ORDER BY region,month 模型不变，查询决定报表显示哪些月份。验证器比较所有列与完整行集合，确保 -df 没有把二月输出行带回来。 哪些结论不能直接推广？ 这里是已知的地区、月份分组，不是动态透视轴或自动小计 API。普通字段的结果也不能代替对 dim_dt 时间粒度移除的验证。对于时间查询，先看 累计 KPI 的上下文 ，不要照搬字段替换。 运行与验证范围 下载完整项目 ，按包内 README.md 运行 verify.py 。需要已有的 dmlc、Python 和 DuckDB；脚本只在你指定的全新输出目录创建练习库。所有案例共用一个入口，结果与诊断保存在输出目录。 本例在 dmlc 0.3.16、2026-11-13 构建的 DuckDB 连接上验证。编译器指纹、用例列表和状态见包内 verification-summary.json。数据库验证和随后执行是独立操作，不保证同一快照。其他引擎需单独验证。 返回案例目录 · 编译与验证（英文） English"},{"url":"examples--index-en.html","title":"Choose a runnable example by business question","text":"Choose a runnable example by business question Each example includes input data, complete model definitions, queries, expected results and replay instructions. Each guide explains the calculation, its applicable scope and what to check before adapting it. 中文 Your question Runnable guide How do I distinguish an id that repeats across regions? Compound keys and uniqueness Which price or category was valid when an event happened? Effective-date range lookup What cutoff should a single cumulative KPI use? Explicit time context Can I define a running sales total once and reuse it? Named running total Should January&#39;s denominator include other months? Filter scope and fixed totals How do I handle business-day midnight and daylight saving? Fixed DuckDB timezone recipe Replay the examples Download the project . From the extracted directory, use Python 3 with the example&#39;s duckdb==1.5.5 dependency and your already-installed compiler: py verify.py --dmlc C:/path/to/dmlc.exe --output C:/path/to/new-practice-output Replace both paths. The output directory must not exist; use a new one for each replay. The script creates its own practice database and local connection configuration there and does not read business data. Do not use Python -O , which disables assertions. Non-Windows users can invoke their Python 3 executable instead of py . Successful replay ends with passed case statuses and records results.json , raw diagnostics and generated SQL under the output directory. Expected failure cases must fail with their specified diagnostics before repair succeeds. The package README also gives JDBC invocation details. It includes readable sources and expected outputs, not compiler binaries or third-party runtimes. The JDBC checks additionally need a JDK and the matching installed jars. Compilation, live validation, execution and result comparison are separate checks. Deliberately invalid cases must produce the expected failure, then pass after repair. Before substituting your tables, check complete keys, time fields, grouping and filter scope. A successful example on one database does not establish every dialect, metric or query grain. The language reference owns syntax; these guides show specific tasks with reproducible answers."},{"url":"examples--index.html","title":"按业务问题选择可运行案例","text":"按业务问题选择可运行案例 先确定要回答的问题，再选模型和查询。 这些案例将输入、完整定义、查询和预期结果放在一起。语法规则仍由语言参考维护。 关联与源数据 你遇到的问题 案例 同一编号在不同地区重复，怎样正确关联？ 复合键关联与唯一性验证 怎样取得交易发生时有效的价格或等级？ 按有效期查找属性 时间与累计 你遇到的问题 案例 只显示一个累计 KPI，时间截止点放在哪里？ 给累计 KPI 指定时间上下文 累计销售额怎样定义一次、在查询中复用？ 定义可复用的累计指标 筛选与整体 你遇到的问题 案例 只显示一月时，分母要保留全年还是只取一月？ 固定分组下控制分母 数据库专用配方 DuckDB 固定业务时区 说明跨午夜、夏令时及业务日筛选。该配方的适用范围与通用 DML 案例分开标注。 怎样使用下载包 每个案例先展示预期结果，再说明运行入口。下载包包含可读的模型、查询、输入和校验脚本，不附带编译器或第三方运行时。使用自己的已安装编译器，在隔离练习目录运行。 编译、数据库验证、执行与结果比对是不同步骤。预期失败的用例应出现指定错误，不能当成普通成功查询。复用时先检查键、时间字段、分组和筛选口径，再改为自己的表名。 页面给出本次验证的范围。单个数据库上的成功，不代表所有方言、任意指标或任意查询粒度都已通过。 中文文档导航 · 语言参考（英文） · 代码片段目录（英文） English task examples"},{"url":"examples--range-lookup-en.html","title":"Look up an attribute valid at an event date","text":"Look up an attribute valid at an event date Match the event date to a non-overlapping validity interval for the same object. This example uses [valid_from, valid_to) : the start is included and the end is excluded. Check the boundary date Label Validity Event date Amount Jan [2024-01-01, 2024-02-01) 2024-01-01 10 Feb [2024-02-01, 2024-03-01) 2024-02-01 20 February 1 belongs to Feb, not Jan. The query returns Feb 20 and Jan 10, with one match per event. Put the lookup rule in the model The target declares dimension: [id, &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39;] . Keep the range expression quoted: its comma belongs inside one YAML list item. The fact&#39;s pk({id}, {event_date}).{D}.label supplies the object id and event date in that order. 完整文件 / Complete file: range/model.dml dialect: duckdb D: type: dataset table: DIM dimension: [id, &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39;] columns: id: valid_from: valid_to: label: F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [fid] columns: fid: id: event_date: amount: label: &#39;pk({id}, {event_date}).{D}.label&#39; measures: sales: SUM({amount}) 完整文件 / Complete file: range/setup.sql CREATE TABLE DIM(id INTEGER, valid_from DATE,valid_to DATE,label VARCHAR); INSERT INTO DIM VALUES (1,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,&#39;Jan&#39;),(1,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,&#39;2024-03-01&#39;,&#39;Feb&#39;); CREATE TABLE FACT(fid INTEGER,id INTEGER,event_date DATE,amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO FACT VALUES (1,1,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,10),(2,1,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,20); 完整文件 / Complete file: range/normal.sql SELECT label, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY label ORDER BY label When replacing the label with a price or tier, define what the effective date and event date mean. This example demonstrates adjacent finite intervals, not open-ended validity, arbitrary many-to-many relationships or allocation rules. Detect an overlap and repair it The verifier moves Jan&#39;s end to February 2, so February 1 matches both intervals. --validate-only reports DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE . Restoring the end to February 1 makes validation and the two-row result pass again. Repair the business validity rule. Arbitrarily retaining one matching record conceals the conflict. 中文 · Task examples · Compile and validate Run and interpret the checks Download the complete project and run verify.py as described in its README. It needs your existing dmlc, Python and DuckDB. The script creates a practice database only in the new output directory you specify. The shared verifier records results and diagnostics there. This example was checked on DuckDB with dmlc 0.3.16, build 2026-11-13. The executable fingerprint and case statuses are in verification-summary.json in the download. Database validation and subsequent execution are independent operations; they do not guarantee a shared snapshot. Verify other engines separately."},{"url":"examples--range-lookup-zh.html","title":"怎样取得交易发生时有效的价格或等级？","text":"怎样取得交易发生时有效的价格或等级？ 用事件日期匹配有效期，并保持同一对象的区间不重叠。 本例使用 [valid_from, valid_to) ：包含起点，不包含终点。 交界点应该匹配哪一条？ 标签 有效期 事件日期 金额 Jan [2024-01-01, 2024-02-01) 2024-01-01 10 Feb [2024-02-01, 2024-03-01) 2024-02-01 20 2月1日已离开 Jan 区间，属于 Feb。查询返回 Feb 20、Jan 10，各事件只匹配一次。 把查找规则放进模型 目标数据集声明 dimension: [id, &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39;] 。事实中的 pk({id}, {event_date}).{D}.label 同时提供对象编号和发生日期。 完整文件 / Complete file: range/model.dml dialect: duckdb D: type: dataset table: DIM dimension: [id, &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39;] columns: id: valid_from: valid_to: label: F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [fid] columns: fid: id: event_date: amount: label: &#39;pk({id}, {event_date}).{D}.label&#39; measures: sales: SUM({amount}) 完整文件 / Complete file: range/setup.sql CREATE TABLE DIM(id INTEGER, valid_from DATE,valid_to DATE,label VARCHAR); INSERT INTO DIM VALUES (1,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,&#39;Jan&#39;),(1,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,&#39;2024-03-01&#39;,&#39;Feb&#39;); CREATE TABLE FACT(fid INTEGER,id INTEGER,event_date DATE,amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO FACT VALUES (1,1,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,10),(2,1,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,20); 完整文件 / Complete file: range/normal.sql SELECT label, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY label ORDER BY label 将标签替换为价格或等级时，仍要明确生效时间与事件时间的含义。本例证明相邻、有限区间的匹配，不代表开放终点、任意多对多关系或分摊规则已经验证。 验证重叠，再修复 脚本将 Jan 的终点改为2月2日，使2月1日同时落入两个区间。 --validate-only 检出 DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE 。恢复2月1日终点后，验证和两行结果重新通过。 正确修复应来自业务有效期规则，不能任意保留其中一条匹配记录来掩盖冲突。 运行与验证范围 下载完整项目 ，按包内 README.md 运行 verify.py 。需要已有的 dmlc、Python 和 DuckDB；脚本只在你指定的全新输出目录创建练习库。所有案例共用一个入口，结果与诊断保存在输出目录。 本例在 dmlc 0.3.16、2026-11-13 构建的 DuckDB 连接上验证。编译器指纹、用例列表和状态见包内 verification-summary.json。数据库验证和随后执行是独立操作，不保证同一快照。其他引擎需单独验证。 返回案例目录 · 编译与验证（英文） English"},{"url":"examples--running-total-en.html","title":"Reuse an ordered running sales measure","text":"Reuse an ordered running sales measure Define the ordered accumulation as a named measure and declare its allowed grain. This example accumulates sales, not gross margins or monthly distinct customer counts. Inspect the definition and result Monthly sales are January 40 and February 20. running_sales returns January 40 and February 60. running_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({sales}) OVER (ORDER BY {month} ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)&#39; dimensions: - [month] ORDER BY month chooses time order; ROWS accumulates from the first row through the current row. There is no partition, so regions are first combined into monthly sales. dimensions: [[month]] restricts the use grain to month. The ISO-formatted month strings sort chronologically. 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/model.dml dialect: duckdb F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [id] columns: id: region: month: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) region_selected: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-d: month}}&#39; region_all_months: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-df: month}}&#39; running_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({sales}) OVER (ORDER BY {month} ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)&#39; dimensions: - [month] 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/running.sql SELECT month, AGG(sales), AGG(running_sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY month ORDER BY month Other queries can reference the same running_sales . To accumulate separately per region, define the partition and allowed grain, then verify its results. Adding region to a query alone does not establish that behavior. Filtering before the window changes its starting point 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/running_february.sql SELECT month, AGG(sales), AGG(running_sales) FROM MODEL WHERE month=&#39;2024-02-01&#39; GROUP BY month ORDER BY month With February alone selected, sales and running sales are both 20. January&#39;s 40 is not retained automatically. This is accumulation over the selected result population. For year-to-date through February, use an explicit YTD scope and inspect its inputs; see the cumulative KPI . A cumulative rank ordered by metric value is not a chronological running total. Ordering is part of the definition. An empty evaluation grain such as {sales: {dimension: []}} requests a total, not an ordered accumulation. 中文 · Task examples · Compile and validate Run and interpret the checks Download the complete project and run verify.py as described in its README. It needs your existing dmlc, Python and DuckDB. The script creates a practice database only in the new output directory you specify. The shared verifier records results and diagnostics there. This example was checked on DuckDB with dmlc 0.3.16, build 2026-11-13. The executable fingerprint and case statuses are in verification-summary.json in the download. Database validation and subsequent execution are independent operations; they do not guarantee a shared snapshot. Verify other engines separately."},{"url":"examples--running-total-zh.html","title":"累计销售额怎样定义一次、在多个查询中复用？","text":"累计销售额怎样定义一次、在多个查询中复用？ 把按时间排序的累计计算定义成有名指标，并写明可用粒度。 本例累计的是销售额，不能用来直接累加毛利率或每月去重客户数。 定义与结果 按月汇总，1月销售40、2月20； running_sales 返回1月40、2月60。 running_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({sales}) OVER (ORDER BY {month} ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)&#39; dimensions: - [month] ORDER BY month 决定时间顺序， ROWS 指明从第一行累计到当前行。本例没有分区，所以所有地区先汇成每月销售； dimensions: [[month]] 将使用粒度限制为月份。ISO 格式的 month 字符串按年月顺序排列。 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/model.dml dialect: duckdb F: type: dataset table: FACT dimension: [id] columns: id: region: month: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) region_selected: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-d: month}}&#39; region_all_months: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {-df: month}}&#39; running_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({sales}) OVER (ORDER BY {month} ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)&#39; dimensions: - [month] 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/running.sql SELECT month, AGG(sales), AGG(running_sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY month ORDER BY month 另一条查询可以直接引用同一 running_sales 。如果要按地区分别累计，应另行定义地区分区和相应允许粒度，并验证结果，不能仅在查询中追加地区就假定公式仍适用。 先筛选再开窗，会改变累计起点 完整文件 / Complete file: fixed/running_february.sql SELECT month, AGG(sales), AGG(running_sales) FROM MODEL WHERE month=&#39;2024-02-01&#39; GROUP BY month ORDER BY month 只保留2月时，本例返回销售20、累计20，不会自动保留1月的40。这是选中结果范围内的累计。要表达截至2月的年累计，应使用具有时间上下文的 YTD 口径并核对其输入范围，参见 累计 KPI 。 不要把按指标值排序的累计排名宏当作按时间累计；排序字段属于指标定义的一部分。 运行与验证范围 下载完整项目 ，按包内 README.md 运行 verify.py 。需要已有的 dmlc、Python 和 DuckDB；脚本只在你指定的全新输出目录创建练习库。所有案例共用一个入口，结果与诊断保存在输出目录。 本例在 dmlc 0.3.16、2026-11-13 构建的 DuckDB 连接上验证。编译器指纹、用例列表和状态见包内 verification-summary.json。数据库验证和随后执行是独立操作，不保证同一快照。其他引擎需单独验证。 返回案例目录 · 编译与验证（英文） English"},{"url":"examples--time-context-en.html","title":"Give a cumulative KPI an explicit cutoff","text":"Give a cumulative KPI an explicit cutoff A cumulative measure needs a time context. A date filter in the same query also scopes ordinary sibling measures. DML does not silently choose today or the latest fact date. Compare two scopes in one result January has revenue 10 and profit 3; February has revenue 20 and profit 3. In the February query, YTD margin is (3+3)/(10+20)=20% . Ordinary revenue is 20, not cumulative revenue 30. 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/model.dml dialect: duckdb F: type: dataset table: YTD_FACT dimension: [id] columns: id: dt_month: revenue_amount: profit_amount: measures: revenue: SUM({revenue_amount}) profit: SUM({profit_amount}) margin: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({profit} * 1.0, {revenue})&#39; margin_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{margin: ytd}&#39; 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/setup.sql CREATE TABLE YTD_FACT(id INTEGER, dt_month DATE,revenue_amount INTEGER,profit_amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO YTD_FACT VALUES (1,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,10,3),(2,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,20,3); 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/february.sql SELECT AGG(margin_ytd), AGG(revenue) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month=&#39;2024-02-01&#39; The model defines margin_ytd as &#39;{margin: ytd}&#39; . Components are calculated over the cumulative period before division. Averaging 30% and 15% answers a different question. Observe missing context 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/missing_context.sql SELECT AGG(margin_ytd) FROM MODEL This query fails compilation with a missing dim_dt diagnostic. Adding the February filter returns 20%. That filter is not an independent cutoff parameter: ordinary revenue in the same query also becomes February-only. Define and verify each scope if a report needs metrics over different periods. Grouped months can exist without sales 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/months.sql SELECT dt_month, AGG(margin_ytd), AGG(revenue) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month ORDER BY dt_month This fixture returns twelve months. January has cumulative margin 30% and revenue 10; February has 20% and revenue 20. March through December have cumulative margin 20% and ordinary revenue NULL. The cumulative branch uses calendar-generated months. These rows are not new transactions, and NULL is not a measured zero. Filter explicitly if the report needs one cutoff month. After changing the range, check both output months and every metric&#39;s input scope. 中文 · Task examples · Compile and validate Run and interpret the checks Download the complete project and run verify.py as described in its README. It needs your existing dmlc, Python and DuckDB. The script creates a practice database only in the new output directory you specify. The shared verifier records results and diagnostics there. This example was checked on DuckDB with dmlc 0.3.16, build 2026-11-13. The executable fingerprint and case statuses are in verification-summary.json in the download. Database validation and subsequent execution are independent operations; they do not guarantee a shared snapshot. Verify other engines separately."},{"url":"examples--time-context-zh.html","title":"只显示一个累计 KPI，时间截止点放在哪里？","text":"只显示一个累计 KPI，时间截止点放在哪里？ 累计指标需要明确的时间上下文；同一个查询的日期筛选也会影响其他普通指标。 不应默认为今天或数据中最新日期。 一个比率，两种时间范围 一月收入10、利润3；二月收入20、利润3。二月查询中，累计毛利率为 (3+3)/(10+20)=20% ，普通收入为20，而不是累计收入30。 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/model.dml dialect: duckdb F: type: dataset table: YTD_FACT dimension: [id] columns: id: dt_month: revenue_amount: profit_amount: measures: revenue: SUM({revenue_amount}) profit: SUM({profit_amount}) margin: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({profit} * 1.0, {revenue})&#39; margin_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{margin: ytd}&#39; 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/setup.sql CREATE TABLE YTD_FACT(id INTEGER, dt_month DATE,revenue_amount INTEGER,profit_amount INTEGER); INSERT INTO YTD_FACT VALUES (1,&#39;2024-01-01&#39;,10,3),(2,&#39;2024-02-01&#39;,20,3); 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/february.sql SELECT AGG(margin_ytd), AGG(revenue) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month=&#39;2024-02-01&#39; 模型中 margin_ytd 使用 &#39;{margin: ytd}&#39; 。先在累计范围计算组成项，再相除，不能把30%与15%直接平均。 缺少时间上下文时 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/missing_context.sql SELECT AGG(margin_ytd) FROM MODEL 这条查询在本例中编译失败，提示缺少 dim_dt 。添加二月筛选后得到20%。但这不是一个独立于其他指标的截止参数；同一查询的普通收入也受该筛选约束。若一个报表要同时呈现不同期间口径，应显式定义并分别核对每项指标的范围。 按月份分组不保证只出现有销售的月份 完整文件 / Complete file: ytd/months.sql SELECT dt_month, AGG(margin_ytd), AGG(revenue) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month ORDER BY dt_month 本夹具返回12个月：一月累计30%、收入10；二月累计20%、收入20；三月至十二月累计20%、普通收入为 NULL。累计分支使用日历产生的月份，不能把这些行描述为新增交易，也不能把 NULL 当成零收入。 如果报表只需要某个截止月份，请显式筛选。改变日期范围后，仍需检查输出月份与每个指标的范围。 运行与验证范围 下载完整项目 ，按包内 README.md 运行 verify.py 。需要已有的 dmlc、Python 和 DuckDB；脚本只在你指定的全新输出目录创建练习库。所有案例共用一个入口，结果与诊断保存在输出目录。 本例在 dmlc 0.3.16、2026-11-13 构建的 DuckDB 连接上验证。编译器指纹、用例列表和状态见包内 verification-summary.json。数据库验证和随后执行是独立操作，不保证同一快照。其他引擎需单独验证。 返回案例目录 · 编译与验证（英文） English"},{"url":"explanation--core-concepts.html","title":"DML, DMX, and Model Query","text":"DML, DMX, and Model Query A report combines business definitions with choices about grouping, filters, and presentation. DML keeps reusable definitions in a model, so reports can consume the same measures at compatible grains. Metrics can reference other metrics, while analytical context determines how each reference is evaluated. A margin definition can therefore serve a regional report and a year-to-date KPI: the time transformation recalculates profit and revenue before division rather than averaging monthly margins. Measures from separate datasets can also compose at compatible grains; their output keys and missing sides must still match the intended report population. The profitability example follows this through composition, accumulation and comparison. DML models, DMX expressions and the compiler can provide the calculation core of an analytical semantic layer; agreeing business definitions remains a human responsibility. The illustrated small journey through DML introduces these ideas with four example sales and a grouping explorer. For a complete executable example, see Compile your first model query . The DML core specification defines the language contracts. 1. The model, query, and expression layers Layer Language Content Location Model DML, Dato Measure Language Datasets, measures, and column macros .dml YAML Query Model Query Measure selection, grouping, and report filters .sql or DML type: query Expression DMX, Dato Measure eXpression Formulas, references, and modifiers Model formulas and query expressions DML is a standalone semantic-layer compiler that works with model files and generates SQL for a database to execute. Database permissions and application authorization control access; dashboards and scheduling belong to the surrounding system. A model filter alone is not an access-control policy. DML checks declaration structure and analytical compatibility, such as grain and time context. It does not provide complete static SQL expression type checking. Physical value types belong to the database, and the checked model schema rejects column type: declarations. A bad conversion or function argument can therefore survive compilation and fail at the database. See column metadata and value types . A dataset binds columns to a physical table or SQL relation. Its dimension: declares the complete source-row key. transactions: type: dataset table: fact_transactions dimension: [transaction_id] columns: transaction_id: amount: discount: net_amount: amount - discount measures: sales: SUM({net_amount}) number_of_transactions: COUNT(1) avg_transaction_value: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({sales}, {number_of_transactions})&#39; sales and number_of_transactions are atomic measures. They inherit the enclosing dataset and directly aggregate its rows or column expressions without referencing other measures. avg_transaction_value is composite because its formula references other measures, even though both use transactions . A composite measure has no dataset: property. In a model formula, bare amount names a physical SQL column. {net_amount} expands the model&#39;s calculated column. Braces retain that distinction even when logical and physical names happen to match. Column macros define reusable column expressions or cross-dataset lookups. They can expose attributes to measures without putting a join in each consumer query. The target dataset&#39;s complete key determines the lookup arguments; see source relations and lookups . Model Query references measures through AGG(...) . Model formulas use aggregation functions such as SUM and COUNT directly. AGG is the query interface to a measure, not a replacement for SUM inside a model formula. A one-off measure can start in an inline DML block in the query file. Exploration does not require changing the shared model first. Review useful definitions before promoting them to shared measures. 2. Source relations, lookups and metric composition DML combines data at different stages. Choosing the stage determines which keys, filters and row populations you must check. Task Mechanism Result contract Reuse an attribute by its complete key pk(...) lookup in a model formula One target row per lookup key. Single and compound keys are supported. Find the attribute valid at an event date pk(id, event_date) against [id, &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39;] The checked finite intervals match [start,end) . Intervals for the same id must not overlap. Compare independent facts such as sales and budget Measures evaluated on aligned dimensions The normal key-union composition preserves groups present on either side, including missing values on the other. Prepare a bridge, join, union or subquery relation Dataset sql: or a database view The prepared relation needs its own complete key. Define allocation weights and verify totals when one source row can match several rows. Consumer Model Query SQL does not directly accept JOIN, UNION or subqueries. That restriction does not prohibit preparing supported relations in dataset SQL. Filters built into source SQL remain part of its population; they do not automatically become rules that respond to every consumer context. Compound and effective-date lookups do not imply a general relationship graph with arbitrary path selection or automatic many-to-many allocation. Independent measure aggregation also does not repair duplicate lookup keys or a wrong alignment contract. Follow the compound-key and effective-date examples for complete models, deliberate key failures and repair. See composition semantics for unmatched groups and NULL keys, and dataset SQL for source ownership and the disclosed derived-relation validation-probe limitation. 3. Dimension roles and grain A dimension field is one named DML field. A dimension is a list of fields. The declaration site determines its role. Declaration Meaning Dataset dimension: Complete source-row identity Formula dimension modifiers Evaluation dimension of one measure reference Query GROUP BY or columns.dimension Result grouping Materialize dimension: Stored grouping and key Measure dimensions: Alternative allowed evaluation dimensions, one per inner list Grain describes what one row represents in a particular context. For example, [transaction_id] identifies a transaction, while [region, dt_month] groups a report by region and month. A reporting group can contain many source rows, so it cannot replace the source key. The modifier dimension: [] clears the evaluation dimension for a grand total. It does not declare dataset identity. +d adds fields for an intermediate calculation. An explicit outer aggregate combines those intermediate values when a formula needs a coarser result. The compiler trusts the declared source key. Duplicate pk(...) targets can multiply rows even when compilation succeeds. Live validation checks observed key uniqueness. Result comparisons establish a different claim: whether the query returns the intended answer. Dimension semantics explains these roles and their consequences in more detail. 4. Time context and dim_dt dim_dt represents the active time field of a measure&#39;s query context. It lets a formula refer to the current time grain without naming dt_date , dt_month , or dt_quarter directly. Macro prerequisites still limit which grains a macro accepts. A grouped time field or a time filter can supply the context. Read strict diagnostics and inspect generated joins separately from syntax acceptance. The presence of a time comparison or key-union join does not itself imply a strict warning. Declaring a standard fine time field such as dt_date makes supported coarser fields available through the system calendar. These virtual fields do not require duplicate physical declarations. Editor go-to-definition for a derived dt_month resolves to the explicit source time column. A single KPI still needs an explicit cutoff. In the runnable example , a February filter gives YTD margin 20% over January and February, while ordinary sibling revenue is February-only. Without time context the query fails. Grouped calendar queries can also return months with cumulative values but NULL current revenue, so select output periods deliberately. The calendar must cover the intended dates on the executing connection. The time context specification defines dim_dt and standard time fields. System macros provides a reference copy of time and formula macro definitions. 5. Reuse and integration Custom fn-macro definitions wrap a caller&#39;s measure formula, while dt-macro definitions change its time context. Both compose through the modifier syntax without changing the compiler. Database scalar calls reuse functions installed on the executing connection. The extension guide shows executed examples and a missing-function failure. These mechanisms do not establish a general module system or portable support for every UDF kind. Set the model&#39;s SQL dialect explicitly. A recognized dialect is not evidence that every feature has executed successfully on that engine. Generated SQL can contain multiple statements, intermediate relations and calendar dependencies, so verify the execution environment too. For an application that needs caller-bound filters, check the JDBC parameter boundary before adopting the query interface. The checked physical SQL parameters work, while semantic Model Query placeholders fail. Materialization parameters and internal literal parameterization do not supply the missing caller-visible binding API. Compilation, validation and execution establish different facts. Live key validation can catch duplicates before a lookup multiplies rows, but it does not share a guaranteed snapshot with subsequent execution. Database EXPLAIN acceptance does not prove the returned numbers. Compare complete keys, row multiplicity, NULLs and unrounded values against an independent answer on the intended scope."},{"url":"explanation--dimensions.html","title":"Dimension semantics and derived grain","text":"Dimension semantics and derived grain Use this guide to reason about dimension behavior when modeling datasets, writing measures, compiling queries, or diagnosing row multiplication. It explains the language model; the normative reference remains DML evaluation semantics . 1. The object model A dimension field is one named DML field. A dimension is a list containing one or more dimension fields. Examples: [order_id] [order_id, line_id] [product_id, dt_month] Each line above is one dimension. [product_id, dt_month] is one dimension containing two fields. Measure dimensions: has one additional list level because it can contain alternative allowed dimensions: dimensions: - [customer_id] - [account_id, dt_month] The outer list contains two allowed dimensions. Each inner list is one dimension. A grand total has no dimension. In measure-modifier syntax, {dimension: []} clears the evaluation dimension to request that context; the empty list is an operation, not a zero-field dimension. 2. The five dimension roles The list structure is the same, but its DML location determines its behavior: DML location Role Behavior Dataset dimension: Dataset dimension Declares the dimension fields whose complete value combination identifies one source row. Formula plus dimension modifiers Evaluation dimension Determines the dimension at which one measure reference is evaluated. Query GROUP BY or columns.dimension Query dimension Groups the returned rows and determines what one result row represents. Materialize dimension: Materialization dimension Defines the grouping and key of the materialized relation. Measure dimensions: Allowed dimensions Constrains the alternative dimensions at which the measure may be evaluated. It does not declare source identity or query grouping. These roles are not interchangeable. For example, adding [region] to a measure&#39;s dimensions: does not add region to a query result. The query still needs GROUP BY region or columns.dimension: [region] . 3. Grain is derived from a dimension Grain describes what one row represents after a dimension is applied in a specific context. Context Dimension Derived grain Order-line dataset [order_id, line_id] One source row per order line Monthly product query [product_id, dt_month] One result row per product and month Customer evaluation [customer_id] One intermediate value per customer Monthly account materialization [account_id, dt_month] One materialized row per account and month Dimension is the language contract; grain is the row meaning produced by that contract. Use grain to compare row meanings, reason about fan-out, or explain why two dimensions are incompatible. Do not use a desired reporting grain as a substitute for source-row identity. Adding a dimension field usually produces a finer grain, and removing one usually produces a coarser grain. That intuition is not sufficient proof of compatibility: field derivation, time relationships, measure constraints, and dataset boundaries still matter. Let the compiler validate the declared relationship. 4. Dataset dimension: source-row identity The dataset dimension contains the fields that identify one physical source row: Order Line: type: dataset table: tabOrderLine dimension: [order_id, line_id] columns: order_id: line_id: product_id: net_amount: Uniqueness applies to the complete [order_id, line_id] value combination. order_id may repeat across lines, and line_id may repeat across orders, while the complete dataset dimension remains unique. Do not replace the physical identity with a repeatable business grouping. If the source contains several ledger entries for the same [account_id, invoice_id] , that list is not a valid dataset dimension even when reports commonly group by it. Use the physical entry key as the dataset dimension and [account_id, invoice_id] as a query or materialization dimension. pk(...) relies on the target dataset dimension: the lookup arguments correspond to the target dimension fields; their arity and meaning must match that dimension; one lookup must match at most one target row. The compiler trusts this contract and may lower a lookup to a LEFT JOIN . Duplicate target keys can therefore multiply rows and measures. Adding DISTINCT or GROUP BY to compiled SQL does not repair the model contract. 5. Query dimension: result grouping In Model Query SQL: SELECT dt_month, product_id, AGG(net_sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month, product_id The query dimension is [dt_month, product_id] . Each result row represents one month and product value combination. In DML-format Query, the equivalent role is explicit: Monthly Product Sales: type: query columns: dimension: [dt_month, product_id] net_sales: &quot;{net_sales}&quot; A query without grouping has no query dimension and returns a grand-total context when its measures permit one. When compatible measures come from different datasets, the compiler aggregates each measure independently to the query grain and then combines the results using shared dimension fields. This avoids the usual aggregate fan-out caused by joining detail rows first. It does not remove the need for valid dataset dimensions or unique pk(...) lookups. A query-dimension field may also be a measure-as-dimension : a measure result used as a query-dimension field (often bound with {name: {+d: dim}} ). That is still a query-dimension role, not dataset dimension: identity. Authoring contract: Measure-as-dimension . 6. Evaluation dimension: one measure reference The query dimension supplies the starting context for a measure reference. Formula structure, allowed dimensions, and modifiers determine its effective evaluation dimension. Given query dimension [dt_month, region, brand] : Measure reference Dimension operation Resulting evaluation dimension {sales} No dimension modifier Resolved target dimension {sales: {+d: customer_id}} Add dimension field customer_id [dt_month, region, brand, customer_id] {sales: {-d: brand}} Remove dimension field brand [dt_month, region] {sales: {dimension: [region]}} Replace the complete dimension [region] {sales: {dimension: []}} Clear the dimension Grand-total context These operations affect only that measure reference; they do not change the query GROUP BY . -d removes a dimension field but retains a query filter on that field when the compiler pushes filters to the dataset. -df removes the field and blocks its related filter propagation for that reference. Use the latter when a denominator must include values excluded by the query filter. See Measure modifiers for syntax and filter behavior. 7. Allowed dimensions: measure validity Measure dimensions: restricts valid evaluation dimensions when the business definition requires it: Revenue per Customer: type: measure formula: DIVIDE({net_sales}, {active_customers}) dimensions: - [customer_id] [customer_id] is one closed allowed dimension. It constrains evaluation; it does not identify dataset rows and does not group query output. An ellipsis permits additional dimension fields while keeping the listed fields required: dimensions: - [customer_id, ...] This accepts compatible evaluation dimensions that contain customer_id . Omitting measure dimensions: permits any dimension compatible with the measure and its dataset dependencies. If a formula needs a required dimension field that the query does not provide, add it to the relevant measure reference with +d and perform the required outer aggregation. Do not add a query grouping merely to satisfy an intermediate calculation. 8. Materialization dimension: stored output identity A materialization dimension declares the grouping and key of stored results: Monthly Account Balance: type: materialize dimension: [account_id, dt_month] measures: [closing_balance] dimension_columns: are functionally dependent attributes carried with those keys. They are not additional fields in the materialization dimension. If an attribute is derivable from the materialization dimension, keep it in dimension_columns: rather than changing the stored row identity. 9. Compatibility and validation Compilation checks the declared model contract: dimension fields resolve through the relevant datasets and column macros; evaluation dimensions satisfy measure dimensions: constraints; modifiers can produce a valid evaluation dimension; measures from different datasets support the query dimension; time modifiers have the required active time field. Compilation does not connect to live data and cannot prove dataset dimension uniqueness. Run validateOnly when current evidence is missing or stale. For the compiled query, it runs the generated dataset-key checks on the selected connection and checks database acceptance and planning of the main query through EXPLAIN ; it does not execute the main result rows. Validation evidence covers the model and query contract, connection, and source state observed at validation time. A relevant model change, source refresh, key-policy change, or connection change makes that evidence stale. If validation reports DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE , fix the source key, dataset dimension, overlapping ranges, or source data. If a validation query cannot execute or returns a malformed result, handle VALIDATION_QUERY_FAILED separately; it is not evidence of duplicate keys. Validation proves neither business correctness nor parity with a trusted report. When migrating an existing query, compare both results on the same filters, time window, and query dimension. 10. Authoring workflow The forward-analysis workflow applies these roles to model and query changes. Use the dmlc CLI covers compilation and live validation. Related references Quick orientation: DML, DMX, and Model Query Measure-as-dimension: Measure-as-dimension Normative language semantics: DML evaluation semantics Measures and allowed dimensions: Measure declarations Dimension modifiers: Measure modifiers Model Query syntax: Model Query SQL Failure patterns: Troubleshooting index Worked model/query examples: Examples by task"},{"url":"explanation--measure-as-dimension.html","title":"Measure-as-dimension","text":"Measure-as-dimension Measure-as-dimension calculates a label per entity, then groups metrics by that label. The label has a query-dimension role. It does not identify source rows. Entity keys and evaluation stages Classification needs a complete entity key because a label belongs to an entity at a particular grain. Let Q be the ordinary report fields and K the entity key. With +d: K , the classifier is evaluated at Q ∪ K . The compiler attaches the label by the complete key, then aggregates ordinary metrics at Q plus the label. Given atomic sales: SUM({amount}) , this query fragment groups customers by their sales value. report: type: query columns: dimension: - sales_bucket: &#39;{sales: {+d: customer}}&#39; total_sales: &#39;SUM({sales: {+d: customer}})&#39; The explicit outer SUM adds the sales of customers with the same label. This example uses the sales value itself as the label. A business bucket requires its own classifier definition and verification. The classification use site requires an explicit key. A bare {measure} is rejected. A finer stage inside the measure definition or a sibling metric does not supply the missing key. The current checked build compiles the checked +d: customer and dimension: [customer] count classifiers to customer aggregation followed by label grouping. These probes establish the checked forms only. Population and reaggregation Classification scope, entity membership, and report scope are separate decisions. -d retains time filters, so removing time grouping does not imply lifetime classification. A NULL label can be a valid bucket. It does not prove that an entity is absent, and classification does not create inactive entities. Multiple labels need compatible complete entity keys and independent definitions. Additive amounts can be summed from entity grain. Ratios, averages, and distinct counts need components that preserve their definitions across reaggregation. Label-channel filters A filter over a classifier label or sibling measures at label grain belongs after classification. DML queries can express such a predicate under filters: , for example &quot;{首购月} is not null OR {销售额} &gt; 0&quot;: . Model Query can represent a measure predicate as HAVING . The semantic stage matters more than that keyword: compiled SQL commonly uses WHERE on the relation produced by classification. Moving the predicate before classification changes the population used to calculate the label. Limitations This pattern describes query columns.dimension ; it does not establish classifier support in materialize.dimension_columns . Inspect the generated classification and reaggregation stages, then validate keys and compare result values on your own data."},{"url":"explanation--visual-introduction.html","title":"A small journey through DML","text":"A small journey through DML A traveler arrives with one question: how much did each region sell? Open the illustrated, interactive tutorial . It now includes twelve chapters, graded exercises, saved progress, and a final report builder. The exercise guide follows the complete beginner project. This page retains the shorter conceptual introduction. Each sale has an identity The traveler records four sales. These are invented rows for the illustration, not records fetched from a database. id region amount 1 North 10 2 North 20 3 South 30 4 South 40 The source dataset has dimension: [id] : one row represents one sale. Two sales can share a region, so region cannot identify a source row. A query can still group those rows by region. The dimension explanation describes source identity and report grouping in detail. DML records the reusable definitions The traveler keeps a notebook of definitions. DML is that model, written in YAML. This fragment declares two atomic measures on the sales dataset: FACT_SALES: type: dataset table: TABLE_SALES dimension: [id] columns: id: region: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) cnt: COUNT(1) sales sums the amount. cnt counts source rows. Both are atomic measures: they inherit FACT_SALES and directly aggregate its rows or column expressions without referencing other measures. Reports can reuse them at compatible evaluation grains. DMX expresses a calculation The notebook also records how to calculate an average: average_sale: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({sales}, {cnt})&#39; The YAML declares a measure. The formula inside it is DMX. {sales} and {cnt} reference measures, and DIVIDE returns NULL for a zero denominator. average_sale is composite because it references other measures; it has no dataset: property. DMX is shared expression syntax inside model definitions and query expressions. It is not a separate processing step between the model and query. The expression reference owns the syntax. The query chooses what one result row represents The traveler turns a telescope toward the regions: SELECT region, AGG(sales), AGG(average_sale) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region AGG references a model measure. GROUP BY region chooses one result row per region. For the four invented sales, the arithmetic gives these values: region sales average_sale North 30 15 South 70 35 Without grouping fields, the same model measures give total sales of 100 and an average sale of 25. Grouping by id gives one result per sale. The source identity remains [id] in every case. At the declared source key [id] , the checked compiler simplifies the aggregate to a row expression and reports a strict finding. That simplification relies on id being unique in the source data. The total and regional queries pass strict compilation on the checked build. The tutorial compiler record identifies the executable used for the interactive project, its fingerprint and the checks performed. The interactive page calculates these small example tables in the browser. It does not run DML or connect to a database. Compilation produces SQL The compiler combines the model and query to generate SQL. The database executes that SQL against the real source table. DML model + Model Query | dmlc | generated SQL | database | result rows A successful compile confirms the checked compiler behavior. Live key validation and result comparisons provide separate evidence about the source data and answer. The first-query tutorial uses this same model and regional query for an executable exercise. For more detail, continue with core concepts , measure declarations , or Model Query SQL ."},{"url":"explanation--window-placement.html","title":"Window placement","text":"Window placement A window calculation depends on the rows available at its evaluation stage. Detail windows operate before a later measure aggregate. Composite windows operate on aggregated measure results. Supported placements Calculation Placement Input rows Attribute of each source row Calculated column under dataset columns: Detail rows Rank or share of grouped measures Composite formula with no dataset: Aggregated rows Atomic measure containing OVER Rejected in the checked build No supported placement Inspect the generated SQL to confirm where each window runs. Compilation, database execution and result comparison are separate checks. Detail window fact: type: dataset table: FACT_SALES dimension: [id] columns: id: customer: region: amount: customer_amount: &#39;SUM({amount}) OVER (PARTITION BY {customer})&#39; measures: repeated_customer_amount: &#39;SUM({customer_amount})&#39; The window repeats a customer&#39;s total on every detail row for that customer. Summing the calculated column counts the repeated total once per source row. The result is not a deduplicated customer total, so this example illustrates placement rather than a recommended sales metric. A row-order-sensitive window also depends on its ordering, frame, tie-break key, and source population. Changing a filter&#39;s stage can change every window result in a partition. A detail window can also select a last observation before aggregation. For a source with dt_month and revenue_amount , the checked pattern adds this column and measure: # Under the dataset&#39;s columns: is_last_month: &#39;IIF({dt_month} = MAX({dt_month}) OVER (), 1, 0)&#39; # Under the same dataset&#39;s measures: last_month_revenue: &#39;SUM({revenue_amount} * {is_last_month})&#39; MAX(...) OVER () chooses one latest month in the detail-window input. The measure sums only amounts from that month. There is no entity partition: if one entity has no observation in that month, this does not carry forward its last known balance. For inventory or account balances, define whether “last” is per entity or global, how missing observations behave, and which filters scope the window before adopting a formula. Post-aggregation window Given atomic sales: SUM({amount}) , this composite formula ranks aggregated sales. customer_rank: type: measure formula: &#39;rank() over (partition by {-d: customer} order by {sales} desc)&#39; At query grain [region, customer] , the partition expression retains the other query fields, here region . The checked SQL groups by region and customer, then ranks SUM(amount) within each region. Physical fact rows are not the ranking input. +d establishes a finer aggregation stage. It does not turn a grouped value into a detail-window attribute. Source filters, filters on window results, and final report filters can select different populations. Model Query also accepts checked raw window forms. The query reference describes the query surfaces. Acceptance of one form does not establish support for arbitrary frames or dialects. Row windows and calendar periods A post-aggregation row window uses the ordered rows that remain at its stage. Filtering a running-sales query to February before that window excludes January. The named running-total example verifies this filtered case and a reusable window measure. Use a time macro such as ytd when the business definition is a calendar period through an explicit cutoff. Use a row window when the definition is an ordered frame over the selected rows. Missing months, filter placement and tie-breaking can change a row frame. A working SUM window does not establish cumulative distinct counts or cumulative ratios; test those definitions separately."},{"url":"guide--cumulative-margin-en.html","title":"Why you cannot average monthly gross margins","text":"Why you cannot average monthly gross margins Calculate cumulative gross margin by adding revenue and cost over the period, then dividing cumulative gross profit by cumulative revenue. A simple average gives every month the same weight, even when revenues differ. A small example The shared profitability data contains these North sales totals: Period Revenue Cost Gross margin January 2025 100 60 40% February 2025 300 240 20% January–February 400 300 25% The cumulative result is (400 − 300) / 400 = 25% . Averaging 40% and 20% gives 30%, which answers a different question: the unweighted average of monthly ratios. The two methods agree in some cases, such as equal monthly revenue, but cannot be substituted generally. See the calculation change Open the metric theatre , select North, and choose the fourth scene. Switch from the monthly context to year to date. Revenue changes from 300 to 400, cost from 240 to 300, and the margin from 20% to 25%. Apply the period to the named ratio with formula: &#39;{gross_margin: ytd}&#39; . This recalculates the ratio over the cumulative period, rather than adding monthly ratios. The short form was executed on DuckDB and checked against independent SQL; the SQL Server compiler record alone is not evidence of database execution. The model is: Inspect the complete DML model FACT_PROFITABILITY: type: dataset table: TABLE_PROFITABILITY dimension: [id] columns: id: region: dt_month: revenue_amount: cost_amount: measures: net_revenue: SUM({revenue_amount}) sales_cost: SUM({cost_amount}) gross_profit: type: measure formula: &#39;{net_revenue} - {sales_cost}&#39; gross_margin: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({gross_profit}, {net_revenue})&#39; gross_margin_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{gross_margin: ytd}&#39; gross_margin_prior_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{gross_margin: {ytd, ya}}&#39; margin_change_pp: type: measure formula: &#39;100 * ({gross_margin_ytd} - {gross_margin_prior_ytd})&#39; A difference in percentage points North&#39;s prior-year cumulative margin is 20%. Moving from 20% to 25% is an increase of 5 percentage points . Relative growth is (25% / 20%) − 1 = 25%; label these calculations separately. Read the complete worked example or download the comparison query . The compiler record covers strict compilation and SQL inspection, not database execution. Use decimal monetary columns in your physical table. Zero revenue produces NULL; missing history remains missing. Continue learning How composite measures reuse definitions · Change the calculation context · 中文"},{"url":"guide--cumulative-margin-zh.html","title":"为什么累计毛利率不能直接平均各月毛利率？","text":"为什么累计毛利率不能直接平均各月毛利率？ 累计毛利率应先汇总期间内的收入和成本，再用累计毛利润除以累计收入。直接平均各月毛利率，会给收入不同的月份相同的权重。 用两个月看清区别 同一套毛利率示例数据 中，North 的数值如下： 期间 净收入 销售成本 毛利率 2025 年 1 月 100 60 40% 2025 年 2 月 300 240 20% 1—2 月累计 400 300 25% 累计结果为 (400 − 300) / 400 = 25% 。将 40% 与 20% 平均得到 30%，回答的是“各月毛利率的简单平均”，不是累计毛利率。两种算法在各月收入相等等情形下可能一致，但不能普遍互换。 自己切换一次 打开 指标构造剧场 ，选择 North 和第四幕，从当月切换到本年累计：收入由 300 变为 400，成本由 240 变为 300，毛利率由 20% 变为 25%。 直接对已定义的毛利率应用 ytd ： formula: &#39;{gross_margin: ytd}&#39; 。它会在累计期间重新计算组成项与比率，不累加各月毛利率。下面的模型使用这一简写： 查看完整 DML 模型 FACT_PROFITABILITY: type: dataset table: TABLE_PROFITABILITY dimension: [id] columns: id: region: dt_month: revenue_amount: cost_amount: measures: net_revenue: SUM({revenue_amount}) sales_cost: SUM({cost_amount}) gross_profit: type: measure formula: &#39;{net_revenue} - {sales_cost}&#39; gross_margin: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({gross_profit}, {net_revenue})&#39; gross_margin_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{gross_margin: ytd}&#39; gross_margin_prior_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{gross_margin: {ytd, ya}}&#39; margin_change_pp: type: measure formula: &#39;100 * ({gross_margin_ytd} - {gross_margin_prior_ytd})&#39; 百分点与增长率也要区分 North 去年同期累计毛利率为 20%。从 20% 升到 25%，增加的是 5 个百分点 ；相对增长率为 (25% / 20%) − 1 = 25%。报表中应分别标明。 可查阅 完整案例 、 比较查询 和 编译记录 。该编译记录覆盖 SQL Server 方言的严格编译与 SQL 检查，不代表 SQL Server 执行结果。另在 DuckDB 上执行了简写公式，验证 North 累计为25%、去年同期累计为20%，并与独立 SQL 对齐。物理表金额列应使用小数类型；收入为零时比率为 NULL，缺少历史值不自动补零。 接着学 引用已有度量 · 改变计算上下文 · English"},{"url":"guide--duplicate-lookup-en.html","title":"Why duplicate dimension keys can double sales after a lookup","text":"Why duplicate dimension keys can double sales after a lookup When one fact row matches two dimension rows, a join can return that fact row twice. Summing the joined amounts then counts both matches. A lookup is safe only when the declared target key matches the actual data&#39;s uniqueness. One region, two matches The lesson has two North sales: 10 and 20. With one North directory entry, the matched sales total is 30. Add a second North entry and each sale matches twice: Source sale Matches Contribution after join North, amount 10 2 20 North, amount 20 2 40 Total 4 joined rows 60 The source sales have not changed. The join result has more rows. A later aggregation can hide the extra rows while retaining the inflated amount. Inspect the rows Open the lookup exercise and enable the invalid duplicate North entry. Compare the directory, matched rows and North total. The logical dataset DIM_REGION declares dimension: [region]; its physical source is TABLE_REGION. Repeated North values violate that source-key declaration. Validate the real connection Before executing the report, run dmlc --validate-only on the intended connection. It checks whether the full declared dimensions of datasets reached by the query agree with the actual data. In this example, two North entries cause DIM_REGION to report DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE . Use the exercise&#39;s exact command and query , correct the declaration or source data, and validate again before execution to avoid duplicated lookup results. Revalidate after relevant changes to the model, query, connection or source data. A legitimate one-to-many relationship needs an explicitly designed calculation. The browser toggle affects only invented browser data. Validation probes source keys and query planning; it is not a comparison of executed report results. See grain and joins . 中文 · Download the lesson project"},{"url":"guide--duplicate-lookup-zh.html","title":"维表重复为什么会让销售额翻倍？","text":"维表重复为什么会让销售额翻倍？ 一条事实记录如果匹配到两条维表记录，关联结果就可能出现两次。再对关联后的金额求和，会把两次匹配都计入。查找是否安全，取决于目标数据是否真的满足声明的键唯一性。 一个地区，两次匹配 课程中 North 有两笔销售，金额分别为 10 和 20。地区目录只有一条 North 时，合计为 30；增加第二条 North 后，每笔销售都会匹配两次： 源销售记录 匹配次数 关联后计入的金额 North，金额 10 2 20 North，金额 20 2 40 合计 4 条关联结果 60 源销售记录并未改变，变多的是关联结果。后续聚合可能隐藏重复的明细行，却仍保留被放大的金额。 先看明细发生了什么 打开 属性查找练习 ，启用无效的第二条 North 目录记录，比较目录、匹配结果和 North 合计。逻辑数据集 DIM_REGION 声明 dimension: [region]，对应物理表 TABLE_REGION。North 重复意味着该源键声明不符合实际数据。 再验证真实连接 执行报表前，在目标连接上运行 dmlc --validate-only ，检查查询涉及数据集的完整键声明与实际数据是否一致。本例中，两条 North 记录会使 DIM_REGION 报告 DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE 。使用 练习中的完整命令和查询 ，修正键声明或源数据，重新验证通过后再执行，避免关联结果重复。 模型、查询、连接或相关源数据变化后，应重新验证。合法的一对多关系需要明确设计计算方式。 浏览器开关只改变虚构示例数据。验证会探测源键和查询计划，不等于执行报表并对照结果。参见 粒度与关联排查 。 English · 下载课程项目"},{"url":"guide--share-denominator-en.html","title":"Why a region filter changes the sales-share denominator","text":"Why a region filter changes the sales-share denominator A sales share needs both a part and a whole. Removing the region grouping from the denominator does not necessarily remove the region filter. Decide whether the whole means the selected region or all regions. Keep the question fixed In February, North has sales of 30 and South has 70. A North-only report still shows North when you change the denominator: Denominator calculation Whole North share Remove region grouping, keep its filter 30 100% Remove region grouping and its related filter 100 30% In DMX, d means dimension and f means filter. The reference {sales: {-d: region}} removes region grouping while keeping its related filter. The reference {sales: {-df: region}} also removes that related filter. These settings apply to this reference; they do not edit the original measure, source key or report GROUP BY. Try both meanings Open the denominator exercise . Keep North selected, then switch from -d to -df. The denominator changes from 30 to 100; the displayed share changes from 100% to 30%. February remains fixed in both calculations. If no related region filter exists, the two settings give the same denominator in this example. Choose the business meaning first For a share of all regions, remove the related region filter from the denominator. For a share within the selected slice, retain it. Neither choice is universally correct; the report must say which whole it uses. Other filters are not automatically removed. The browser calculates invented data locally. Download the existing lesson project for its model and recorded compiler findings; the interactive table is not a database result. Read exact modifier rules . 中文 · Interactive journey"},{"url":"guide--share-denominator-zh.html","title":"筛选地区后，销售占比的分母为什么变了？","text":"筛选地区后，销售占比的分母为什么变了？ 占比需要明确“部分”和“整体”。从分母移除地区分组，并不等于移除地区筛选。你需要先决定：整体是当前选中的地区，还是全部地区？ 保持报表问题不变 示例二月销售额为 North 30、South 70。报表只显示 North，切换分母的计算方式： 分母的计算方式 分母 North 占比 移除地区分组，保留地区筛选 30 100% 同时移除地区分组及其相关筛选 100 30% 在 DMX 中，d 表示 dimension（维度），f 表示 filter（筛选）。引用 {sales: {-d: region}} 移除地区分组，保留相关筛选；{sales: {-df: region}} 还会移除该字段的相关筛选。设置只作用于这次引用，不修改度量原定义、源键或报表的 GROUP BY。 亲手切换两种分母 打开 分母练习 ，保持 North 选中，从 -d 切换到 -df。分母由 30 变为 100，占比由 100% 变为 30%；二月条件保持不变。若没有相关地区筛选，本例中两者的分母相同。 先确定业务含义 “占全部地区销售额的比例”需要从分母移除相关地区筛选；“在当前所选范围内的占比”则保留它。没有普遍正确的一种分母，报表必须说明整体的范围；其他筛选不会因此全部移除。 浏览器在本地计算虚构示例数据，并非执行数据库。可 下载原课程项目 查看模型与真实编译 findings，或查阅 修饰符完整规则 。 English · 进入互动教程"},{"url":"how-to--build-analysis.html","title":"Build an analysis","text":"Build an analysis Use this workflow to add reusable measures and a query that consumes them. For a first compilation, follow Compile your first model query . For syntax lookup, use Model Query . Phase 1: Define the required answer Record the report contract before writing SQL. Decide whether the answer needs source rows or grouped measures. List the fields that identify one result row. State the time range, comparison period, and report filters. Separate report filters from conditions that define a business measure. Record the source population, NULL behavior, and required output columns. List assumptions that could change the conclusion. Check business assumptions before modeling and after execution, including the source population, reporting period, missing values and denominator. Phase 2: Draft the consumer query Write the intended SELECT , WHERE , and GROUP BY before adding measures. Use existing business names wherever possible. SELECT dt_month, 渠道, AGG(销售额), AGG({销售额: yoy}) AS 销售额_yoy, AGG(同店销售额) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = &#39;2025-03-01&#39; GROUP BY dt_month, 渠道 Keep report grouping, time windows, and channel slices in the query. Put reusable definitions, such as same-store eligibility, in the model. Add sorting or a row limit when the requested result depends on it. Predicate order does not change grammar acceptance. Use a distinct alias for each modified measure reference. Check emitted output columns because the compiler can change their order or omit a requested time field on a known defective shape. Phase 3: Find missing measure definitions For each AGG(...) in the draft, inspect the target model. Reuse a measure when its definition matches the requested metric. If a similar measure has different semantics, preserve the distinction in its name and description. Add a measure when the draft needs a reusable business definition. Keep a report-only slice in the query unless the slice defines the metric itself. Trace dependencies to atomic measures. For a same-store metric, the dependencies can include a customer-month amount, a customer-month eligibility calculation, and an outer rollup. Phase 4: Extend the model Define dependencies before their consumers where that order helps readers. Same-file forward references are supported in the checked cases. Declare the complete source-row identity on each dataset. Define atomic measures from dataset columns. Add measure dimensions: when a business calculation requires a particular evaluation dimension. Add +d at the reference that needs a finer evaluation dimension. Use an explicit outer aggregate to combine intermediate values. Define composite ratios from the required components. Add descriptions that state population, grain, and NULL behavior. Test dimension-constrained measures with queries that include the required fields. A filter on dt_month does not by itself satisfy a closed [customer, dt_month] evaluation dimension. For model patterns, see Measures . For descriptions and names, follow Write model metadata . Phase 5: Validate the model and query Use the selected dmlc.exe as the source of truth for uncertain behavior. Record its path, version, build timestamp, and dialect. Lint the changed model. Strict-compile a minimal query for each new calculation. Strict-compile the complete consumer query. Inspect diagnostics, notes, SQL, grouping, joins, filters, aliases, and output order. If a relevant connection is available, run validate-only before trusted execution. Execute a representative slice and compare results with the report contract. If an independent SQL or report result exists, compare row multiplicities, keys, NULLs, and all measures on the same source snapshot. dmlc --lint model.dml --output-format=json dmlc --compile --strict query.sql --model=model.dml --output-format=json --output-dir=gen dmlc --validate-only query.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default --output-format=json Run the final command only when default is the intended configured connection. Use the dmlc CLI covers output capture and validation failures. Check each semantic rule against the actual formula. A same-store count cannot exceed a comparable total count when both use the same population. A subset amount can exceed the total when excluded rows contain negative values. Missing prior-year input can produce NULL, zero, or another declared fallback. Do not assume every YoY metric returns zero. If a strict warning remains, retain the finding and its SQL. An emitted query is not a passed strict compile. If live checks are unavailable, record not run and the missing prerequisite. For analytical conclusions, run queries that could disprove each headline claim. Keep that evidence with the report. Phase 6: Review the final artifacts Follow Phase 6 technical review after the applicable Phase 5 checks pass. Supply the model, consumer query, original report contract, compiler identity, and validation evidence. Re-run affected checks after fixes. Keep business-definition changes visible and return to Phase 4 when a proposed optimization changes the metric. Deliver the model and query Save the reusable model, consumer SQL, and validation evidence together. Record remaining findings and checks that were not run."},{"url":"how-to--compile-and-validate.html","title":"Use the dmlc CLI to check and run queries","text":"Use the dmlc CLI to check and run queries Use this guide to compile a Model Query, inspect diagnostics and generated SQL, validate source keys, and compare results. Read available flags from your installed executable with --help and --advanced-help . 1. Identify the executable and model Record the executable path, version, build timestamp, and SHA-256 before comparing behavior with a documented build. Keep the model, included definitions and relevant configuration with the query: the same query text can produce different SQL after those inputs change. $dmlc = (Get-Command dmlc.exe).Source &amp; $dmlc --version Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $dmlc -Algorithm SHA256 &amp; $dmlc --advanced-help Install dmlc.exe and add its directory to PATH. Run these commands in the directory containing your model and query. Match the model filename with the SQL FROM name. For model.dml , use FROM MODEL . For acme.dml , use FROM acme . Pass --model=model.dml to select a file, or pass its containing directory for filename discovery. See query discovery . 2. Lint and strict-compile Lint the model, then strict-compile the consumer query. &amp; $dmlc --lint model.dml --output-format=json &amp; $dmlc --compile --strict query.sql --model=model.dml --output-format=json --output-dir=gen Inspect the process exit code after each command. Read the JSON status, diagnostics, notes, emitted SQL, and output order. Check the generated grouping, joins, predicates, aliases, and projected columns against the query contract. If strict compilation returns findings, preserve the warning and emitted SQL. Resolve the intended population and NULL behavior before changing the query. Removing --strict does not make the strict check pass. Use --output-dir for compile artifacts. Use --output for commands whose help documents a named output file. These flags are not interchangeable. 3. Capture JSON without changing its encoding For query result files, use the product output flag. &amp; $dmlc --model=model.dml query.sql -o outputs/result.json --overwrite For lint or compile evidence, capture stdout separately from stderr. A Node.js argument array avoids shell quoting and gives explicit UTF-8 decoding. Save this script as compile.cjs beside the model and query. const fs = require(&#39;node:fs&#39;); const { spawnSync } = require(&#39;node:child_process&#39;); const result = spawnSync(process.argv[2], [ &#39;--compile&#39;, &#39;--strict&#39;, &#39;query.sql&#39;, &#39;--model=model.dml&#39;, &#39;--output-format=json&#39;, &#39;--output-dir=gen&#39; ], { encoding: &#39;utf8&#39;, cwd: __dirname }); if (result.error) throw result.error; fs.writeFileSync(&#39;compile.stdout.json&#39;, result.stdout); fs.writeFileSync(&#39;compile.stderr.txt&#39;, result.stderr); const report = JSON.parse(result.stdout); console.log(JSON.stringify({ processExit: result.status, report }, null, 2)); process.exitCode = result.status ?? 1; Run it with the selected executable. node compile.cjs $dmlc If JSON parsing fails, retain the raw streams and process exit. Diagnose that failure before interpreting it as a language error. In Windows PowerShell 5.1, run commands separately because &amp;&amp; is unsupported. Avoid merging stderr into JSON stdout. If you need to suppress stderr interactively, use 2&gt;$null in PowerShell. 4. Validate source keys and database acceptance If the target connection is configured, run validate-only before trusted execution or when relevant validation evidence becomes stale. &amp; $dmlc --validate-only query.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default --output-format=json Replace default with the intended connection. Inspect status , summary , checks , generatedSql , diagnostics , next , and error . Confirm that compilation, reached-dataset key checks, and the generated SQL&#39;s EXPLAIN probe pass. Validate-only does not execute the main result rows or establish business correctness. If DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE appears, fix the complete dataset key, source ranges, or source data. Do not patch generated SQL with DISTINCT to conceal duplicate keys. If a validation query fails to execute, handle VALIDATION_QUERY_FAILED separately. Re-run validation after a relevant model, query contract, connection, or source-state change. If a prerequisite is missing, record the check as not run and name the missing prerequisite. 5. Execute and compare results Run the consumer query on the validated connection. &amp; $dmlc --model=model.dml query.sql --connection=default -o outputs/result.json --overwrite Check keys, row multiplicity, NULLs, and all measures. If an independent result exists, compare both queries on the same source snapshot and scope. If a DuckDB cache is locked, identify the process and connection that owns it. Use another configured connection only when it represents the intended source. Do not change source data merely to avoid a lock. For specific failures, use the troubleshooting index and error codes . Practice a model/data contract repair Run the compound-key and effective-range examples to observe normal validation, a deliberate duplicate or overlap failure, and repaired validation followed by result comparison. This catches inconsistencies between the declared key and observed data before trusting a lookup result. Validation and query execution are separate operations; a successful check is not a same-snapshot or future-uniqueness guarantee. Inspect a change or recover an existing query For a supported variance analysis, use --explain-change with a named measure, baseline and explicit time scope. Read the required inputs from dmlc --advanced-help . For a model containing net_revenue , dt_month and region , the checked invocation is: dmlc --explain-change --measure=net_revenue --dim_dt=dt_month --current=2025-02-01 --baseline-modifier=pp --dimensions=region --model=model.dml The comparison fixture gives baseline 300, current 400, and region contributions +200 and -100. These are fixture values, not the amounts in every example on this site. Check the baseline and current values before interpreting ranked dimensions. Contributions and surprise scores help select an investigation; they do not establish causes or eliminate confounding factors. For an existing SQL query, --to-dml can generate a draft model and query. dmlc --to-dml raw.sql --output=recovered.dml Use a new output path and keep the generated draft warning. These commands were checked with dmlc 0.3.16; the available options vary by operation, so do not copy compile-only flags onto recovery commands. Review inferred keys, formulas, populations and warnings, then compile, validate and compare the recovered query&#39;s results against the original on the same source snapshot. Draft generation does not certify a production semantic model. When integrating these tools, keep database and application responsibilities explicit. Database permissions and application authorization control access; a model filter alone is not an access-control guarantee. Generated SQL may have multiple statements, temporary relations and calendar dependencies. Verify execution on your target engine and measure representative workloads before making performance claims. The compiler does not supply dashboards or scheduling."},{"url":"how-to--define-macros.html","title":"Extend DML with custom modifiers and database functions","text":"Extend DML with custom modifiers and database functions DML is extensible at two distinct layers: define reusable measure/time transformations in DML, or call scalar functions installed in the target database. Custom modifiers compose with measure references; database functions provide calculations whose implementation belongs to that database. Use a custom macro to reuse a time transformation or a formula over a caller-selected measure. Read the modifier reference for invocation and composition rules. Custom macro definitions (dt-macro and fn-macro) Users can define their own macros in .dml — both dt-macro (time-range shifts) and fn-macro (formula wrappers). Define once, reference by name in any measure or query, just like built-in modifiers. Two macro types: Type Purpose Key property {measure} placeholder dt-macro Shift/expand the time window value: (single-point shift) or range: [start, end] No — operates on time context fn-macro Wrap the measure in a derived formula formula: using {measure} Yes — replaced by the caller&#39;s measure Use value: for a time-point shift and range: [start, end] for a time window. A fn-macro uses {measure} for the caller&#39;s measure; a dt-macro uses {dim_dt} for the resolved time field. An optional prerequisite: restricts valid evaluation grains. Choose the extension point You want to… Use What you maintain Apply one calculation to several metrics fn-macro A DML formula over {measure} Reuse a time shift or window dt-macro A DML time transformation Reuse an existing database calculation A database scalar-function call The database function, its permissions and deployment Custom modifiers keep analytical intent in the model and use the same invocation syntax as built-in modifiers. Database functions reuse logic close to the data, but introduce a dependency on that database&#39;s function implementation. Neither removes the need to choose the correct evaluation grain. A verified composition example Custom modifiers use the same reference form as built-in modifiers. They do not require editing the compiler. Start in an isolated DuckDB practice database with two sales: CREATE TABLE sales(id INTEGER, dt_month DATE, amount DECIMAL(12,2)); INSERT INTO sales VALUES (1, DATE &#39;2025-01-01&#39;, 20), (2, DATE &#39;2025-02-01&#39;, 30); Save this complete model as model.dml : dialect: duckdb S: type: dataset table: sales dimension: [id] columns: id: dt_month: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) double_value: type: fn-macro formula: &#39;{measure} * 2&#39; prior_month: type: dt-macro value: date({dim_dt}, &#39;-1 month&#39;) With January sales of 20 and February sales of 30: SELECT AGG({sales: double_value}) AS doubled, AGG({sales: prior_month}) AS previous, AGG({sales: {double_value, prior_month}}) AS previous_doubled FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = &#39;2025-02-01&#39; Save the query as query.sql . With the practice database configured as default , run: dmlc --compile --strict query.sql --model=model.dml --output-format=json dmlc --validate-only query.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default --output-format=json dmlc query.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default The executed values were 60, 20, 40 . Strict compilation, validate-only and execution all passed on the checked DuckDB connection. The model declared dialect: duckdb , a unique source id , dt_month , and sales: SUM({amount}) . This is reusable language-level composition: the macro receives the caller&#39;s measure and its evaluation context. It is not an arbitrary compiler plugin API. Grain rules, time prerequisites and modifier ordering still apply. A custom name does not bypass those rules. Calling a function defined in your database DML can retain scalar function calls in generated SQL; the database supplies the implementation. For example, install this function in the DuckDB practice database before running DML: CREATE FUNCTION business_net(x) AS x * 0.8; DuckDB implements this form as a persistent SQL scalar macro. It verifies database-defined scalar-function invocation, not Python UDF registration, aggregate UDFs or table-valued functions. Add the following entry beside sales under S.measures to call the function on source values before aggregation: # Under the sales dataset&#39;s measures: net_sales: SUM(business_net({amount})) Then compare that measure with a call on the aggregated result: SELECT AGG(net_sales), business_net(AGG(sales)) AS net_total FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = &#39;2025-02-01&#39; Both returned 24 for February sales of 30. Generated SQL retained SUM(business_net(amount)) and business_net(SUM(amount)) . These are different evaluation stages: their equality here follows from the linear function. Rounding, thresholds or other nonlinear functions can give different results; choose the stage deliberately. Validate the function on the intended connection DML does not install the function or translate its implementation between databases. The function must be visible to the executing connection, accept the generated argument types, and be permitted for that database user. Function naming, schema qualification, deployment and portability remain database-specific. An intentionally missing function passed strict compilation , then failed validate-only during EXPLAIN (exit 65); direct execution failed (exit 70). Therefore compile success alone does not establish that a UDF exists or is callable. Use compile and validate , then compare actual results. What was verified The examples above ran with dmlc 0.3.16, executable SHA-256 c019a84c3208aee31cb5fa068232fa24d3258e9cdc83dfc2a91584a86a5ba091 , and DuckDB 1.5.5. They verify custom fn-macro , custom dt-macro , their composition, source-level and aggregate-result scalar calls, and missing-function diagnosis. Other engines, schema-qualified names, procedural/native UDFs, aggregate UDFs and table functions were not tested here."},{"url":"how-to--design-materializations.html","title":"Choose a materialization grain","text":"Choose a materialization grain Use a materialization when repeated queries can reuse stored aggregates at a stable grain. Materialization reference owns declaration syntax and eligibility. Performance &amp; materialize design principles Quick checklist for improving query performance: Ensure time columns follow dt_date/dt_week/dt_month/dt_quarter/dt_year convention (first day of period). Prefer a small set of reusable measures (e.g. sales, qty) rather than many one-off measures. Keep Model Query SQL simple: FROM MODEL , select query dimension fields + AGG(measure) , then GROUP BY . When queries are slow due to repeated aggregation, add materialize at the right grain. When to use materialize : queries repeatedly aggregate the same base data at a stable grain (e.g. monthly by product/store/category) and data is append-only or refreshable by period. Avoid when data is very small, the grain is ad-hoc, or model semantics are still being explored. Grain design principles: Grain — fine enough for downstream rollups, not so fine it explodes row count. Common: DWD (daily + keys), DWS (month/quarter + analysis dims), ADS (report-ready). Store additive measures such as sales and quantity for downstream rollups. Do not sum distinct-customer counts across groups unless the customer sets are disjoint. Keep the entity key or verify a supported representation for the intended rollup. Put row-identity fields in dimension: . Put functionally dependent attributes, such as category_name derived from category_id , in dimension_columns: . See the materialization declaration rules . DWS_SALES_DATE_CATEGORY: type: materialize parameters: dt_date: date dimension: - dt_date - category_id - cust_id measures: - 销售额 - 销售量 materialize is a modeling-time optimization concept. Persisting a dataset into DuckDB as a physical table is a separate operation. See also: materialization examples for worked examples. For setup, hit verification, refresh and result checks, follow the materialization workflow (中文) ."},{"url":"how-to--design-measures.html","title":"Design a reusable measure","text":"Design a reusable measure Use this guide after the analysis workflow identifies a missing business calculation. Define its population and intermediate grain before choosing a formula. Metric-eligibility predicates in intermediate columns Use an intermediate column when a predicate defines which rows contribute to one metric while the dataset population remains meaningful. Do not classify predicates from names such as state or delete_time ; the same-looking condition may instead define the permanent source population. columns: valid_amount: description: Row-level amount gated by validity checks formula: &#39;iif(state = 1 and delete_time is null, prod_amount, null)&#39; measures: total: SUM({valid_amount}) SUM ignores NULL, so the aggregate value matches a filtered sum for groups that contain at least one accepted row. It is not generally equivalent to a source WHERE : a group containing only rejected rows disappears under WHERE but can remain as a row with total = NULL under the gate. Sibling counts, joins, windows, and downstream filters can also change. Use this rewrite only after proving rejected-only group behavior, sibling-metric gates, distinct/join/window population, and absent-vs-present-NULL equivalence. Otherwise retain a row-removing predicate at query/relationship scope or use a minimal documented dataset sql: escape for a permanent source invariant. Decomposition chain example: tax_rate_effective (rate resolution) → amount_excl_tax (tax conversion) → eligible_amount (metric-specific gate) → SUM({eligible_amount}) (measure). The compiler inlines these intermediate expressions; inspect generated SQL to verify lowering and population behavior. Derivative measures Apply report-specific modifiers at the consumption site. Define a derivative measure in .dml only when the modified value must be reused everywhere (e.g. listed in a type: materialize object) or encoded as a named business metric. Use DMX references — never AGG() in measure formulas: sales_last_month: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: pp}&#39; sales_yoy_growth: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: yoy}&#39; sales_by_product: type: measure formula: SUM({sales: {+d: product}}) These measures preserve their named transformation; evaluation still depends on the query context and modifier/filter rules. CTEs and multi-step logic If your SQL uses CTEs, translate them into one of: Measures with modifiers (preferred) — if the CTE just adds a filter or dimension field Column-macros — if the CTE computes a derived column Separate queries — if the CTE genuinely produces an intermediate result set Example : SQL with CTE that filters to &quot;completed orders&quot; -- Original SQL WITH completed AS ( SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = &#39;completed&#39; ) SELECT product, SUM(amount) FROM completed GROUP BY product # DML approach completed_sales: type: measure formula: SUM({amount: {+f: {status: &#39;completed&#39;}}}) -- Model Query SQL: retain the row-removing population restriction SELECT product, AGG(completed_sales) FROM MODEL WHERE status = &#39;completed&#39; GROUP BY product Dimension-constrained measures ⚠️ Measure dimensions: contains allowed dimensions. Each inner list is one allowed dimension; the outer list contains alternatives. It does not define dataset row identity or query grouping; those belong to dataset dimension: and query GROUP BY / columns.dimension: respectively. See dimension roles . Add this constraint only when the metric must be evaluated with a specific dimension: # &quot;Average order value per customer&quot; — customer_id is required during evaluation aov_per_customer: type: measure formula: DIVIDE({sales}, {number_of_transactions}) dimensions: - [customer_id] # ← the only allowed dimension Other formulas wanting to use it must explicitly {aov_per_customer: {+d: customer_id}} to evaluate it with customer_id , then roll it up. Document the constraint clearly—it is powerful but can produce surprising results. Translate SQL into measures Checklist to turn a metric spec or existing SQL (CTEs, JOINs) into DML measures and boring Model Query SQL ( FROM MODEL + AGG(...) ). Start from dimensions, not SQL text: Final query dimension? (e.g. [customer] or [customer, dt_month] ) Intermediate evaluation dimension? (e.g. [customer] for first purchase) Map: evaluate directly on the dataset dimension → atomic measure; change an evaluation dimension → +d / dimension ; constrain allowed dimensions → dimension-constrained measure . Confirm that intermediate and result grains are compatible. If logic truly needs JOIN/subquery and cannot be expressed in DML, stop and reassess—do not bypass Model Query SQL rules. Dimension-constrained measures (encode CTE-like intermediate rows): Define a measure with one allowed dimension: dimensions: [[customer_id]] Roll up with SUM({that_measure: {+d: customer_id}}) (or AVG , etc.) dimensions: restricts validity; +d on that measure in another formula adds the required dimension field to its evaluation dimension first_month: type: measure description: first month with sales &gt; 0 (customer grain only) formula: &#39;MINIF({dt_month}, {sales: {+d: dt_month}} &gt; 0)&#39; dimensions: - [customer_id] first_month_sales_by_customer: type: measure description: sales in customer&#39;s first month (customer grain only) formula: &#39;SUMIF({sales: {+d: dt_month}}, {dt_month} = {first_month})&#39; dimensions: - [customer_id] first_month_sales_total: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({first_month_sales_by_customer: {+d: customer_id}})&#39; Inside a customer-restricted measure, {sales: {+d: dt_month}} is that customer&#39;s sales in that month. To group a report by first_month (query dimension = measure result) while aligning sibling measures with +d: customer_id , see measure-as-dimension: Measure-as-dimension . &quot;Exists / ever&quot; logic (retention, repeat): Customer-level flag: ISNULL(MAXIF(1, &lt;condition&gt;), 0) with dimensions: [[customer_id]] Total with flag: SUM({flag: {+d: customer_id}}) Rate: DIVIDE(total_flag, total_customers) Turn CTEs into measure(s): CTE pattern DML approach customer×month aggregation {sales: {+d: [customer, dt_month]}} first purchase month MINIF({dt_month}, …) at customer grain per-customer flags customer-grain MAXIF final KPI SUM({customer_measure: {+d: customer_id}}) and ratios Target query shape: SELECT AGG(kpi_1), AGG(kpi_2), ... FROM MODEL . +d in DISTINCTCOUNTIF / SUMIF conditions (cross-grain filtering) When counting entities at one grain while filtering at another, put {+d: dimension_field} on the measure in the condition — not on the column. Wrong: DISTINCTCOUNTIF({entity: {+d: entity}}, …) — invalid; modifiers only on measures Correct: DISTINCTCOUNTIF({entity}, {sales: {+d: entity}} &gt; 0 AND …) Cohort scope: define first purchase at an explicit entity grain and history/as-of scope, then compare it with the observation period. Reuse a named classifier only after its allowed dimensions and attachment grain are validated. Inlining a failing expression does not prove the semantics or bypass an incompatible grain. See measure-as-dimension for checked shapes and release defects. Backward-looking repurchase metrics Define the business sentence first (who, observation month, window). Build customer-grain flags, then aggregate: SUM({flag: {+d: customer_id}}) or DISTINCTCOUNTIF(...) . Review with the business owner before changing semantics."},{"url":"how-to--review-model.html","title":"Review a DML model","text":"Review a DML model Run this technical review after the applicable checks in Forward analysis, Phase 5 pass. Review model reuse, query structure, evaluation grain, and validation evidence. Prepare an independent review Give a reviewer a focused bundle. Include the final artifacts and contract rather than the full conversation. The bundle contains the following material. The model and its includes, with changed definitions identified. The consumer queries and original report contract. The executable path, version, build timestamp, fingerprint, and dialect. Compile, live validation, and result-comparison evidence. The required semantic checks and their assumptions. Known findings and missing prerequisites. For a large change, assign model reuse, consumer queries, and result checks to separate reviewers. Keep each responsibility explicit. The author applies accepted fixes and makes the final delivery decision. 6.1 Reconcile the report contract Compare each consumer query with the Phase 2 draft. Check the result grain and population. Check that all required measures resolve to the intended definitions. Check the time range, comparison period, and report slices. Check output columns, aliases, types, and effective order. Remove debug columns that the delivered report does not need. 6.2 Review model definitions Check whether another report can reuse each new measure. Merge duplicate formulas only when population, grain, filters, and NULL behavior match. Move report-specific slices to the query unless the slice defines the business metric. Keep intermediate measures that encode a required evaluation dimension. Retain a named derivative when it is a reusable business metric or a materialization dependency. Check that names, descriptions, and formulas agree. Inspect warnings about similar measures before deciding that two definitions are redundant. Lint changed models and strict-compile affected consumer queries after a merge or rename. 6.3 Review consumer queries Check that grouping and measure modifiers express the requested report. Keep a distinct explicit alias for modified measure references. Remove redundant aliases on plain AGG(measure) when consumers do not need them. Keep ORDER BY and LIMIT when result meaning or a consumer contract depends on them. Check resolved time context and macro prerequisites. Inspect join equality, source membership, fill behavior, and projected columns. Keep SQL readable in saved files. Encode it as a string when a tool requires that format. 6.4 Check results against explicit assumptions Re-run affected result checks after model or query edits. For subset counts, confirm that both counts use comparable populations. For subset amounts, account for negative values before applying an inequality. For ratios, check the declared zero-denominator and missing-input behavior. For time comparisons, check prior-period coverage and the declared NULL or fill rule. Compare representative rows with an independent result when one exists. Do not impose a generic rule that missing prior-year data yields zero. Inspect the formula and emitted SQL. Record absent live evidence as not run . 6.5 Recheck affected artifacts After a fix, lint changed models and strict-compile changed consumers. Re-run applicable live validation and result checks when the fix changes their inputs. Inspect SQL as well as totals, especially after a grain change or measure merge. If a strict warning remains, report the finding. An emitted SQL artifact does not constitute a passed strict check. 6.6 Deliver the artifacts and review evidence Return the model, consumer queries, review findings, accepted changes, and check results. Distinguish passed checks, reproduced defects, and checks that were not run. For exports and dashboards, keep measures numeric and check output names against the consumer. 6.7 Resolve findings Recheck accepted fixes in the updated model and affected queries. Record unresolved findings and their effect on the results. Return to Phase 4 when a proposed optimization changes a business definition. Record that change explicitly. 6.8 Check analytical claims separately Check business assumptions during modeling and after execution. Technical review alone does not establish that a business conclusion follows from the data."},{"url":"how-to--troubleshoot-query.html","title":"Troubleshoot a query","text":"Troubleshoot a query Use this procedure when compilation fails, a strict warning appears, or emitted SQL contradicts the intended query. Use the selected dmlc.exe to resolve uncertain behavior. Debugging workflow (Model Query SQL) Use when compilation reports COMPILE_ERROR or messages mention UNION , JOIN , subquery , aggregation is not possible , ref {...} not defined , measure cannot be redefined , or use existing measure . Read the full error — including generated -- dialect: … SQL in the payload. Classify query type — detail (existence, list, single record) vs aggregation. Aggregation ⇒ FROM MODEL + AGG() (or FROM &lt;name&gt; when the model file is &lt;name&gt;.dml ). Choose FROM — aggregation: FROM MODEL (or FROM &lt;name&gt; when the file is &lt;name&gt;.dml ); detail: FROM MODEL.&lt;dataset&gt; / FROM &lt;name&gt;.&lt;dataset&gt; , no AGG , no GROUP BY . Keep the name aligned with the .dml file ( model.dml ↔ FROM MODEL ). Remove unsupported SQL — no subqueries, UNION, JOIN. Use fn-macros from system_macros.dml ( rank , pot , grand ) for portable windows, +d measures for multi-level aggregation, and .dml definitions for complex logic ( Measure design , Modifier stacking ). Dataset window placement for hand-authored OVER : Window placement . Raw OVER() in Model Query is allowed when no fn-macro covers the pattern. Measure vs column — in queries use AGG(measure) ; in .dml use {column} / &#39;{measure: modifier}&#39; ; do not use {...} on bare columns in Model Query SQL except AGG({measure: modifier}) . Debug incrementally — validate SELECT AGG(one_measure) FROM MODEL , then add GROUP BY and filters. Use dmlc --validate-only &lt;query.sql&gt; --model=&lt;model&gt; --connection=&lt;id&gt; --output-format=json . Reading compiler errors Read the compiler message and generated SQL together. If the cause is unclear, reduce the query to one measure, then add columns and filters one at a time. Prevention Checklist Before submitting any DML or Model Query SQL: Measures use business names, not technical ones No AGG() inside .dml measure formulas No redundant aliases on plain AGG(measure) Time modifiers have a resolved time context and satisfy macro prerequisites Time filters use first-day format Time filters may be placed last for readability; predicate order is not a syntax requirement No subqueries, JOINs, or UNIONs Save readable Model Query SQL in a .sql file Query type matches intent (detail vs aggregation) --validate-only passes on the intended database connection No direct aggregation on MODEL.DATASET_* with GROUP BY — use FROM MODEL + AGG() or detail export Sparse / FOJ composites: activate fill default ( isnull / ifnull / coalesce(公式, 字面量) outermost) on inputs you reference — P28b; embedding an activated dep still expands fill Measure-as-dimension: every measure-derived dimension has a complete explicit entity key ( +d preferred); multiple labels have equal independent keys; every output measure safely rolls up from Q ∪ K ; label filters apply after the label exists — P66–P68 Fine-grain CTE WHERE then outer SUM : use SUMIF / SUM(iif) with matching {+d: …} — not bare measure filters on the coarse query — P69 Window placement: detail OVER under columns: then atomic agg; post- GROUP-BY composite / {-d:} / {-df:} — never OVER on atomic measures — P70–P71 Two grain fields aliasing one physical column: inspect every emitted rollup ON clause for the right key (P72); pk() into a declared-dimension sql: dataset is the explicit-key fallback (P77) Cross-dataset -df rollup composites are FULL OUTER: guard partner-only rows with &#39;{primary_measure} is not null&#39;: (P73); partner temps may carry max() filler keys, so &quot;extra rows&quot; can look like valid self-pairs Never write same-object {+d:x,-d:x} / {+d:x,-df:x} — fail loudly, rewrite explicitly — P79 -f does not drop query population filters; non-grain blocked filters need EXISTS — P80 Cross-grain time dimension: replacement needs calendar bridge + broadcast — P81 Bare {m:{+d:x}} is fine grain only; outer SUM / AVG /… required to roll back (except MAD label bindings)"},{"url":"how-to--write-model-metadata.html","title":"Write model metadata","text":"Write model metadata Use descriptions to help readers select the correct dataset, population, and measure before they query the model. Describe each dataset State what one source row represents and which fields identify it. Explain the business meaning of a headline total, required scope filters, and known time or geography limits. description: |- 一行代表一条管道交易明细，transaction_id 标识源记录。 SUM(出厂价金额) 为管道净额，包含调拨和总仓流转，不代表终端 POS 销售。 官方 UL 报告使用 是否统计UL = &#39;是&#39;。 渠道表示购买方侧业务渠道，与卖出方渠道不同。 If two datasets expose seller and buyer perspectives over the same source, describe the different partner_id mappings. Do not imply that the datasets have different source grains unless they do. Explain columns that affect decisions Use object form when a column&#39;s name does not convey its meaning. columns: Businesschannel: description: 购买方侧业务渠道。模型别名为渠道，与卖出方渠道不同。 是否统计UL: description: 官方 UL 报告范围标志。报告和归因分析使用相同筛选。 去年同期出厂价金额: description: 行级去年同期金额。计算同比前检查数据覆盖及缺失值。 Prioritize columns that affect population, grouping, joins, and comparisons. State known limitations as facts about the source. Do not turn a finding from one sample into a permanent property of the column. Match measure names to their formulas Use business terms as measure keys. Add a description that states the population, required evaluation dimension, and NULL or zero behavior when those affect interpretation. If a legacy name misrepresents a formula, rename the canonical measure. Keep an alias only when existing consumers need compatibility. Preserve dataset ownership for atomic definitions. # Fragment under a dataset&#39;s measures mapping. 管道净额: description: 管道净额包含调拨和总仓流转，不代表终端 POS 销售。 formula: SUM({出厂价金额}) 活跃购买方数: description: 有记录的不同购买方代码数量，包含全部购买方类型。 formula: DISTINCTCOUNT({购买方代码}) 活跃药店数: description: 有记录且类型为 Drugstore 的不同购买方代码数量。 formula: &#39;DISTINCTCOUNTIF({购买方代码}, {购买方客户类型} = &#39;&#39;Drugstore&#39;&#39;)&#39; Do not use a comment to compensate for a misleading canonical name. Put a durable limitation in description: so tools that read metadata can expose it. Set internal SQL names when needed Use abbr: for a stable SQL-compatible internal name. Keep the business key or explicit query alias as the consumer-facing name. 进货额(不含税): abbr: SaleAmountNoTax type: measure dataset: transactions description: 不含税的有效进货金额。 formula: SUM({valid_sale_amount}) Before renaming a measure, inspect formula references, queries, and materialization consumers. An abbr does not by itself make every downstream dependency safe to rename. Document derived labels and estimates For a column macro, provide description: and formula: when its meaning requires explanation. # Fragment under a dataset&#39;s column-macros mapping. 购买方渠道: description: 购买方侧业务渠道，来源为 Businesschannel。 formula: &#39;{Businesschannel}&#39; If a label comes from hardcoded rules, identify the rule and its limits. Distinguish a model-defined calendar label from an official business calendar. If a measure estimates terminal activity from pipeline transactions, state which transaction types it includes. Verify the metadata with the model Check that each name, description, and formula describes the same metric. Check dataset descriptions against the declared source key and current scope. Inspect compatibility aliases and their consumers. Lint the changed model. Strict-compile a consumer query for each changed calculation. Inspect emitted aliases and output columns. Run applicable live checks using the CLI guide . For measure syntax and evaluation dimensions, see Measures . For the full model-and-query workflow, follow Forward analysis ."},{"url":"index.html","title":"Define once. Answer more.","text":"A semantic layer built around composable business metrics. Define once. Answer more. Capture agreed definitions in a model. Compose metrics, adjust the analytical context, and generate SQL for your database. Watch metrics come together Start the interactive journey Watch a metric take shape. Five scenes · about 22 seconds The metric construction theatre Region North South All regions February 2025 ƒ Net revenue 300 net_revenue − Sales cost 240 sales_cost Gross profit 60 {net_revenue} − {sales_cost} Gross margin 20 % Gross profit Net revenue gross_margin By region Year to date Compare with last year February margin 20% Year-to-date margin 25% Change from prior-year YTD Current YTD 25% Prior-year YTD 20% +5 pp 01 / 05 Start with agreed definitions. Business and data teams agree what revenue and cost mean. Play the story Previous Next scene 1 2 3 4 5 Use the scenes to read the story with reduced motion. Illustrative data, calculated in your browser. The model and compiler records below show the checked example; this animation does not run a compiler or database. One rule, different questions. A ratio stays a ratio. For North, February margin is 20%. Year-to-date margin is 25%: add revenue and cost first, then calculate the ratio. The simple average of January and February margins would be 30%, a different answer. Metrics compose within semantic constraints. Context and rule order matter. The model makes definitions explicit; people still agree the business meaning. gross_profit = {net_revenue} − {sales_cost} gross_margin = DIVIDE({gross_profit}, {net_revenue}) Inspect the model, queries and compiler record Apply ytd directly to gross_margin. The cumulative context recalculates the ratio from cumulative revenue and cost; it does not sum monthly margins. This record covers strict compilation and SQL inspection for SQL Server. The worked example explains separate DuckDB result checks; these do not establish SQL Server execution parity. Model Comparison query model.dml comparison.sql monthly.sql cumulative.sql Sample data TABLE_PROFITABILITY.csv Compiler record and generated SQL Read the worked example Start with a real question. Why can’t monthly margins be averaged? Which sales belong in the denominator? Why did a lookup double sales? Which price was valid at the transaction date? Can a running total be reused across queries? Make it your own. Learn to define, compose, change context and check the result. Twelve short chapters, with saved progress. Start the interactive journey Find the exact rule. Explore the concepts, practical guides, language reference and complete examples. Documentation From a shared definition to a database answer. DML is a standalone semantic-layer compiler. People agree the business meaning; DML captures datasets and reusable metrics, and DMX expresses calculations. Model Query selects metrics, grouping and filters. dmlc generates SQL; your database executes it to return rows. Your database and application supply access control, dashboards and scheduling. Validate the model on the intended connection and compare its results before relying on the numbers."},{"url":"journey.html","title":"A small journey through DML","text":"An illustrated, interactive tutorial A small journey through DML Define metrics. Compose calculations. Change the context. Check the result. Build a February sales report in twelve chapters. Start with four sales, reuse the measures, then download your project. Predict the result, change a control, then check your answer. The examples run offline with invented data. Start chapter 1 An original watercolor scene inspired by the gentle storybook imagery of The Little Prince. 01 The rows 02 The model 03 The expression 04 The question 05 The compiler 06 The filters 07 The denominator 08 The intermediate grain 09 The previous month 10 The lookup 11 The missing value 12 Your report Enable JavaScript for the exercises, or follow the text exercise guide . The source examples and compiler snapshots are included in the downloadable project. 01 / The rows First, know what one row means. The traveler counted four sales. Each sale needed its own name. A dataset maps a source table into the model. Its dimension: [id] declares the complete source-row key: here, one sale identified by id . Two sales can share a region. That makes region useful for grouping, but insufficient as the source key. Find the two North sales. Their region repeats; their IDs differ. A source key must identify each row. Four invented sales · TABLE_SALES id = sale ID, region = sales region, amount = sale amount. Illustrative data only. No database is connected. id region amount 1 North 10 2 North 20 3 South 30 4 South 40 Even when the report groups by region, the source key stays [id]. Run dmlc --validate-only on the target connection to check the keys reached by the query against actual data. Fix inconsistencies before execution to avoid duplicated lookup results. 02 / DML Keep the definitions in one notebook. \"What shall we call the total?\" The traveler wrote: sales. DML defines the model in YAML. FACT_SALES names our dataset; TABLE_SALES is its physical source table. Measures name reusable business calculations. Atomic measures bind to one dataset and aggregate its rows or column expressions without referencing other measures. Here, sales sums amounts; cnt counts rows. Read the model names This tutorial uses FACT_ for fact datasets, DIM_ for attribute datasets, and TABLE_ for physical tables. Field and measure names use lower_snake_case . Keep a reusable calculation in the model. Choose report grouping and filters in the query. See DML, DMX, and Model Query . model.dml · the reusable model FACT_SALES: type: dataset table: TABLE_SALES dimension: [id] columns: id: region: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) cnt: COUNT(1) {amount} references the model column amount . An empty column entry maps to the physical column with the same name. 03 / DMX A calculation can reuse what is known. The traveler wanted an average sale. The model already had the total and the count. DMX is the expression syntax inside a formula. Braces in {sales} reference the model's measure definition. Its value is calculated for this reference's grouping and filters. average_sale is a composite measure because it reuses measures. For a zero denominator, DIVIDE returns NULL, meaning no value is available. The formula gives 30 ÷ 2 = 15 for North and 100 ÷ 4 = 25 overall. Both regions have two sales here, so averaging their averages happens to agree. For unequal counts, use total sales ÷ total count. The formula, in DMX DIVIDE({sales}, {cnt}) sales Sum the amounts. cnt Count the source rows. See the complete measure declaration average_sale: type: measure formula: 'DIVIDE({sales}, {cnt})' This composite measure omits dataset: . Its atomic dependencies select the source dataset. DMX also appears in query expressions. 04 / Model Query The same measures. A different view. The traveler turned the telescope toward the regions. The query dimension is the report's grouping: it decides what one result row represents. AGG(sales) requests a named measure; SUM({amount}) stays in the model. The model definitions stay the same. If you know PivotTables, GROUP BY region is like putting region in Rows. AGG is specific to Model Query; it is not a standard SQL function. Choose the report grouping All sales By region By sale Try All sales , then By sale . Expect one total row, then four individual rows. Return to By region . query.sql · Model Query SELECT region, AGG(sales), AGG(average_sale) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region FROM MODEL selects model.dml, which contains the dataset and all three measures above. Illustrative result 4 source rows → 2 result rows region sales average_sale North 30 15 South 70 35 Each result row represents one region. The source dataset still has dimension: [id]. North: 30 ÷ 2 = 15. South: 70 ÷ 2 = 35. The formula is evaluated for each result group. The regional query for this four-row model passes strict compilation on the checked dmlc build. The browser calculates these four-row examples locally. This explorer does not compile DML, execute SQL, or fetch database records. JavaScript is disabled. The regional example remains visible. For all sales together, sales = 100 and average_sale = 25. By sale, sales and average_sale both equal that row's amount. 05 / dmlc The compiler writes SQL. The database returns rows. Model + query Definitions and report choices → dmlc Compile to SQL → Database Execute generated SQL → Result rows Read the answer Compilation produces SQL to inspect. To check a report on your data, follow these steps: Compile, read the diagnostics, and inspect the generated SQL. With a connection, run --validate-only to check reached dataset keys and probe SQL with EXPLAIN. Execute the report and compare its rows and values with an independent result. The browser shows calculated examples. Live validation and result comparison need a database. Compile your first query Read the core concepts Read the text edition 06 / Filter the report and the measure Choose which sales answer which question. The traveler found a returned sale among the receipts. Our four sales now belong to February, with customer and status fields. Four January sales add comparison history. Select North . Expect sales of 30, but completed sales of 10. Inspect all eight example sales Inspect the complete model used in chapters 6–12 model.dml · a reusable measure completed_sales: type: measure formula: '{sales: {+f: {status: completed}}}' A modifier tells the model how to calculate this reference. In {sales: ...} , the measure comes before the colon and the modifier settings after it. Here +f adds the completed-status filter; the sales definition stays unchanged. f = filter; + = add. Read this formula as: sales, with an extra completed-status filter. Two ways to write modifier settings {sales: pp} uses a named modifier. Read it as: sales, for the previous period. We will try this in chapter 9. {sales: {+f: {status: completed}}} uses settings with a value: add the condition status = completed. The braces after the colon hold those settings. See modifier rules and examples when you need more forms. Report region All regions North South Report WHERE selects the report's rows. +f restricts the inputs to completed_sales. The other measure still includes returned sales. 07 / Keep the denominator explicit Thirty out of what? A share needs both a part and a whole. d = dimension, f = filter. -d removes grouping; -df also removes its related filter, for this reference only. Select North here. Switch from -d to -df . Expect the denominator to change from 30 to 100, and the share from 100% to 30%. Report region North South All regions Denominator modifier -d: region · keep the filter -df: region · remove its filter model.dml · measure definition The denominator February stays fixed in both contexts. See the Model Query Only the denominator's evaluation changes. February, the source key, and the report GROUP BY stay unchanged. Without a related region filter, both modifiers give the same denominator here. Model shares are decimals, displayed here as percentages. 08 / Calculate at an intermediate grain Sum each customer's sales. Then take the average. South's two February sales both belong to customer C. Its average transaction is 35; its one customer's total is 70. Try SUM , then MAX . North changes to 30, then 20. South stays at 70. Return to AVG before checking your answer. Outer aggregate AVG · average customer sales SUM · total customer sales MAX · largest customer total model.dml · measure definition Stage 1 · region + customer Stage 2 · region d = dimension; +d: customer adds customer to the evaluation dimension for this reference. The outer AVG, SUM or MAX combines customer totals back to the query dimension. The source key and report GROUP BY stay unchanged. See the Model Query Only customers with sales in the selected period enter this average. Compare source, query and evaluation dimensions : source-row identity, result grouping and intermediate calculation. 09 / Compare periods Look back one month. The traveler kept January's pages beside February's. model.dml · comparison measures previous_sales: type: measure formula: '{sales: pp}' sales_change: type: measure formula: '{sales: pop}' In {sales: pp} , sales is the measure and pp is the previous-period modifier. The month context makes that the previous month. pop returns current / previous − 1 as a decimal rate. See time context . In February , North has 30 in sales and 20 in previous sales. Its growth is 30 ÷ 20 − 1 = 50%. Switch to January . Expect NULL comparisons because the example has no December history. Return to February before checking your answer. Current month February 2025 January 2025 query.sql · report question Keep the query filtered to the target month. The model's measures calculate the previous value and growth rate. 10 / Attach an attribute A lookup needs one matching destination. The regional directory names Ada for North and Lin for South. The model looks up the manager through the directory's complete key, [region] . Add an invalid duplicate North entry and inspect the joined rows. DIM_REGION: type: dataset table: TABLE_REGION dimension: [region] columns: region: manager: # Inside FACT_SALES.columns: manager: 'pk(region).{DIM_REGION}.manager' Add an invalid second North directory row Regional directory February rows after lookup The duplicate toggle deliberately violates the declared key. Ordinary compilation does not read source rows. With a database connection, dmlc --validate-only checks the complete dataset dimensions reached by the query and reports DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE when the declared key is not unique. Catch the duplicate key with dmlc For database practice, save this query as lookup.sql beside the downloaded project's model.dml . Selecting manager makes the query reach the DIM_REGION dataset. SELECT manager, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = '2025-02-01' GROUP BY manager Run validation on the connection containing your sample tables. Replace default with that connection's ID. dmlc.exe --validate-only lookup.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default --output-format=json If that connection's TABLE_REGION contains both North entries, expect DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE for DIM_REGION. Correct the dataset dimension declaration or source data, then rerun validation. Validation checks observed keys and probes the main query with EXPLAIN; it does not execute the report's result rows. This browser toggle changes only the invented data on this page, not your database. 11 / Decide what missing means An empty page is not automatically zero. Select Use a literal zero fallback . Both January region rows remain; their previous sales change from NULL to 0. NULL means no prior value is available here. Filling it does not prove that December sales were zero. Missing prior value Keep NULL Use a literal zero fallback model.dml · measure definition The outermost ISNULL(..., 0) supplies a literal fallback wherever this measure is used. It does not create new months or customers. 12 / Build your report Bring the notebook and the question together. Try it · Build a February 2025 report for North and South. Show one row per region with sales, completed sales, previous-month sales, average sales per customer, and each region's share of sales across all regions. Choose the settings below, then click Submit answer. Report month February 2025 January 2025 Report grouping By region All sales together Report region filter All regions North South Measures to include sales · required completed_sales previous_sales average_customer_sales all_region_share sales_change · optional Choose the report settings above, then click Submit answer to check them. Submit answer Show explanation Your regional report is ready. North: sales 30, completed sales 10, previous-month sales 20, average sales per customer 15, share 30%. South: sales 70, completed sales 70, previous-month sales 50, average sales per customer 70, share 70%. To run it outside the browser, open the project's START-HERE.txt. Create the sample tables in an empty practice database, lint the model, strict-compile the query, and inspect the generated SQL. Record strict findings before moving on to live validation and result comparison. Model notebook · model.dml Excerpt of selected measures and any measures they reference. Updates with your choices. Read the complete model notebook Download model.dml Your question · query.sql The query reuses these definitions. Report controls set its grouping and filters. Illustrative result The preview calculates our invented rows, not arbitrary DML. Only recorded companion queries have compiler evidence; other builder combinations need their own compile check. This page makes no database connection. Download my query.sql Download the checked project See the local compiler commands dmlc.exe --lint model.dml --output-format=json dmlc.exe --compile --strict query.sql --model=model.dml --output-format=json --output-dir=gen These compile commands need no database connection. The project includes sample source data and setup SQL for a separate database exercise. Expected join warnings are recorded with the compiler snapshots; an expected warning is not a passed strict check. Run the compiler tutorial Explore the language reference Read the exercise guide Apply this to your own report State what one source row and one result row represent. Check the complete source key. Name each measure's inputs, filters, time period, and intermediate grain. For ratios, state what belongs in the denominator. For missing values, choose a business meaning before filling them. Compile and inspect SQL, validate on the target connection, then compare keys, duplicate rows, NULLs, and every measure with an independent result. Start with Build a model from a business question . Use Design a reusable measure for new calculations. You defined a model, composed measures, changed the context and checked the report. The same ideas apply to a cumulative gross-margin comparison . Keep these symbols close · quick reference Read a reference as: measure, then how to calculate it this time. These are memory cues, not extra syntax or arithmetic. Symbol Recall its meaning +f +d -d -df pp pop AGG pk ISNULL Source dimension dimension: [id] : identify source rows. Query dimension GROUP BY region : group result rows. Evaluation dimension with +d: customer : calculate intermediate customer totals. See modifier rules and examples when you need more forms. DML defines. DMX expresses. Model Query selects and groups. Compiler observations · Original text introduction · Works offline"},{"url":"materialization-workflow.html","title":"物化：从声明到确认结果","text":"物化：从声明到确认结果 结果正确、查询命中、更新后仍正确，三项都要核对。 只添加声明或看到 SQL 变短，不足以证明优化完成。 前提：已有编译器和目标连接，已验证未物化查询。以下是操作流程，不是所有数据库已验证通过的兼容性承诺。 1. 保存未优化结果 固定数据和筛选，保存原始结果、生成 SQL 和完整键。选择可加的销售额、成本、笔数；毛利率应重新计算，不能累加比例。 粒度设计（英文） 说明如何保留后续查询必需的字段。 2. 声明与安装 按 声明参考（英文） 选择粒度、指标和允许的物化。查看当前版本帮助后，在隔离练习库执行： dmlc --advanced-help dmlc --setup model.dml --connection=default setup 会安装数据库物化过程，属于写操作。检查完整诊断。常规查询可能按需安装或刷新所需过程，实际行为以当前版本和连接为准。 3. 确认命中并比较结果 dmlc --validate-only query.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default --output-format=json dmlc query.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default -o optimized.json --overwrite 检查生成 SQL/执行计划是否读取预期汇总对象。逐键逐列比较未优化结果，独立检查重复行、缺失组、NULL 和未格式化比例。更换维度和筛选后再检查，不假定所有问题都命中同一个汇总。 4. 更新与失效 在练习库增加已知记录，先计算应变化的结果，再运行查询。检查缓存是否刷新、输出是否变化。若诊断要求重新 setup，对同一模型与连接执行 setup 后重测。不要手改生成表掩盖问题。 记录源变更、刷新动作、命中对象和新结果。未完成该步骤，就不能宣称“实时更新”。各连接的刷新机制和权限需求分别验证。 5. 保留证据 检查项 证据 正确性 优化前后结果及差异 命中 SQL/执行计划中的实际对象 刷新 变更记录、刷新动作与新结果 效果 相同数据和口径下的计划；耗时需注明缓存和重复次数 本文补齐操作流程，未宣称新的物化实测结果。声明示例不是数据库兼容性报告。"},{"url":"reference--error-codes.html","title":"DMLC Error Codes Reference","text":"DMLC Error Codes Reference Use these codes to identify the failing stage and choose a corrective action. Inspect diagnostics and generated SQL as well as the process exit code. Path display convention User-visible file paths use forward slashes ( / ) on every platform: User input such as ..\\data or ../data is normalized to ../data in errors. Workspace placeholders use &lt;workspaceFolder&gt;/relative/path (never backslashes under the placeholder). Resolved filesystem paths prefer cwd-relative form when possible. Error Code Categories Category Description CLI Exit Code INVALID_INPUT Input validation failed - user can fix and retry 64 VALIDATION User-level validation failed (parsed OK, but arguments are invalid) 65 NOT_FOUND Requested resource not found 66 CONFIG Configuration error (missing/invalid config, unknown connection id, etc.) 78 TIMEOUT Operation timed out - retry with different parameters 69 COMPILE Compilation failed (syntax / model error) 2 EXECUTION Execution failed but may succeed on retry 70 INTERNAL Unexpected internal error - should be reported 70 BLOCKED Operation blocked by policy or state 77 Error Codes File I/O Errors FILE_NOT_FOUND Category : NOT_FOUND Code : FILE_NOT_FOUND Common Causes : File path is incorrect, file does not exist, file is outside workspace folder Suggested Fixes : Verify file path is correct and within workspace folder Example : File not found: /path/to/file.dml FILE_ALREADY_EXISTS Category : BLOCKED Code : FILE_ALREADY_EXISTS Common Causes : Attempting to create a file that already exists without overwrite Suggested Fixes : Use overwrite flag or choose a different file name Example : File already exists: /path/to/file.csv UNSUPPORTED_FILE_TYPE Category : INVALID_INPUT Code : UNSUPPORTED_FILE_TYPE Common Causes : Attempting to use an unsupported file format Suggested Fixes : Use supported file type (parquet, csv, xlsx) Example : Unsupported file type: .txt. Supported types: .csv, .xlsx, .parquet PATH_OUTSIDE_WORKSPACE Category : BLOCKED Code : PATH_OUTSIDE_WORKSPACE Common Causes : File path is outside the workspace folder (including ../ traversal, absolute paths on another drive, or symlinks that resolve outside the workspace) Suggested Fixes : Use a relative path within the workspace folder Example : File path must be within workspace: ../outside/file.csv. Workspace folder: &lt;workspaceFolder&gt; Path display : User-facing paths in errors and CLI output use forward slashes ( / ) on all platforms, including &lt;workspaceFolder&gt;/relative/path placeholders. Notes : When CLI WORKSPACE_FOLDER is configured, input and output files must stay within that directory. NO_SHEETS_IN_EXCEL Category : INVALID_INPUT Code : NO_SHEETS_IN_EXCEL Common Causes : Excel file has no sheets or is corrupted Suggested Fixes : Provide a valid Excel file with at least one sheet Example : Excel file has no sheets: /path/to/file.xlsx Connection Errors CONNECTION_NOT_FOUND Category : CONFIG Code : CONNECTION_NOT_FOUND Common Causes : Connection ID does not exist in connections.json Suggested Fixes : List available connections and pick a valid connection id, or add connection to connections.json Example : Connection not found: production. Available connections: [dev, staging] LIST_CONNECTIONS_ERROR Category : EXECUTION Code : LIST_CONNECTIONS_ERROR Common Causes : Failed to list available connections due to database or config error Suggested Fixes : Check connection configuration and database accessibility Example : Failed to list connections: Connection refused Query Execution Errors EXECUTION_ERROR Category : EXECUTION Code : EXECUTION_ERROR Common Causes : Query execution failed due to SQL error, data issue, database problem, or stale materialize cache after model changes Suggested Fixes : Check query syntax, data types, and database connectivity. If the message mentions obsolete materialize semver or pls re-setup related model , run dmlc -s &lt;model.dml&gt; --connection=&lt;id&gt; then retry the query with the same --model . Example : Execution failed: Column &#39;invalid_column&#39; does not exist Example (materialize obsolete) : materialize abort: procedure PROC_M__CACHED_SALES is obsolete since semver (...) is different from expect: ..., pls re-setup related model EXECUTION_INTERRUPTED Category : EXECUTION Code : EXECUTION_INTERRUPTED Common Causes : Query was interrupted by user or system Suggested Fixes : Retry the query Example : Query execution interrupted TIMEOUT_ERROR Category : TIMEOUT Code : TIMEOUT_ERROR Common Causes : Query took too long to execute Suggested Fixes : Increase timeoutSeconds parameter or simplify query, consider adding materialize to improve performance Example : Query timed out after 30 seconds Compilation Errors COMPILE_ERROR Category : VALIDATION Code : COMPILE_ERROR Common Causes : DML syntax error, model validation error, SQL generation error; illegal modifier composition (e.g. same-dimension +d with -d / -df in one modifier object — language contract: fail loudly, see modifier composition / pitfalls P79) Suggested Fixes : Fix compilation errors, check model.dml exists in workspace folder, review generated SQL in error.details.generatedSql; for conflicting +d / -d / -df , rewrite to one explicit intent instead of relying on cancel Example : Compilation failed — see details.compilationErrors Notes : The current checked build accepts the checked same-field +d conflicts without an error. Express one evaluation stage explicitly and inspect the generated SQL; do not rely on cancellation. COMPILE_TIMEOUT Category : TIMEOUT Code : COMPILE_TIMEOUT Common Causes : Compilation took too long to complete Suggested Fixes : Simplify DML model, reduce complexity, increase timeout Example : Compilation timed out after 30 seconds VALIDATION_ERROR Category : VALIDATION Code : VALIDATION_ERROR Common Causes : Validation failed for parameters or configuration Suggested Fixes : Fix invalid parameters or configuration Example : Validation failed — see details.validationErrors Generation Errors MODEL_RESOLUTION_ERROR Category : NOT_FOUND Code : MODEL_RESOLUTION_ERROR Common Causes : Cannot find &lt;FROM&gt;.dml beside the SQL / under --model=&lt;dir&gt; / on the model path; or --model=acme.dml was used with FROM MODEL (file/FROM mismatch — exit 65); or a file-only operation was given a directory Suggested Fixes : For query, compile and validate commands, pass a directory containing the matching .dml file, or pass a file whose name matches FROM . For setup, pass a .dml file. Example : model file &quot;acme.dml&quot; does not match FROM &quot;MODEL&quot; (expected &quot;MODEL.dml&quot;). FILTER_VALIDATION_ERROR Category : VALIDATION Code : FILTER_VALIDATION_ERROR Common Causes : Filter syntax error or invalid field name Suggested Fixes : Fix filter field name or syntax Example : Filter validation failed: invalid field &#39;dt_invalid&#39; Dimension Validation Errors DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE Category : VALIDATION Code : DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE Common Causes : The complete dataset dimension value combination occurs more than once, or a range-based lookup can match overlapping source rows Suggested Fixes : Fix the source key, dataset dimension: field list, overlapping ranges, or source data, then rerun --validate-only . DISTINCT / GROUP BY in compiled SQL and DISTINCTCOUNT measures do not repair a non-unique pk(...) lookup Example : Declared DML dataset dimension is not unique in dataset &#39;Order Line&#39; on this connection at validation time. VALIDATION_QUERY_FAILED Category : EXECUTION Code : VALIDATION_QUERY_FAILED Common Causes : Validation query failed to execute Suggested Fixes : Check connection configuration and database accessibility Example : Validation query failed: Connection refused Workspace/Operation Errors NO_WORKSPACE Category : BLOCKED Code : NO_WORKSPACE Common Causes : No workspace folder is set or workspace is not accessible Suggested Fixes : Set workspace folder or check workspace permissions Example : No workspace folder configured INVALID_PARAMS Category : VALIDATION Code : INVALID_PARAMS Common Causes : Invalid parameter name or value Suggested Fixes : Fix parameter name or value, check valid options for the parameter Example : Invalid parameter: &#39;invalid_param&#39;. Valid options: [&#39;param1&#39;, &#39;param2&#39;] FILE_EXPORT_ERROR Category : EXECUTION Code : FILE_EXPORT_ERROR Common Causes : Failed to export data to file Suggested Fixes : Retry with overwrite enabled, check file path and permissions Example : File export failed: Permission denied General Errors UNEXPECTED_ERROR Category : INTERNAL Code : UNEXPECTED_ERROR Common Causes : Unexpected internal error occurred Suggested Fixes : Prepare a minimal reproduction using the support checklist ; send it through your existing product contact. A public support channel has not yet been listed Example : Unexpected error: NullPointerException"},{"url":"reference--examples--advanced-measures.html","title":"Advanced measure examples","text":"Advanced measure examples These fragments illustrate distribution, lifecycle, segmentation, and cumulative ranking. Each pattern requires its named source measures and dimensions. They are not acceptance fixtures. Weighted distribution (multi-grain product) Weighted distribution measures a brand&#39;s share of sales weighted by store importance. It requires multiplying measures evaluated at different grains and rolling up. # Store-level sales (restricted to store + category grain) store_cat_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;IIF({sales} &gt; 0, {sales}, 0)&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, store_id, category] # Category-level sales (drop store) cat_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({store_cat_sales: {+d: store_id}})&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, category] # Store weight = store sales / category sales (store + category grain) store_weight: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({store_cat_sales}, {cat_sales: {-d: store_id}})&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, store_id, category] # Brand distribution flag (store + category + brand grain) brand_dist_flag: type: measure formula: &#39;IIF({sales} &gt; 0, 1, 0)&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, store_id, category, brand] # Weighted distribution = SUM(store_weight * brand_flag) at brand grain weighted_distribution: type: measure formula: |- SUM({store_weight: {dimension: [dim_dt, store_id, category]}} * {brand_dist_flag: {dimension: [dim_dt, store_id, category, brand]}}) dimensions: - [dim_dt, category, brand] Key technique : dimension: modifier forces each component measure to evaluate at its required grain inside the SUM() . The compiler generates CTEs for each grain and joins them. Product lifecycle tracking Track product age from first sale, classify by lifecycle stage, and compute rolling averages that adjust for new products. # First sale date per product (restricted to product grain) first_sale_raw: type: measure formula: &#39;MINIF({dt_date}, {sales: {+d: dt_date}} &gt; 0)&#39; # First sale date, looking back up to 6 years first_sale: type: measure formula: &#39;{first_sale_raw: p6y}&#39; dimensions: - [dt_week, product] # Weeks since launch weeks_since_launch: type: measure formula: &#39;datediff(week, {first_sale}, {Dim Calendar}.dt_week)&#39; dimensions: - [dt_week, product] # Flag: product has 2+ years of sales data has_2yr_data: type: measure formula: &#39;IIF(MAX({weeks_since_launch: {+d: dt_week}}) &gt; 104, 1, 0)&#39; dimensions: - [product] # Total sales in first 2 years first_2yr_sales: type: measure formula: |- SUMIF({sales: {+d: dt_week}}, {weeks_since_launch: {+d: dt_week}} &gt;= 0 AND {weeks_since_launch: {+d: dt_week}} &lt; 104) dimensions: - [product] # Rolling 4-week average, adjusted for product age weekly_avg_4w: type: measure formula: |- DIVIDE({sales: p4w}, IIF({weeks_since_launch: {dimension: [product, dt_week]}} &gt;= 3, 4, {weeks_since_launch: {dimension: [product, dt_week]}} + 1)) Key techniques: MINIF with {+d: dt_date} finds first sale at day grain, restricted to [dt_week, product] . Custom dt-macro p6y limits the lookback window (see Multi-level aggregation with +d Custom macro definitions). datediff against {Dim Calendar}.dt_week computes age using the calendar dataset. SUMIF with {+d: dt_week} filters to weeks within the first 104 weeks (2 years). Rolling average denominator adjusts for products younger than 4 weeks. RFM segmentation with percent_rank Full RFM (Recency, Frequency, Monetary) segmentation using percent_rank() in measure formulas and CASE-based scoring. # Monetary: MAT sales (moving annual total) monetary: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: mat}&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] # Recency: last purchase date recency: type: measure formula: &#39;MAXIF({dt_date}, {monetary: {+d: dt_date}} &gt; 0)&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] # Frequency: number of active sales days frequency: type: measure formula: &#39;DISTINCTCOUNTIF({dt_date}, {monetary: {+d: dt_date}} &gt; 0)&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] # Percentile ranks recency_pct: type: measure formula: &#39;percent_rank() over (order by {recency} desc)&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] frequency_pct: type: measure formula: &#39;percent_rank() over (order by {frequency} asc)&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] monetary_pct: type: measure formula: &#39;percent_rank() over (order by {monetary} asc)&#39; dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] # Scores (1-5 scale) recency_score: type: measure formula: |- CASE WHEN {recency_pct} &gt;= 0.8 THEN 5 WHEN {recency_pct} &gt;= 0.6 THEN 4 WHEN {recency_pct} &gt;= 0.4 THEN 3 WHEN {recency_pct} &gt;= 0.2 THEN 2 ELSE 1 END dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] # (frequency_score and monetary_score follow the same pattern) # Segment classification rfm_segment: type: measure formula: |- CASE WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 2 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 2 THEN &#39;Hibernating&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 2 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 4 THEN &#39;At Risk&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 2 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 5 THEN &#39;Cannot Lose Them&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 3 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 2 THEN &#39;About to Sleep&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 3 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 3 THEN &#39;Need Attention&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 4 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 1 THEN &#39;Promising&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 4 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 3 THEN &#39;Potential Loyalists&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 4 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 5 THEN &#39;Loyal Customers&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 5 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 1 THEN &#39;New Customers&#39; WHEN {recency_score} &lt;= 5 AND {frequency_score} &lt;= 3 THEN &#39;Potential Loyalists&#39; ELSE &#39;Champions&#39; END dimensions: - [dim_dt, customer_id] Key techniques: percent_rank() over (...) inside measure formulas — window functions are valid in .dml formulas. All RFM measures restricted to [dim_dt, customer_id] grain — the dim_dt system variable makes them work at any time grain. MAXIF / DISTINCTCOUNTIF with {+d: dt_date} for recency and frequency calculations. CASE expressions compose scores into named segments. Product power ranking with cumulative distribution Classify products into tiers (Top 50%, Top 80%, Top 95%) using window functions for cumulative sum. # Product rank by total first-2-year sales product_power_rank: type: measure formula: &#39;rank() over (order by {first_2yr_sales} desc)&#39; dimensions: - [product] # Tier classification via cumulative distribution product_tier: type: measure formula: |- CASE WHEN SUM({first_2yr_sales}) over (order by {first_2yr_sales} desc) &lt;= 0.50 * SUM({first_2yr_sales}) over () THEN &#39;Top 50%&#39; WHEN SUM({first_2yr_sales}) over (order by {first_2yr_sales} desc) &lt;= 0.80 * SUM({first_2yr_sales}) over () THEN &#39;Top 80%&#39; WHEN SUM({first_2yr_sales}) over (order by {first_2yr_sales} desc) &lt;= 0.95 * SUM({first_2yr_sales}) over () THEN &#39;Top 95%&#39; ELSE &#39;Others&#39; END dimensions: - [product] Key technique : SUM() over (order by ... desc) computes a running total; SUM() over () computes the grand total. Their ratio gives cumulative distribution for Pareto-style classification. All restricted to [product] grain."},{"url":"reference--examples--aggregation.html","title":"Fine-grain aggregation examples","text":"Fine-grain aggregation examples These patterns evaluate a measure at an intermediate grain, then aggregate or filter those values. The fragments name their additional model dependencies. Measure modifiers defines the payload syntax. Multi-level aggregation with +d +d enables two-step aggregation : define a measure at an intermediate grain, then roll up to the query grain. The compiler generates a CTE ( WITH ... AS (SELECT ... GROUP BY ...) SELECT ... ). Pattern : dimension-constrained measure → rollup measure → query # Step 1: require customer_id during evaluation customer_sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount}) dimensions: - [customer_id, ...] # customer required; additional query fields allowed # Step 2: evaluate with dimension [customer_id], then roll up to the query dimension total_customer_sales: type: measure formula: SUM({customer_sales: {+d: customer_id}}) -- Step 3: use any compatible query dimension ([brand], [region], total, etc.) SELECT brand, AGG(total_customer_sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY brand RFM analysis — full example using +d + fn-macros: # .dml: customer-grain measures for RFM last_purchase_month: type: measure formula: &#39;MAXIF({dt_month}, {sales: {+d: dt_month}} &gt; 0)&#39; dimensions: - [customer_id] purchase_months: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: &#39;COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN {amount} &gt; 0 THEN {dt_month} ELSE NULL END)&#39; dimensions: - [customer_id] -- Model Query SQL: RFM with ranks SELECT AGG(sales) AS M, AGG(sales, &#39;rank&#39;) AS M_rank, AGG(purchase_months) AS F, AGG(purchase_months, &#39;rank&#39;) AS F_rank, AGG(last_purchase_month) AS R, AGG(last_purchase_month, &#39;rank_asc&#39;) AS R_rank FROM MODEL GROUP BY customer_id Inline measures — define temporary measures directly in Model Query SQL via /** dml ... **/ comment blocks, without modifying .dml . Full syntax and rules: Inline measures ( /** dml ... **/ ) . /** dml temp_customer_sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount}) dimensions: - [customer_id, ...] temp_avg_customer_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;AVG({temp_customer_sales: {+d: customer_id}})&#39; **/ SELECT brand, AGG(temp_avg_customer_sales) AS avg_customer_sales FROM MODEL GROUP BY brand Fine-grain filter, then +d roll-up SQL often does: aggregate at a fine grain → WHERE on that grain → SUM to a coarser report grain. WITH sales AS ( SELECT product, region, SUM(amount) AS total, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM fact GROUP BY product, region ) SELECT product, SUM(total) AS grand_total FROM sales WHERE cnt &gt; 3 -- filter fine-grain rows GROUP BY product HAVING SUM(total) &gt; 500 -- report-level HAVING Preferred DML — put the fine-grain predicate inside the roll-up formula; keep query filters: for report scope / HAVING only: DATASET_SALES: type: dataset table: FACT columns: product: region: measures: total: SUM(amount) cnt: COUNT(1) grand_total: type: measure formula: |- SUMIF({total: {+d: region}}, {cnt: {+d: region}} &gt; 3) # No qualifying fine rows -&gt; NULL. Use an explicit fill only if required. report: type: query filters: &quot;{grand_total} &gt; 500&quot;: columns: dimension: - product grand_total: How to read it: both {cnt: {+d: region}} and {total: {+d: region}} evaluate at the same fine grain (product, region) ; iif / SUMIF decides which fine rows enter the outer SUM ; the query dimension stays product . Avoid Why Query filters: &quot;cnt &gt; 3&quot; with only product in columns.dimension cnt left its defining grain; compile/fusion can rewrite to product-level HAVING COUNT(1) &gt; 3 (wrong) filters: &quot;{cnt: {+d: region}} &gt; 3&quot; Compiles sometimes, but inverts the mental model (drill-down buried in a report filter) SUM({total: {+d: region, +f: &#39;{cnt} &gt; 3&#39;}}) The predicate references an aggregate measure; +f expects input-row filtering Fallback when you want SQL-literal steps: keep a fine-grain query ( dimension: [product, region] , filters: &quot;cnt &gt; 3&quot; ) and re-aggregate in presentation after: — prefer the measure form above for curation. Also see measure design , and pitfalls P69 . Limitations of +d : Full N×N co-occurrence matrix (same dimension as both rows and columns) not supported — requires dimension aliasing/self-join. Use multiple 1×N queries or export detail data. When +d appears on a query dimension binding ( columns.dimension: - name: &#39;{m: {+d: K}}&#39; ), that is measure-as-dimension , not the roll-up pattern above — see Measure-as-dimension . Co-occurrence analysis (1×N pattern) — count customers who bought both a target brand and each other brand, using +d + -d + +f : /** dml target_brand_customers: type: measure formula: &#39;MAXIF(1, {sales: {+d: customer_id, -d: brand, +f: {brand: &#39;&#39;BrandA&#39;&#39;}}} &gt; 0)&#39; dimensions: - [..., customer_id] current_brand_customers: type: measure formula: &#39;MAXIF(1, {sales: {+d: [brand, customer_id]}} &gt; 0)&#39; dimensions: - [brand, customer_id] co_occurrence: type: measure formula: &#39;SUM({current_brand_customers: {+d: [brand, customer_id]}} * {target_brand_customers: {+d: customer_id}})&#39; **/ SELECT brand, AGG(co_occurrence) AS overlap_with_brandA FROM MODEL GROUP BY brand ORDER BY AGG(co_occurrence) DESC Key: -d: brand drops dimension field brand from the target measure&#39;s evaluation dimension ( [customer] only), +f filters to the target brand, and the product of the two flags counts co-occurring customers. Measure dimensions: is always array-of-arrays: each inner list is one allowed dimension and the outer list contains alternatives: month_active_flag: type: measure formula: &#39;ISNULL(MAXIF(1, {month_qty: {+d: [prod_id, dt_month]}} &gt; 0), 0)&#39; dimensions: - [store_id, dt_month, ...] Within an allowed dimension, ... (ellipsis) permits additional dimension fields beyond the listed ones, but the listed fields remain required in the measure reference&#39;s evaluation dimension."},{"url":"reference--examples--composable-metrics.html","title":"Compose a metric, then change the question","text":"Compose a metric, then change the question This independent example models profitability. It complements the sales model in the interactive journey . Watch the overview animation to see how the definitions connect. Agree the definition Business and data teams agree what net revenue and sales cost include. The model names these calculations, derives gross profit, and divides profit by revenue to obtain gross margin. DML and DMX capture the rules; the compiler generates SQL for the database. Together they can form the calculation core of an analytical semantic layer. The model The logical dataset is FACT_PROFITABILITY; its physical source is TABLE_PROFITABILITY. Source field names differ from measure names so references are unambiguous. The complete source key is [id]. Use decimal types for revenue_amount and cost_amount in the physical table; CSV does not carry SQL column types, and integer division would lose the fractional margin. FACT_PROFITABILITY: type: dataset table: TABLE_PROFITABILITY dimension: [id] columns: id: region: dt_month: revenue_amount: cost_amount: measures: net_revenue: SUM({revenue_amount}) sales_cost: SUM({cost_amount}) gross_profit: type: measure formula: &#39;{net_revenue} - {sales_cost}&#39; gross_margin: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({gross_profit}, {net_revenue})&#39; gross_margin_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{gross_margin: ytd}&#39; gross_margin_prior_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{gross_margin: {ytd, ya}}&#39; margin_change_pp: type: measure formula: &#39;100 * ({gross_margin_ytd} - {gross_margin_prior_ytd})&#39; Download model.dml , source CSV , or source data as JSON . Change the context For North in February 2025, revenue is 300 and cost is 240, giving a 20% margin. January revenue is 100 and cost is 60, giving a 40% margin. Year-to-date revenue is 400 and cost is 300: the cumulative margin is 25%, not the 30% simple average of monthly margins. Apply the time modifier directly to the named ratio: formula: &#39;{gross_margin: ytd}&#39; . This recalculates gross margin over the cumulative period from its revenue and cost dependencies; it does not sum or average monthly percentages. Prior-year YTD uses &#39;{gross_margin: {ytd, ya}}&#39; . SELECT region, dt_month, AGG(gross_margin_ytd) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = &#39;2025-02-01&#39; GROUP BY region, dt_month Compare the same period North&#39;s January–February 2024 revenue is 200 and cost is 160, giving a 20% cumulative margin. The current cumulative margin of 25% is 5 percentage points higher . Relative growth would be 25%; it is a different calculation and is not the displayed difference. SELECT region, dt_month, AGG(gross_margin_ytd), AGG(gross_margin_prior_ytd), AGG(margin_change_pp) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = &#39;2025-02-01&#39; GROUP BY region, dt_month Download the monthly query , cumulative query , or comparison query . Standard time calculations also require the compiler&#39;s DML_CALENDAR on the target connection. Inspect the evidence The compiler record includes the executable identity, exact queries, process exits, statuses, diagnostics and generated SQL. The three checked queries pass strict compilation. Inspection confirms that the cumulative branches sum revenue and cost before division; the prior-year branch shifts the cumulative range by one year. These observations cover this example and compiler build. A separate DuckDB execution check of these short formulas passed strict compilation and source-key validation. Independent SQL confirmed February YTD margins of 25% for North and 33⅓% for South, with prior-year YTD of 20% for both. This does not establish SQL Server execution parity. The browser independently calculates the invented rows; it does not execute DML. A zero revenue denominator produces NULL, and a missing prior period remains missing rather than becoming a fabricated zero. Continue Use the core concepts , modifier reference , and compile and validate guide for the exact rules and checks."},{"url":"reference--examples--measure-formulas.html","title":"Measure formula examples","text":"Measure formula examples These fragments require the datasets, columns, and measures named in each formula. They are examples rather than executable acceptance fixtures. Business metric examples Financial metrics account_receivable: type: measure formula: |- {amount: {+f: {account: &#39;Accounts Receivable (A/R)&#39;}}} outstanding: type: measure description: All unpaid amounts (including not yet due) formula: |- {open_balance} balance_due: type: measure description: Overdue amounts only (past due_date) formula: |- {open_balance: {+f: &#39;due_date &lt;= date(current_date)&#39;}} Transaction counts number_of_invoices: type: measure formula: |- {number_of_transactions: {+f: {transaction_type : &#39;invoice&#39;}}} number_of_checks: type: measure formula: |- {number_of_transactions: {+f: {transaction_type : &#39;check&#39;}}} number_of_received_checks: type: measure formula: |- {number_of_checks: {+f: &#39;customer is not null&#39;}} General modeling rules (naming, description: , abbr ): model review . Pitfalls: Troubleshooting index P1, P25. Common patterns Average calculations avg_invoice_value: type: measure formula: |- divide({amount: {+f: {transaction_type: &#39;invoice&#39;}}}, {number_of_invoices}) Frequency calculations avg_bill_frequency: type: measure formula: |- divide(sum({number_of_invoices: {+d: dt_date}}), datediff(day, min({dt_date}), max({dt_date})) + 1) Year-over-year metrics annual_avg_revenue: type: measure formula: |- divide(sum({revenue: {+d: dt_year}}), distinctcount({dt_year}))"},{"url":"reference--examples--models.html","title":"Model examples","text":"Model examples These fragments cover query dimensions, fill, materializations, and multi-dataset grain. They require the model objects named in each example. Dimension modifiers Business question : What is the share of each product within its category? Product share within category SELECT category, product, AGG(sales) as product_sales, AGG({sales: {dimension: [category]}}) as category_total FROM MODEL GROUP BY category, product Here {sales: {dimension: [category]}} drops product and region , so sales is evaluated at category-only grain. Using pot (percentage of total) pot drops all dimensions, giving each row&#39;s share of the grand total: SELECT category, product, AGG(sales) as product_sales, AGG({sales: pot}) as share_of_grand_total FROM MODEL GROUP BY category, product For share within a parent dimension (e.g. product / category total), use the dimension modifier instead: -- Share of category (product / category total) SELECT category, product, AGG(sales) as product_sales, DIVIDE(AGG(sales), AGG({sales: {dimension: [category]}})) as category_share FROM MODEL GROUP BY category, product Hidden dimension fields Sometimes a dataset has dimensions that should not be exposed to end users but are needed for internal calculations. transactions: type: dataset dimension: - dt_date - product - region columns: dt_date: product: region: transaction_id: # internal dimension, not for analysis amount: transaction_id can still be used in +d modifiers but won&#39;t clutter the MODEL table. Moving annual total (MAT) SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales), AGG({sales: mat}) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month BETWEEN date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;, &#39;-12 months&#39;) AND date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;, &#39;-1 month&#39;) GROUP BY dt_month mat computes a rolling 12-month total ending at each dt_month . Key points : +d drills down — appends a dimension field to the evaluation dimension, aggregates at that finer grain, then rolls back up. -d removes dimension fields (roll up). dimension: [list] replaces the entire evaluation dimension. Use these to create share calculations, parent/child ratios, and grain-shifted comparisons. Combo columns Business question : For each product, show the minimum invoice quantity where the invoice was paid. Conditional aggregation min_paid_qty: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: MINIF({quantity}, {is_paid} = 1) SELECT product, AGG(min_paid_qty) FROM MODEL GROUP BY product Calendar densification vs fill default Densify (every date appears): ensure a calendar / spine grain exists, e.g. flag on Dim Calendar , then query at that grain. Dim Calendar: type: dataset table: DML_CALENDAR dimension: - dt_date columns: dt_date: has_data: source: &#39;1&#39; Fill default when row missing (block null contagion after join / FOJ): activate with outermost isnull / ifnull / coalesce(公式, 字面量) . Applies at use sites of that measure only — see missing-row fill . daily_sales: type: measure formula: &#39;isnull(SUM({sales: {+d: dt_date}}), 0)&#39; # activate Do not rely on SUM(isnull({sales: {+d: dt_date}}, 0)) alone to fix missing join rows for later composites — activate the measure you will reference, or activate the composite result. Key points : MINIF / MAXIF / AVGIF for conditional single-value aggregations. Sparse dates → densify; sparse measure rows after join → fill default at use sites (P28b). allows: ALL for materialization selection See allows: ALL for syntax and example. allows: ALL permits compatible materialization candidates; it does not relax measure grain constraints. Materialize Business question : A dashboard queries monthly sales, yoy growth, and MAT for the past 24 months every hour. The underlying dataset is very large. Solution: Materialize Define the measures, then a materialization object that pre-aggregates them at a fixed grain: sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount}) sales_yoy: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: yoy}&#39; sales_mat: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: mat}&#39; DWS_SALES_DATE_PRODUCT: type: materialize parameters: dt_date: date dimension: - dt_date - product measures: - sales - sales_yoy - sales_mat A dataset can also declare its own materialization inline ( materialize: &lt;name&gt; on the dataset) — see Dataset definition ( type: dataset ) ; the standalone object form is documented in materialization declarations . ⚠️ There is no measure-level materialize: true flag. The compiler rejects it with undefined modifier &#39;materialize&#39; (verified with dmlc -c ). Materialization is declared via type: materialize or dataset-level materialize: &lt;name&gt; — never on a measure. How it works The compiler registers the materialization and pre-aggregates the listed measures at the declared dimension: grain Queries referencing those measures at a compatible grain become candidates to be served from the materialized table instead of scanning raw data (subject to allows: — see Query definition ( type: query ) ) The materialization is refreshed through its stored procedure ( parameters: ), not in real time Query with non-materialized filter SELECT dt_month, product, region, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE region = &#39;North&#39; GROUP BY dt_month, product, region If the materialization grain is dt_date + product (no region ), this query cannot be served from it. Consider materializing at multiple grains if needed. When to materialize Scenario Materialize? Measure scanned frequently, raw data very large Yes Measure used in dashboards with strict SLA Yes Measure queried ad-hoc, rarely No (overhead not worth it) Measure grain = raw data grain No (no aggregation benefit) Measure involves complex window functions Yes Key points : materialization is declared with a type: materialize object (grain via dimension: , measures via measures: ), not on the measure itself. Materialized measures are still referenced normally in queries ( AGG(sales) ). See Materialize definition ( type: materialize ) and materialization design . Materialize as ETL replacement Business question : A stored procedure builds a fact table ( INSERT INTO RPT_X SELECT ... FROM raw LEFT JOIN dim1 LEFT JOIN dim2 ... ) and a report then consumes RPT_X . Can the stored procedure be replaced by DML? Solution: Model the ETL pipeline as a materialize object Instead of treating RPT_X as an externally built table: dataset, model the entire pipeline : raw source as a table: dataset, enrichment via pk() chains, and RPT_X as a type: materialize that pre-aggregates from the enriched source. # 1. Dimension datasets (the stored procedure&#39;s #temp tables) Dim Customer: type: dataset table: MDM_STORE dimension: [store_id] columns: store_id: store_name: hq_id: Dim Org: type: dataset table: MDM_ORG dimension: [org_id] columns: org_id: emp_name: # 2. Source fact dataset with pk() chain enrichment # (replaces the stored procedure&#39;s SELECT ... LEFT JOIN ...) raw_sales: type: dataset table: RAW_SALES dimension: [dt_month, store_id, prod_id] columns: dt_month: store_id: prod_id: amt: # pk() chain enrichment replaces LEFT JOINs store_name: pk({store_id}).{Dim Customer}.store_name hq_id: pk({store_id}).{Dim Customer}.hq_id # nested pk() chain: hq_id → Dim Customer → ... (hq_id is itself a pk result) hq_name: pk({hq_id}).{Dim Customer}.store_name # three-hop chain: store → hq_id → org_id → emp_name hq_org_id: pk({hq_id}).{Dim Customer}.org_id hq_emp: pk({hq_org_id}).{Dim Org}.emp_name measures: sales: SUM({amt}) # 3. Materialize replaces INSERT INTO RPT_X # Grain keys (dt_month, store_id, prod_id) go in dimension:. # Functionally dependent attributes (store_name, hq_name, hq_emp) go in # dimension_columns: — the compiler rejects derivable attributes in dimension:. RPT_X: type: materialize parameters: dt_month: range dimension: - dt_month - store_id - prod_id dimension_columns: store_name: hq_name: hq_emp: measures: - sales # 4. Report query consumes the materialize report: type: query allows: - RPT_X filters: dt_month: { range: [&#39;2024-01-01&#39;, &#39;2024-03-01&#39;] } columns: dimension: [dt_month, store_name] sales: &#39;{sales}&#39; When to use this pattern : The golden SQL is a stored procedure with INSERT INTO ... SELECT ... FROM raw JOIN dims . The stored procedure&#39;s temp tables map cleanly to dimension datasets with pk() chain lookups. The stored procedure&#39;s INNER JOIN population restrictions become measure gates; preserve the original population and inspect the emitted predicates. Caveats : Stored procedures that use @month (last balance month) to refresh all historical months&#39; master data cannot be exactly reproduced with pk() chains that join on archive_month (per-month master data). Document this as a known limitation that needs result comparison on the same source snapshot. CROSS APPLY table-valued functions and CASE WHEN key remapping require sql: escapes on dimension datasets; declare the resulting keys and validate their uniqueness. Uneven dimensions Business question : Compare sales by region with inventory by warehouse. They share dt_date and product but not region or warehouse . The challenge You cannot directly compare measures from datasets with completely different dimensions unless you align them. sales: type: dataset table: fact_sales dimension: - dt_date - product - region - customer columns: dt_date: product: region: customer: amount: inventory: type: dataset table: fact_inventory dimension: - dt_date - product - warehouse columns: dt_date: product: warehouse: units: total_sales: type: measure dataset: sales formula: SUM({amount}) total_inventory: type: measure dataset: inventory formula: SUM({units}) Common grain: by product and date SELECT dt_date, product, AGG(total_sales), AGG(total_inventory) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_date = date(current_date) GROUP BY dt_date, product This works because both measures share dt_date and product . The model implicitly handles the missing dimensions. If you need region + warehouse in the same query You generally cannot group by both region and warehouse because total_inventory has no region and total_sales has no warehouse . Option 1 : Two separate queries -- Sales by region SELECT dt_date, region, AGG(total_sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_date, region -- Inventory by warehouse SELECT dt_date, warehouse, AGG(total_inventory) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_date, warehouse Option 2 : Use modifiers to drop incompatible dimensions SELECT dt_date, region, AGG(total_sales) as sales, AGG({total_inventory: {-d: warehouse}}) as inventory_dropped_warehouse FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_date, region {-d: warehouse} removes warehouse from inventory&#39;s grain, so inventory is evaluated at dt_date + product grain and can be joined with sales at dt_date + region + product grain. But this is usually semantically questionable — be careful. Selected period and all-history totals -d removes grouping; it does not mean all history when a month filter remains. For these model fragments, use separate explicit query scopes: -- Selected month SELECT product, region, AGG(total_sales) AS period_sales FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product, region -- All history SELECT product, region, AGG(total_sales) AS all_time_sales FROM MODEL GROUP BY product, region Run the statements separately with the model definitions required by this page. For a complete executed comparison within one report, see fixed-axis denominators (中文) . Its ordinary month grouping field is distinct from dim_dt time context. Key points : Measures from different datasets can be queried together if they share enough dimensions. If dimensions don&#39;t align, use separate queries or carefully apply -d modifiers. Dropping dimensions to force compatibility can produce misleading results — document your intent."},{"url":"reference--examples--queries.html","title":"Query examples","text":"Query examples The introductory examples use the shared sales model . The shared model supplies sales and the listed transaction columns; it does not create source data. Date examples use the current date, so a historical fixture may return no rows. Example prerequisites: Basic queries, time comparisons of sales, rankings, shares and product counts use that model. The expense examples additionally require an expense measure; outstanding-balance examples require customer and outstanding ; account-growth examples require account and revenue . Those are adaptation fragments, not runnable additions to the shared model. For an executable dataset with fixed dates and expected results, use the checked sales exercise . Basic queries Total sales SELECT AGG(sales) FROM MODEL Sales by region SELECT region, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region Sales this month SELECT AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) Sales this month by region SELECT region, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY region Detail query — paid transactions SELECT dt_date, transaction_id, amount, is_paid FROM MODEL.transactions WHERE is_paid = 1 ORDER BY dt_date DESC Time modifiers This month vs last month SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales) as this_month, AGG(sales, &#39;pp&#39;) as last_month, AGG(sales, &#39;pop&#39;) as mom_rate FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY dt_month This month vs same month last year SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales) as this_month, AGG(sales, &#39;ya&#39;) as last_year_month, AGG(sales, &#39;yoy&#39;) as yoy_rate FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY dt_month Year-to-date growth SELECT dt_date, AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;) as ytd_sales, AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;, &#39;ya&#39;) as ytd_last_year, AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;, &#39;yoy&#39;) as ytd_growth FROM MODEL WHERE dt_date = date(current_date) GROUP BY dt_date Note the stacking : &#39;ytd&#39;, &#39;ya&#39; means &quot;year-to-date one year ago&quot;. Rolling 12-month total SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales), AGG({sales: mat}) AS rolling_sales FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month ORDER BY dt_month DESC LIMIT 12 Specific months/quarters -- August this year SELECT AGG(expense) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of year&#39;, &#39;+7 months&#39;) -- Q2 this year SELECT AGG(expense) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_quarter = date(current_date, &#39;start of year&#39;, &#39;+1 quarter&#39;) Nested modifiers in .dml # In model.dml sales_ytd: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: ytd}&#39; sales_ytd_last_year: type: measure formula: &#39;{sales: {ytd: ya}}&#39; -- In query SELECT dt_date, AGG(sales_ytd), AGG(sales_ytd_last_year) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_date = date(current_date) GROUP BY dt_date Key points : Time modifiers need a resolved time context and must satisfy macro prerequisites; time filters can supply context too. yoy and pop return decimal growth rates (0.15 = 15%). pp and ya return the absolute previous-period value. Prefer modifiers over manual date math. Rank and filters Top 10 products by sales SELECT product, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product ORDER BY AGG(sales) DESC LIMIT 10 Using rank modifier SELECT product, AGG(sales), AGG({sales: rank}) AS sales_rank FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product {sales: rank} returns the rank of each product&#39;s sales within the query result (descending). Rank within the selected period SELECT product, AGG(sales) as period_sales, AGG({sales: rank}) as selected_period_rank FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product The query already groups only by product. Keep the month filter and rank sales directly; no time-grain removal is needed. Removing a grouping field is not the same as removing the time filter. Filtering by rank SELECT product, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product HAVING AGG({sales: rank}) &lt;= 10 HAVING filters after aggregation, so rank is available there. HAVING clause — customers with outstanding balance SELECT customer, AGG(outstanding) FROM MODEL GROUP BY customer HAVING AGG(outstanding) &gt; 0 Key points : rank returns RANK() OVER (ORDER BY measure DESC) . rank_asc returns ascending rank. Use -d to remove time or other dimensions before ranking. Combine with HAVING for dynamic Top N filtering. Share of total ( pot ) SELECT product, AGG(sales) AS sales, AGG(sales, &#39;rank&#39;) AS sales_rank, AGG({sales: pot}) AS share_of_total FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product ORDER BY AGG(sales, &#39;rank&#39;) pot is measure / grand . Cumulative Pareto share is not a system fn-macro; use OVER() in Model Query SQL when you need a running total. RFM &amp; co-occurrence patterns For RFM analysis and co-occurrence analysis, see the worked fine-grain aggregation examples . Dimension without dt Sales by region (all time) SELECT region, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region Product share by region (no time grouping) SELECT region, product, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region, product Selected period versus all history -- Selected month: retain the month filter. SELECT region, product, AGG(sales) AS selected_period_sales FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY region, product -- All history: remove the month filter, retain the same grouping. SELECT region, product, AGG(sales) AS all_time_sales FROM MODEL GROUP BY region, product Run these as separate queries. -d removes grouping, not the time filter. These queries do not group by time, so they do not need -d: dim_dt . Applying time-grain removal to individual measures here is rejected by the checked compiler. For denominator-specific removal of grouping and filters, see dimension and filter modifiers . Period conversion SELECT dt_year, AGG({sales: {dim_dt as quarter: pp}}) as last_quarter_at_quarter_grain FROM MODEL WHERE dt_year = &#39;2024-01-01&#39; GROUP BY dt_year dim_dt as quarter means the pp modifier operates at quarter grain, even though the query groups by year. The measure is evaluated at the pivoted grain (quarter), then the result is joined back to the query grain (year). Note: dim_dt as &lt;grain&gt; is only useful when the pivoted grain differs from the query&#39;s time grain. If the query already groups by dt_quarter , then dim_dt as quarter: pp is rejected as unnecessary — use AGG({sales: pp}) directly instead. Key points : Queries without time filters analyze all historical data. -d: dim_dt removes the active time field from a measure&#39;s evaluation dimension (use dim_dt to target the active time field, not a specific field like dt_date — -d can only remove dimension fields that are in the current query dimension). dim_dt as &lt;grain&gt; changes the time granularity for one measure reference. Be careful with mixing: a measure without time may not be comparable to a measure with time in the same row. Comparison patterns Period-over-period SELECT AGG(sales) as this_month, AGG(sales, &#39;pp&#39;) as last_month FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) Growth rate by account SELECT account, AGG(revenue) as current, AGG(revenue, &#39;pp&#39;) as previous FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY account HAVING AGG(revenue, &#39;pop&#39;) &gt; 0.10 -- 10% growth Year to date vs last year SELECT AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;) as this_ytd, AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;, &#39;ya&#39;) as last_ytd FROM MODEL WHERE dt_date = date(current_date) GROUP BY dt_date Conditional counts Modifiers apply to measures , not columns. Use {+d: product} on the measure inside the condition: -- Count of products with per-product sales above threshold SELECT DISTINCTCOUNTIF(product, AGG({sales: {+d: product}}) &gt; 1000) as prod_cnt FROM MODEL Or define once in .dml : formula: &#39;DISTINCTCOUNTIF({product}, {sales: {+d: product}} &gt; 1000)&#39; then SELECT AGG(high_sales_product_count) FROM MODEL ."},{"url":"reference--examples--README.html","title":"Examples by task","text":"Examples by task For complete runnable projects, open the task examples (中文) . Each project includes input data, a model, queries, expected outputs and a replay entry. The pattern pages below are fragments unless a page says otherwise. Use the compile and validate guide to check a complete model and query. Pattern Page Shared sales model Source model Totals, time comparisons, ranking, and filters Query examples Grouping, fill, materialization, and multiple datasets Model examples Intermediate grain and fine-grain predicates Aggregation examples Business formulas Measure formula examples Distribution, lifecycle, and segmentation Advanced measures"},{"url":"reference--examples--shared-model.html","title":"Shared model for query examples","text":"Shared model for query examples The introductory query examples use this model as model.dml . Later domain fragments name additional dependencies. Compilation does not establish source-key uniqueness or result correctness. # model.dml (shared) transactions: type: dataset table: fact_transactions dimension: - transaction_id columns: transaction_id: dt_date: product: region: category: amount: quantity: is_paid: sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount})"},{"url":"reference--expressions.html","title":"DMX expressions and functions","text":"DMX expressions and functions This page owns reference forms and intrinsic functions. Measure modifiers owns modifier behavior. Model Query SQL owns query grammar and aliases. Functions in examples are expression syntax; emitted SQL depends on the selected dialect. Reference syntax In .dml formulas Reference type Syntax Example Column {column_name} SUM({amount}) Measure {measure_name} DIVIDE({sales}, {count}) Cross-dataset column {DATASET}.column {products}.price Measure with modifier {measure: modifier} {sales: yoy} Measure with nested modifiers {measure: {modifier: sub}} {sales: {+d: customer}} In Model Query SQL Reference type Syntax Example Column (dimension) bare name SELECT product, dt_month Measure AGG(measure_name) AGG(sales) Measure with modifier AGG({measure: modifier}) AGG({sales: yoy}) Measure with positional modifier AGG(measure, &#39;modifier&#39;) AGG(sales, &#39;yoy&#39;) Key rule : {...} braces are for .dml only. In Model Query SQL, use bare names for dimensions and AGG(...) for measures. The only exception: AGG({measure: modifier}) for modifier form. pk(...) lookups Lookups support single, compound and effective-date keys. Supply the target&#39;s complete key, not the consumer query&#39;s grouping fields. These attribute lookups differ from preparing a relation in dataset SQL or composing independently aggregated measures; see source relations, lookups and metric composition . Cross-dataset column lookups in .dml formulas: # Single PK formula: &#39;SUM({amount} * pk(customer_id).{customers}.tier_discount)&#39; # Composite PK formula: &#39;SUM(pk(brand_id, tag_id).{DATASET_PRODUCTS}.state)&#39; Supply one expression per target dimension field, in declaration order. pk(1, tag_id) has the correct arity for [brand_id, tag_id] ; it deliberately fixes brand_id to 1. Whether that lookup is correct depends on the intended key and source uniqueness. Compound and effective-date keys The target&#39;s dimension: is its complete source key, not the report grouping. These two lookup patterns use the target fields in declaration order: # Target with a compound key: dimension: [id, region] # Column in the referring dataset: label: &#39;pk({id}, {region}).{D}.label&#39; # Target with an object key and validity interval: dimension: [id, &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39;] # Column in the referring dataset: label: &#39;pk({id}, {event_date}).{D}.label&#39; Keep &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39; quoted so the comma stays inside one YAML list item. The event date matches [valid_from, valid_to) : the start is included and the end is excluded. A February 1 event therefore matches an interval starting February 1, not one ending that day. This is not the database&#39;s array-generating range() function. Repeated ids across regions are valid when the whole compound key is unique. Effective-date intervals for the same id must not overlap. A duplicate target or overlap can multiply query rows after compilation succeeds. --validate-only checks the observed key contract; it does not repair the data or guarantee a shared snapshot with subsequent execution. The compound-key and effective-date exercises include complete models, boundary dates, deliberate validation failures and repaired results. They demonstrate finite, adjacent intervals, not open-ended bounds or a general many-to-many allocation policy. For a bridge or allocation analysis, define its full key, weights and expected totals separately; a lookup arity error does not settle that task&#39;s expressibility. Functions Core aggregation functions Valid in both .dml and Model Query SQL. In .dml , they aggregate raw columns ( SUM({amount}) ) or drill-down measures ( SUM({sales: {+d: product}}) ). In Model Query SQL, they aggregate drill-down measures only (e.g. SUM(AGG({sales: {+d: product}})) sums per-product sales into a total). Use AGG(measure) alone for a plain measure reference. Function Syntax Description SUM SUM({column}) Sum COUNT COUNT({column}) Count non-null COUNT(DISTINCT ...) COUNT(DISTINCT {column}) Count distinct MAX MAX({column}) Maximum MIN MIN({column}) Minimum AVG AVG({column}) Average DML-specific functions The context column identifies where a function can appear within a valid measure expression. Model Query SQL still requires measure aggregation through AGG(...) ; these functions do not permit raw-column aggregation in SELECT . Function Syntax Context Description AGG() AGG(measure) Model Query SQL only References a measure in queries (not an aggregation function) DIVIDE() DIVIDE({num}, {den}) Both Safe division (returns NULL on divide-by-zero) DISTINCTCOUNT() DISTINCTCOUNT({column}) Both Count distinct values SUMIF SUMIF({val}, {condition}) Both Conditional sum COUNTIF COUNTIF({condition}) / COUNTIF({val}, {condition}) Both Matching rows / matching non-null values AVGIF AVGIF({val}, {condition}) Both Conditional average MAXIF MAXIF({val}, {condition}) Both Conditional maximum MINIF MINIF({val}, {condition}) Both Conditional minimum DISTINCTCOUNTIF DISTINCTCOUNTIF({val}, {condition}) Both Conditional distinct count Conditional aggregation examples : # In .dml completed_sales: type: measure formula: SUMIF({amount}, {status} = &#39;completed&#39;) high_value_customers: type: measure formula: &#39;DISTINCTCOUNTIF({customer_id}, {sales: {+d: customer_id}} &gt; 1000)&#39; -- In Model Query SQL (modifier form) SELECT AGG(sales, &#39;+f&#39;, region = &#39;North&#39;) FROM MODEL Conditional and utility functions Function Syntax Description IIF IIF(condition, true_val, false_val) Inline conditional ISNULL / IFNULL / COALESCE ISNULL({expr}, default) Null coalescing NULLIF NULLIF({a}, {b}) Return NULL if a = b Utility functions use the SQL expression layer. They are valid formula syntax, but they are not canonical DML intrinsic symbols. The DML function catalog reserves that identity for DML-owned functions such as AGG , DIVIDE , and SUMIF . An outermost null-coalescing formula with a literal fallback activates missing-row fill at that measure&#39;s use sites. daily_sales: type: measure formula: SUM(ISNULL({sales: {+d: dt_date}}, 0)) Date functions (SQLite dialect) Function Syntax Description date() date(current_date) Current date date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) — First day of current month date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;, &#39;-1 month&#39;) — First day of last month date(current_date, &#39;start of year&#39;) — First day of current year date(current_date, &#39;-N days&#39;) — N days ago Dialect note : Match your date functions to the target dialect. DML supports a SQLite-style date expression language and lowers supported forms to the selected dialect. Inspect the emitted SQL; these expressions are not a promise that the target database accepts the same text natively. Modifier syntax in Model Query SQL Two equivalent forms in Model Query SQL: Form Syntax Example Positional AGG(measure, &#39;mod1&#39;, &#39;mod2&#39;) AGG(sales, &#39;yoy&#39;) Brace AGG({measure: mod}) AGG({sales: yoy}) Alias rules : See Model Query alias rules for the canonical alias rules table ( AGG(sales) auto-aliases; AGG({sales: yoy}) requires AS ). Runtime variables These are evaluation-context placeholders, not caller-supplied query parameters. For application binding, see JDBC parameters and query kind : checked physical SQL binding does not establish semantic Model Query binding. Variable Context Meaning {dim_dt} Time-aware expressions and modifiers Resolved standard time field for the current evaluation context {measure} fn-macro formula The calling measure that the macro wraps {dt_date} , {dt_week} , {dt_month} , {dt_quarter} , {dt_year} Standard time references The named standard time field Time context defines resolution and period conventions. Define macros covers macro declarations and variable use. Database-defined scalar functions Scalar calls can be retained in generated SQL, including inside an atomic measure and around an aggregated measure result. DML owns the measure context; the target database owns the function implementation and availability. See verified custom modifiers and database functions for a working example, evaluation-stage differences and validation requirements. This is not a blanket claim for aggregate or table-valued UDFs. Complete lookup examples pk() takes arguments in the target dataset dimension order; it is not restricted to a single key. The compound-key example declares [id, region] and checks duplicate target combinations with validate-only. The range example uses [id, &#39;range(valid_from, valid_to)&#39;] and verifies inclusive starts, exclusive ends and overlapping-range rejection on its fixture. These examples do not establish arbitrary many-to-many allocation support."},{"url":"reference--join-null-equality.html","title":"Join NULL equality","text":"Join NULL equality DML distinguishes joins that combine independently grouped keys from joins that preserve a driving row. The join&#39;s purpose determines how NULL keys compare. Equality by join purpose Purpose Typical SQL Equality in ON NULL-key behavior Combine keys from independent measure results, called key-union composition FULL OUTER JOIN , or densification with UNION and LEFT JOIN Ordinary = NULL keys on different sides remain unmatched Attach attributes while preserving a driving row, including internal nullpk attachment LEFT JOIN NULL-safe equality Matching structural keys can attach even when a component is NULL NULL-safe equality can use &lt;=&gt; , IS NOT DISTINCT FROM , or an equality test with an explicit both-NULL branch, depending on the dialect. A LEFT JOIN introduced for a particular calculation does not establish a global equality policy for every join. Output with NULL alignment keys If both sides of a composition contain a NULL key, ordinary = leaves those groups unmatched. The output can contain two rows whose displayed key is NULL. coalesce in the key projection does not merge them. Non-NULL matching keys align normally. A presence filter can restrict the result to groups from a chosen source. That filter changes the output population, not the equality operator. An explicit expression such as coalesce(region, &#39;UNKNOWN&#39;) can make missing keys share a grouping value. The chosen sentinel then becomes part of the business key and can collide with a real value. Population, fill, and densification A presence measure identifies whether a source group exists. An arbitrary nullable business measure does not prove presence. Missing-row fill supplies a fallback measure value. Densification supplies missing entity or calendar rows. Neither operation establishes membership in a source population. Build evidence The current checked cross-dataset example emits full join ... on t2.region = t1.region and passes strict compilation without findings. That result establishes compilation of this shape, not the correctness of every sparse-key population. Inspect complete result keys, one-sided groups and NULL handling before accepting the shape for a report. The core specification, cross-dataset auto-join defines composition. Model review lists the checks for changed queries."},{"url":"reference--materialization.html","title":"Materialization declarations","text":"Materialization declarations This page owns type: materialize , its grouping key, attributes, and measure selection. Choose a materialization grain covers design decisions. Materialization examples shows declaration fragments. Materialize definition ( type: materialize ) &lt;name&gt;: type: materialize parameters: # stored-procedure parameters &lt;param&gt;: &lt;column_name&gt; filters: # materialize-level filters &lt;column&gt;: &lt;value&gt; allows: [&lt;materialize_name&gt;] # allowed materialize tables dataset: &lt;dataset_name&gt; # optional, for direct dataset materialize dimension: [col1, col2] # materialization grain — grain keys only dimension_columns: # functionally dependent attribute columns &lt;col&gt;: &lt;formula&gt; # (1:1 with grain keys, not grain themselves) measures: [&lt;measure1&gt;, &lt;measure2&gt;] # measures to materialize dimension: vs dimension_columns: — dimension: contains the materialization dimension: its dimension fields are the grain keys that uniquely identify a row (the compiler&#39;s GROUP BY keys). dimension_columns: lists attribute columns derivable from those grain keys — e.g. store_name depends on store_id , region_code depends on store_id . The compiler carries dimension_columns: through the GROUP BY without treating them as grain. Placing a derivable attribute in dimension: causes a compile error (&quot;can be derived from [...], should it be moved to dimension_columns instead?&quot;). No-formula inheritance — a dimension_columns: entry without a formula: inherits the column from the source dataset. The column must exist in the source dataset&#39;s columns: (else &quot;not found from relate dataset(s)&quot;). When the materialize grain is coarser than the source dataset&#39;s dimension, the compiler checks derivability: a column that varies at the finer source grain fails with &quot;definition from dataset [...] cannot be derived from dimension [...], please advise&quot;. When the materialize grain matches the source dataset&#39;s dimension, any source column is accepted (1:1 row mapping). Formula scope — when a dimension_columns: entry has a formula: , the formula may only reference pk() chain lookups and the materialization dimension&#39;s own fields — not source dataset columns or measures (errors &quot;not defined, did you mean …?&quot;). Constraints — one of: dimension + measures (measure-list form), or dataset + dimension + dimension_columns (dataset-scoped form), or dataset only (inherits grain from dataset). Materialization examples contains declaration patterns. There is no measure-level materialize: true flag — the compiler rejects it ( undefined modifier &#39;materialize&#39; , see Troubleshooting index P64). Declaration forms: this section, and dataset-level materialize: &lt;name&gt; in model structure . Eligibility, selection and measured performance allows: controls eligible materializations. A declaration alone does not establish which stored aggregate a query selects. Inspect generated SQL or the execution plan for the actual object on the target engine. Compare optimized and unoptimized results at complete keys, including unmatched groups, NULLs and ratios. After changing source data, check refresh and the new results. Measure representative workloads only after these checks; shorter SQL or faster compilation does not establish database query performance. Refresh permissions and scheduling belong to the deployment. For setup, hit verification, refresh and result checks, follow the materialization workflow (中文) ."},{"url":"reference--measures.html","title":"Measure declarations","text":"Measure declarations This page owns measure properties and formula forms. Design measures covers authoring decisions. Formula examples contains business patterns. Basic syntax measure_name: type: measure description: Business description dataset: dataset_name formula: aggregation_expression dimensions: # optional allowed evaluation dimensions - [customer_id] - [customer_id, ...] # listed fields remain required prerequisite: validity_expression formula: is required. dataset: binds a top-level atomic measure to its source. A measure under a dataset&#39;s measures: block inherits that dataset. Composite measures reference other measures and omit dataset: . dimensions: is an array of arrays. Each inner list is one alternative allowed evaluation dimension. A flat list fails with string found, array expected . An ellipsis permits additional fields, while every listed field remains required. The property constrains evaluation; it does not declare dataset identity or the query&#39;s grouping. prerequisite: adds a grain-validity expression. The common metadata fields description , abbr , and tags are described in model metadata fields . Design measures covers how to choose these constraints. Notes: Apply query-specific filtering and grouping in the model query (SQL WHERE / GROUP BY , or DML format filters: / columns: dimension: ) — not in one-off measures. Model Query rules: Model Query SQL . dataset: is only for atomic measures. Their formulas directly aggregate that dataset&#39;s rows or column expressions without referencing other measures. Composite measures reference other measures and must not declare dataset: . This remains true when all referenced measures use the same dataset. See Measure References and pitfalls P39 . Measures may return numeric, date or classifier values. Keep display formatting in the consumer; choose aggregation and type to match the business definition. For monetary or precision-sensitive raw fields, prefer casting once in an atomic measure (for example SUM(CAST({amount} AS DECIMAL(38, 2))) ) and reusing that measure downstream. When business rules rely on &gt; 0 / &lt; 0 comparisons, ensure the base measure is already typed as intended (e.g. DECIMAL) before reusing it in MINIF / MAXIF / DISTINCTCOUNTIF conditions. Simple aggregations COUNT number_of_transactions: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: count(distinct transaction_id) number_of_products: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: count(distinct product) SUM amount: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM(CAST({raw_amount} AS DECIMAL(38, 2))) transaction_quantity: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM(quantity) MAX/MIN last_order_date: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: MAX(dt_date) AVG avg_invoice_quantity: type: measure formula: &#39;AVG({invoice_quantity: {+d: transaction_id}})&#39; Filtered measures Use inline filters with {+f: condition} . Modifier syntax reference: Function modifiers . expense: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: |- {debit: {+f: {account_type: [&#39;Expense&#39;, &#39;Other Expense&#39;]}}} sales: type: measure description: Sales revenue from income accounts formula: |- {credit: {+f: {account_type: [&#39;Income&#39;, &#39;Other Income&#39;]}}} food_and_drink_expense: type: measure formula: |- {expense: {+f: &quot;instr(account, &#39;Food &amp; drink&#39;) &gt; 0&quot;}} number_of_credit_card_transactions: type: measure formula: |- {number_of_transactions: {+f: {credit_card : &#39;yes&#39;}}} number_of_unpaid_invoices: type: measure formula: |- {number_of_invoices: {+f: &#39;open_balance &gt; 0&#39;}} Measure references Reference other measures in formulas: Composite measures must not declare dataset: . If formula: references another measure, omit dataset: . Atomic measures use dataset: to bind the source rows or column expressions they aggregate without referencing other measures. Atomic examples below ( credit / debit ) keep dataset: ; the composite profit omits it. See pitfalls P39 . # Base measures credit: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM(credit) debit: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM(debit) # Derived measure (composite — no dataset:) profit: type: measure formula: |- {credit: {+f: {transaction_type: [&#39;Income&#39;,&#39;Other Income&#39;,&#39;Expense&#39;,&#39;Other Expense&#39;]}}} - {debit: {+f: {transaction_type: [&#39;Income&#39;,&#39;Other Income&#39;,&#39;Expense&#39;,&#39;Other Expense&#39;]}}} Measure aliases Create aliases for existing measures: revenue: |- {sales} income: |- {sales} outgoing: |- {expense} Complex formulas Conditional aggregations SUMIF / DISTINCTCOUNTIF / MINIF / MAXIF that reference other measures are composite: do not declare dataset: (even when a column in the formula “belongs” to a known dataset). Use dataset: only on a separate atomic measure if you need column-scoped aggregation. See Measure References and pitfalls P39 . incoming_payment: type: measure formula: |- SUMIF({payment: {+d: customer}}, {customer} is not null) outgoing_payment: type: measure formula: |- SUMIF({payment: {+d: vendor}}, {vendor} is not null) Nested aggregations max_amount_per_invoice: type: measure formula: |- MAX({sales: {+d: transaction_id}}) daily_avg_sales: type: measure formula: |- divide(sum({sales: {+d: dt_date}}), distinctcount({dt_date})) weekly_avg_sales: type: measure formula: |- divide(sum({sales: {+d: dt_week}}), distinctcount({dt_week})) avg_sales_per_trx: type: measure formula: |- divide(sum({sales: {+d: transaction_id}}), distinctcount({transaction_id})) Window functions Window placement — full contract: Window placement . Summary: Intent Placement Detail OVER (no GROUP BY ; row attribute for later DISTINCTCOUNTIF / outer SUM / LAG gates) Calculated column under columns: , then atomic agg Post- GROUP-BY rank / share / running total Transitional atomic agg + composite window / {-d:} / {-df:} / grand (no dataset: ) Atomic measures reject OVER ( window functions are not allowed in atomic measures ). Pitfalls: P70 , P71 . Detail (bare partition cols under columns: ): fact: type: dataset table: FACT_SALES columns: store_id: prod_id: qty: net_qty: &#39;SUM({qty}) over (partition by {store_id}, {prod_id})&#39; measures: sum_net_qty: &#39;SUM({net_qty})&#39; Post-aggregation (grain refs on the composite): total_sales: type: measure dataset: fact formula: SUM({qty}) region_rank: type: measure formula: &#39;rank() over (partition by {-d: product} order by {total_sales} desc)&#39; In Model Query SQL — prefer fn-macros from system_macros.dml ( rank , rank_asc , pot , grand ) when they cover the pattern: SELECT product, AGG(sales, &#39;rank&#39;) AS sales_rank FROM MODEL GROUP BY product SELECT product, AGG({sales: pot}) AS share FROM MODEL GROUP BY product See function modifiers for macro categories and behavior. ISNULL and IIF all_product_sales: type: measure description: Product sales including products with zero sales formula: |- SUM(isnull({sales: {+d: product}}, 0) * iif({DATASET_PRODUCT_SERVICE}.product is not null, 1, 0)) all_customer_sales: type: measure description: Customer sales including customers with zero sales formula: |- SUM(isnull({sales: {+d: customer}}, 0) * iif({DATASET_CUSTOMER}.customer is not null, 1, 0)) Fill default when row missing Feature: fill default when a joined / FOJ / materialize row is missing (blocks null contagion into further math). Activate by wrapping the measure formula outermost with isnull / ifnull / coalesce(公式, 字面量) . The default applies at use sites of that measure (query column, rename, {m: pp} / other dt modifiers, or embedded in another formula) when the site can be sparse (FOJ / nullpk / materialize wrap). Two distinct rules (do not conflate): No invented outer wrap: mentioning a filled measure inside a composite does not by itself wrap that composite’s result column. Activate on the result if its column must be non-null when missing. Embedding still expands: when a composite embeds an already-activated dependency (or a chain of formula refs into one), sparse FOJ use sites still expand fill at the caller of that embedding — e.g. deep = {q2fp1} * 3 with q2fp1 = {q2f} + 1 and q2f = isnull(SUM(...), 0) yields (coalesce(dep, 0) + 1) * 3 on missing partner rows, not NULL. That is fill of the activated dep, not an automatic isnull(deep, …) . 上期: type: measure formula: &#39;isnull({销售额: pp}, 0)&#39; # activate 上上期: type: measure formula: &#39;{上期: pp}&#39; # use site → 0 本期减上期: type: measure formula: &#39;{销售额} - {上期}&#39; # 上期 use site → 0 (no contagion) 本期减上期_fill: type: measure formula: &#39;isnull({销售额} - {上期}, 0)&#39; # activate on result # Nested embed (activation on q2f; deep expands fill of q2f at FOJ) q2f: { dataset: secondary, formula: &#39;isnull(SUM({q2}), 0)&#39; } q2fp1: { formula: &#39;{q2f} + 1&#39; } deep: { formula: &#39;{q2fp1} * 3&#39; } Not activation (still may be null when the row is missing): Shape Why Bare {销售额: pp} Not activated isnull({a}, {b}) / coalesce(a, b, 0) Measure complement, not (公式, 字面量) isnull(…, 0) + 1 as the activated measure itself Eval-at-null on that measure (often → 1), not “row missing → 0” for this measure’s identity Result column never activated Dep may fill inside the formula body; the result column is not auto-wrapped at its further use sites unless activated Same idea as MetricFlow fill_nulls_with / Looker post-agg COALESCE : declare on a measure; fill when that measure is used. DML does not create missing calendar rows — only fills when the joined column is null. Densify dates separately (calendar / FOJ grain). Pitfall: Troubleshooting index P28b. Compiler notes (dmlc): docs/fill-default-when-row-missing.md . Reusable chronological running measures A named composite measure can contain a post-aggregation window. The running-sales example defines SUM({sales}) OVER (ORDER BY {month} ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) at the declared [month] grain. It has no regional partition, and filtering to February changes its starting point. A running sum of additive amounts is not a cumulative distinct count or a cumulative ratio. The example includes the full model, queries and expected rows. dimension: [] clears evaluation grouping for a total; it does not specify an ordering or request accumulation. For a balance at the last observation, define whether the date is per entity or global and how missing observations and ties behave. An ordered sum or one last-date recipe does not establish every semi-additive calculation."},{"url":"reference--model-composition.html","title":"Model composition and name resolution","text":"Model composition and name resolution This reference defines same-file visibility, include scope, and logical versus physical names in dataset SQL. Same file The checked build resolves same-file forward references. A measure can precede its dataset or another measure. A calculated column can precede its dependency, and a pk() target dataset can appear later. Dependency-first order helps readers but is not a compilation requirement for these cases. Missing names and cycles remain errors. Includes Each included file resolves from its own definitions and includes. A child cannot borrow a dataset defined only in its parent. A failed include invalidates the containing model. Include paths resolve relative to the including file first. Environment model paths are deployment inputs and do not declare dependencies. Session history does not extend a module&#39;s declared visibility. A reusable base model can own shared datasets and measures, with each report including that model. Logical versus physical SQL In dataset sql: , FROM {Customers} references the logical dataset. FROM Customers names a physical relation. Within column formulas, {net_amount} expands the model column, while bare net_amount is physical SQL. Reverse-generated SQL can deliberately retain physical references. Direct query files For direct DML compilation, the query key must match the file stem: report.dml contains report: {type: query, ...} . A reusable model without a query is consumed by Model Query SQL; its FROM name follows the model filename ( query discovery ). Check includes, names and generated SQL with your installed compiler before running a report."},{"url":"reference--model-query-sql.html","title":"Model Query SQL","text":"Model Query SQL This page owns SQL query syntax, model discovery, aliases, grouping, and output rules. YAML query declarations describes type: query . Compile a first query provides a complete tutorial. Two query surfaces Syntax Format When to use Model Query SQL .sql file or inline string SQL-literate users, CLI, dashboards DML format type: query in .dml YAML YAML-native environments, dmlc --to-dml output Query types Aggregation queries Use FROM … with at least one measure via AGG() . The name after FROM is the model file without .dml (case-insensitive). Most tutorials use the conventional pair FROM MODEL ↔ model.dml . Model file Write in SQL model.dml FROM MODEL acme.dml FROM acme 渠道表现模型.dml FROM 渠道表现模型 How --model= interacts with that name (query / compile / validate-only): You pass What happens --model=some/dir dmlc looks for &lt;FROM&gt;.dml inside that directory ( FROM MODEL → dir/model.dml , FROM acme → dir/acme.dml ) --model=acme.dml that file is the model; SQL must say FROM acme (not FROM MODEL ) omitted same discovery beside the SQL file (or on the model search path) Do not put .dml in the FROM clause. Nested queries/ layouts: put the .dml beside the SQL, or pass --model= as the directory that contains it. setup ( dmlc -s ) takes a .dml file , not a directory. -- model.dml SELECT product, dt_month, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;, &#39;-1 month&#39;) GROUP BY product, dt_month -- acme.dml (dmlc --model=acme.dml … or --model=&lt;dir containing acme.dml&gt;) SELECT product, dt_month, AGG(sales) FROM acme GROUP BY product, dt_month In the IDE, a pinned Model may still let navigation treat FROM MODEL as that pin. On the CLI, keep FROM aligned with the file you use (or with the .dml discovered under --model=&lt;dir&gt; ). Detail queries Use FROM &lt;model&gt;.&lt;dataset&gt; for dataset rows. Direct GROUP BY is rejected: define reusable measures and query the model for grouped metrics. Use AGG() on the model aggregation surface. -- model.dml SELECT dt_date, transaction_id, raw_amount FROM MODEL.transactions WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) -- acme.dml SELECT ClaimNumber, ClaimOpenDate FROM acme.Claim Detail queries expose dataset rows. They also accept the checked ungrouped aggregate and window expressions. A direct GROUP BY is rejected. Aggregation queries expose reusable model measures at the requested grouping. Model Query SQL specification Fundamental constraints Prohibited Why Alternative Subqueries Not supported in Model Query SQL Model the calculation with measures, or prepare the relation in dataset sql: UNION / UNION ALL Not supported in Model Query SQL Prepare a union relation in dataset sql: or a database view JOIN Not supported in Model Query SQL Model attribute lookups with pk(...) , or prepare a relation in dataset sql: {...} on bare columns Braces are for .dml only — silently stripped Use bare names: SELECT product, AGG(sales) These consumer-query restrictions do not prohibit SQL throughout DML. Dataset sql: can prepare supported joins, unions and subqueries; reference declared datasets as {DATASET} and respect physical table ownership. That relation must have an explicit correct key. The dataset reference also describes the validation-probe failure observed for the checked derived relations, separately from their successful execution. Filters baked into its SQL remain part of its population; moving SQL to the model does not automatically make every rule responsive to consumer context. See dataset definitions . Window functions ( OVER() ) are allowed in Model Query SQL — the compiler passes them through to generated SQL. For common patterns, prefer fn-macros from system_macros.dml ( rank , rank_asc , pot , grand ) — see Function modifiers . Where OVER belongs in a .dml model ( window placement ): Window placement . SELECT rules Aggregation : Use AGG(measure) for every measure. Use bare names for dimensions. Detail : Use bare names for all columns. Add DISTINCT when listing unique values. -- Aggregation SELECT product, dt_month, AGG(sales), AGG({sales: yoy}) AS sales_yoy FROM MODEL GROUP BY product, dt_month -- Detail SELECT DISTINCT product FROM MODEL.transactions Alias rules Pattern Alias needed? AGG(sales) No — auto-aliases to sales AGG(sales) AS sales accepted but redundant(pass-through) AGG({sales: yoy}) Yes — AS sales_yoy AGG(sales, &#39;pop&#39;) AS sales Wrong — circular reference; use AS sales_mom AGG({sales: yoy}) AS sales Wrong — circular reference; use AS sales_yoy A prior select-list alias is a SQL result column , not a measure. When a later expression uses that name, such as AGG(current_value) after AGG(sales) AS current_value , the name refers to the prior result column. It does not fall back to an unrelated same-named DML measure. Aliasing a measure reference with a modifier back to the measure&#39;s own name (e.g. AGG(sales, &#39;pop&#39;) AS sales ) causes a circular reference because the compiler treats the alias as a redefinition of the measure. Use a distinct alias (e.g. AS sales_mom ). Without a modifier, AGG(sales) AS sales is accepted as a redundant pass-through. The first argument of AGG names the measure: use AGG(sales) for its ordinary value or AGG({sales: yoy}) to modify this reference. Use bare column names elsewhere in Model Query SQL. WHERE rules Use bare names for columns: WHERE customer = &#39;John Doe&#39; Predicate order is not a syntax requirement. Use first-day format for time filters: dt_month = &#39;2024-01-01&#39; Prefer = over BETWEEN for single periods Don&#39;t add redundant null filters unless explicitly requested GROUP BY rules Every non-aggregated SELECT field must appear in GROUP BY Keep GROUP BY order aligned with SELECT when output order matters. The checked compiler accepts a different order and may reorder visible columns; inspect artifacts.sql and effectiveOutputOrder Time modifiers need a resolved time context: a grouped time field or a time filter can supply it. Prefer explicit time grouping for multi-period reports; a single-period filter can supply a hidden time context -- Correct: time modifier + matching time field in GROUP BY SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales), AGG({sales: yoy}) AS sales_yoy FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month -- Wrong: yoy with neither a grouped time field nor a time filter SELECT product, AGG(sales), AGG({sales: yoy}) FROM MODEL GROUP BY product ORDER BY / LIMIT Always specify ASC/DESC Only add if semantically necessary (Top N, extreme values) Don&#39;t add &quot;nice to have&quot; sorting Measure reference rules In queries: AGG(measure) — always wrap in AGG() Modifier form: AGG({measure: modifier}) or AGG(measure, &#39;modifier&#39;) Never use {...} on bare columns in Model Query SQL (except modifier form inside AGG ) — braces on bare columns are silently stripped to the bare name; use bare names directly If the compiler says use existing measure &quot;X&quot; , use AGG(X) — don&#39;t re-express the formula Time handling Field naming : dt_date , dt_week , dt_month , dt_quarter , dt_year — all first-day format. System derivation : when the model explicitly declares a finer standard time field such as dt_date , supported coarser fields such as dt_month are available automatically in that dataset. Use the derived name directly; do not add duplicate physical columns only to satisfy a query, filter, or editor diagnostic. Go-to-definition on the derived name resolves to the explicit source time column. Filtering : WHERE dt_month = &#39;2024-01-01&#39; (first day, = not BETWEEN for single periods). The date function reference describes SQLite-style expression forms. The compiler lowers supported forms to the selected dialect. For the checked query requesting both dt_year and dt_month , compilation rejects the query because dt_year is absent from the generated output. Do not treat this combination as a successful two-field report. If you only need the year as a display label, query at the required month grain and derive the label from that key after querying; verify the result grain separately. This does not establish that every query containing multiple time fields is unsupported. HAVING -- Customers with outstanding balance SELECT customer, AGG(outstanding) FROM MODEL GROUP BY customer HAVING AGG(outstanding) &gt; 0 -- Products selling less than last month SELECT product, AGG(sales) as this_month, AGG(sales, &#39;pp&#39;) as last_month FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product HAVING AGG(sales) &lt; AGG(sales, &#39;pp&#39;) Measure-as-dimension note: a predicate on the classifier label (and/or sibling measures at label grain) is a label-pass filter. Model Query text may show HAVING , but the semantic contract is “after the label exists.” Compiled SQL often applies it as WHERE on the post-label CTE. Do not treat a doc-surface HAVING as permission to filter before classification. See classifier-label filters . Quick reference Pattern SQL Total SELECT AGG(sales) FROM MODEL By dimension SELECT product, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY product Filtered SELECT AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE region = &#39;North&#39; Top N SELECT product, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY product ORDER BY AGG(sales) DESC LIMIT 10 Growth rate SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales, &#39;yoy&#39;) AS sales_yoy FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month Period comparison SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales), AGG(sales, &#39;pp&#39;) AS sales_pp FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month Share of total SELECT product, AGG({sales: pot}) AS sales_share FROM MODEL GROUP BY product Ranking SELECT product, AGG(sales, &#39;rank&#39;) AS sales_rank FROM MODEL GROUP BY product Multi-level agg SELECT brand, AGG(total_customer_sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY brand (requires +d measure in .dml or inline /** dml ... **/ ) Inline measure /** dml temp_m: ... **/ SELECT AGG(temp_m) FROM MODEL Filtered measure SELECT AGG(sales, &#39;+f&#39;, region = &#39;North&#39;) FROM MODEL Time-comparison syntax can produce strict findings for key-union joins on the checked build. These examples show query forms. Read the diagnostics and inspect the emitted joins before executing. Aggregation lookup table Model Query SQL ( FROM MODEL ) requires all aggregations to go through measures defined in model.dml or inline /** dml ... **/ blocks ( inline measures ). Raw-column aggregations are rejected. What you want Wrong (will fail) Right Total rows COUNT(*) Define 笔数: COUNT({ID}) in model.dml or inline, then AGG(笔数) Sum of raw column SUM(出厂价金额) Define 销售额: SUM({出厂价金额}) in model.dml or inline, then AGG(销售额) Average of raw column AVG(销售数量) Define 均价: DIVIDE(SUM({出厂价金额}), NULLIF(SUM({销售数量}),0)) in model.dml or inline, then AGG(均价) Scalar MIN/MAX of dimension SELECT MIN(dt_month) FROM MODEL Use detail dataset: SELECT MIN(dt_month) FROM MODEL.&lt;dataset&gt; Filter groups by row count HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 100 Define 行数: COUNT({ID}) in model.dml or inline, then HAVING AGG(行数) &gt; 100 The only exception is scalar aggregation on detail datasets ( FROM MODEL.&lt;dataset&gt; ) for metadata exploration — not for business-metric dashboards. Inline measures ( /** dml ... **/ ) Define temporary measures directly in Model Query SQL without editing .dml files. The compiler loads the .dml named by FROM ( FROM MODEL → model.dml ; FROM acme → acme.dml ), merges inline definitions, then compiles the query. /** dml temp_sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount}) **/ SELECT product, AGG(temp_sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY product After the exploratory calculation has a shared business meaning, move the same formula into the .dml model under a stable name. Consumers can keep referring to the named measure without repeating its formula. A composite margin can be defined inline or in the model as DIVIDE({net_revenue} - {sales_cost}, {net_revenue}) ; applying ytd to that measure recalculates its components over the cumulative scope. It does not average the displayed monthly ratios. Use cases : Ad-hoc aggregation without modifying .dml (solves the measure requirement ) Multi-level aggregation with +d and dimensions — define intermediate measures inline RFM analysis — define customer-grain measures inline, query with rank fn-macros Rules : Must use multi-line YAML format (one property per line) Inline measures can reference existing .dml measures and datasets Inline measures can reference each other (defined earlier in the block) Same syntax as .dml measure definitions ( type , formula , dataset , dimensions ) Filters vs measure semantics Put in Model Query SQL WHERE Put in .dml measures Channel, region, data-quality flags shared with query grain Cohort anchors, entity eligibility Time window at query grain ( dt_period , dt_month ) Cross-period customer flags at customer grain JDBC parameters and query kind The checked JDBC distribution binds positional ? parameters in physical SQL. SELECT value FROM t WHERE id = ? and SELECT SUM(value) * ? FROM t , with 2 bound, returned 20 and 60 in the fixture. Equivalent semantic queries SELECT AGG(sales) FROM MODEL WHERE id = ? and SELECT AGG(sales) * ? FROM MODEL failed during parsing; their literal controls with 2 returned 20 and 60. Physical SQL parameter binding does not establish semantic Model Query parameter support. Internal literal parameterization for compilation-cache reuse is also distinct from a caller-visible binding API or result caching. The literal controls demonstrate expression behavior, not a recommendation to concatenate untrusted values into SQL. For an application that needs caller-bound semantic queries, this is an integration constraint; physical SQL binding does not meet that requirement. A rejected guessed CLI flag alone would not establish this boundary. The replay source is in the task-example project . A time filter also scopes sibling measures A query-level month filter can supply a YTD measure&#39;s time anchor, but it also scopes ordinary measures in the query. In the single-KPI example , February YTD margin uses January and February while ordinary revenue is February-only. An unanchored query fails, and grouped calendar queries can produce months with a cumulative value but no current-period revenue. These are separate output-membership and metric-scope questions; do not silently choose today&#39;s date or the latest fact date."},{"url":"reference--model-structure.html","title":"Model structure","text":"Model structure This page owns model-level fields, datasets, column macros, and common metadata. Model composition owns include visibility and name resolution. Model-level fields Field Type Description includes string[] Paths to other .dml files to merge (symbols are unioned) dialect string Target SQL dialect; set explicitly. See SQL dialect for recognized names, the default and execution scope. column-macros object Shared column definitions across multiple datasets &lt;name&gt; object | string A dataset, measure, dt-macro, fn-macro, materialize, or query Object name pattern : any string except reserved keywords ( includes , column-macros , datasets , measures , columns , dimensions[dt] , dt-macros , fn-macros , materializes , queries , dialects , and singular forms). Simplified measure definition : a top-level mapping value that is a bare string is treated as a measure formula shorthand: sales: SUM({amount}) # equivalent to: sales: type: measure formula: SUM({amount}) SQL dialect The checked compiler recognizes adb_mysql , duckdb , mssql , mysql , postgres and sqlite . The default when dialect: is omitted is mssql . A configured DuckDB connection does not change the model&#39;s target dialect. Declare dialect: duckdb when targeting DuckDB. BigQuery, Snowflake, Databricks and ClickHouse are not recognized targets in that check. A requirement for an unrecognized engine must be resolved before using dmlc for that deployment. Dialect recognition is separate from execution coverage. The runnable examples mainly verify DuckDB results; the overview records SQL Server compilation separately. Those checks do not establish feature parity across all six engines. Validate generated SQL and compare results on the intended database. Generated SQL can require multiple statements, intermediate relations and calendar objects. Dataset definition ( type: dataset ) &lt;name&gt;: type: dataset table: &lt;table_name&gt; # required (or sql:) # sql: &lt;SELECT ...&gt; # alternative to table: — derived dataset dimension: [col1, col2] # dataset grain dimensions materialize: &lt;name&gt; # optional, declare a materialize table for this dataset # or: # materialize: # name: &lt;name&gt; # parameters: # &lt;param&gt;: &lt;value&gt; columns: # required col1: # direct column (pass-through) col2: expr # calculated column (inline expression) col3: # full column definition abbr: &lt;short&gt; # abbreviation for display description: &lt;text&gt; formula: expr # calculated column (block form) # source: expr # alternative to formula: data-source verification formula prerequisite: &lt;expr&gt; # column-level grain constraint dimensions[dt]: # optional explicit time metadata; coarser standard dt_* fields may be system-derived dt_date: date dt_month: month measures: # atomic measures (inherit enclosing dataset; no dataset:) &lt;measure_name&gt;: &lt;formula or full definition&gt; abbr: &lt;short&gt; description: &lt;text&gt; Constraints : Exactly one of table: or sql: (mutually exclusive). table: is exclusive. table: policy means dataset Policy owns that physical table. Other datasets must not FROM / JOIN the physical name in sql: . Write from {Policy} / join {Policy} (the compiler substitutes the dataset query name), or look up attributes with pk(...).{Policy}.Col and omit that join. To share a table without ownership, use sql: select * from &lt;table&gt; instead of table: . Fact-table pattern: dimension datasets ( Claim , Policy ) use table: ; a fact that only needs those attributes can stay on an unowned source table and look up via pk() , instead of joining the owned table in sql: . Visibility: same-file forward dataset/measure/column references are supported in the checked build. Included files must resolve through their own definitions/includes, not through their parent. See model composition . For direct file compilation, name the query after the file stem ( report.dml → report ). allows: selects eligible materializations ( ALL , NONE , or explicit names); it does not override measure dimensions. columns: is required for the illustrated measure-query form. dimensions[dt] values are enums: year , quarter , month , week , date , hour . materialize: accepts either a scalar name or an object with required name: and optional parameters: . Both forms define the same dataset-scoped materialize identity. Dataset SQL can prepare supported joins, unions and subqueries before measure evaluation. UNION ALL retains row multiplicity: a key that identifies each input separately may not identify the combined rows. Define identity for the combined relation rather than assuming a branch&#39;s original key remains unique. The checked examples use column-only source datasets through {DATASET} references. Their ability to execute does not establish every validation path: in the checked derived-query fixtures, validate-only fails with VALIDATION_QUERY_FAILED because its uniqueness probe refers to an unavailable intermediate source relation. This is a validation execution failure, not evidence of a duplicate key or of uniqueness. Keep that failure visible; independently check the derived relation&#39;s complete key and compare query results on the intended connection before trusting the model. This additional check does not make the failed compiler validation pass. Column definition shapes : Shape YAML Meaning Direct amount: (null) Pass-through to underlying table column Calculated (inline) net: amount - discount (string) Derived column via expression Calculated (block) net: { formula: amount - discount } Derived column with metadata Verified net: { source: amount - discount } Declares a data-source verification formula; compilation alone does not establish equality of source values Brace binding in formulas : Form in a formula: Binds as Example Bare amount Physical (native) table column — compiler passes the name through to SQL net: amount - discount when both are Direct columns {calc_amt} Calculated column (or measure / column-macro) — DMX expands the definition doubled: &#39;{calc_amt} * 2&#39; Bare names on calculated columns mean &quot;treat this identifier as a native SQL column,&quot; which can appear to work via SELECT-alias order but does not expand the calculated definition. Brace when the formula should reuse that definition. Dependency-first ordering is a readability convention. A forward pk() target in the same file compiled successfully in the checked release; unresolved names can instead indicate a missing include, misspelling, or include visibility violation. See model composition . Column-macros Column-macros define columns shared across multiple datasets: column-macros: datasets: [tx, orders] # scoped datasets columns: # shared column definitions region: &#39;{stores}.region&#39; # cross-dataset lookup dimensions[dt]: dt_month: month # OR per-dataset scope: &lt;dataset_name&gt;: &lt;col&gt;: &lt;formula&gt; Common metadata fields Metadata support is object-specific; consult the matching schema definition: Field Type Description abbr string SQL-safe short name for internal use only — temp tables, joins, and materialize output. Never appears in final query column aliases (those use the business name key or an explicit AS alias) description string Human-readable description tags string[] Free-form tags prerequisite string (formula) Grain validity constraint (measures, columns, dt-macros, fn-macros) Column metadata is not a SQL type declaration Column properties must conform to the DML schema. Nest properties under the intended column: a sibling type: text inside columns: instead defines a different column named type , so accepting that model says nothing about the intended column&#39;s type. In the checked build, both type: text and type: banana under a column are rejected as an unsupported property. This is metadata-schema validation, not evidence of complete static expression type checking. Define physical column types in the database and use supported expressions or casts when a conversion is required. Top-level type: dataset and type: measure identify DML object kinds; they do not declare SQL value types."},{"url":"reference--modifiers.html","title":"Measure modifiers","text":"Measure modifiers This page owns modifier payloads, filter effects, and composition. Expressions and functions owns references and intrinsic functions. Fine-grain aggregation examples shows complete stages. Payload structure In {measure: {modifier_a: value, modifier_b: value}} , the outer object has exactly one target-measure key. Its nested modifier map can contain several keys, subject to modifier constraints . Key or form Payload dim_dt Formula string or object with value and range fields dim_dt as date , dim_dt as week , dim_dt as month , dim_dt as quarter , dim_dt as year , dim_dt as hour Period conversion variant; at most one time variant per modifier object +f Filter-name string, list of filter names, or inline object such as {column: value} -f Filter-name or column-name string, or a list of those names Bare modifier name A dt-macro or fn-macro name Time modifiers The system macro reference copy contains time-shifting dt-macro definitions and formula-wrapping fn-macro definitions. Growth-rate macros return decimal rates. The selected executable&#39;s packaged resources govern available macros, formulas, and prerequisite constraints. Two macro types: Type Returns Examples dt-macro Absolute value pp , ya , ytd , mat , p3m , ... fn-macro (growth) Decimal rate pop , yoy , mom Stacking : Modifiers can be stacked: AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;, &#39;ya&#39;) = year-to-date one year ago. See modifier stacking for full stacking semantics. Time context: time modifiers require a resolvable dim_dt and must satisfy their macro prerequisites. Grouping by a time field is the clearest form; a time filter can also supply the context. See Model Query time rules . Period conversion with dim_dt : SELECT dt_year, dt_quarter, AGG({sales: {dim_dt as quarter: pp}}) as last_quarter FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_year, dt_quarter dim_dt as quarter makes pp operate at quarter grain even if the query groups by year + quarter. Function modifiers The system macro reference copy defines macros such as rank , pot , grand , firstday , and lastday . The selected executable&#39;s packaged resources determine the available names, formulas, and prerequisites. An unknown modifier diagnostic lists available names on the checked build. Filter modifier +f is not a system macro — it is a schema-level modifier: Modifier Syntax Returns Description +f: condition {measure: {+f: {col: val}}} Filtered value Filter the measure while aggregating +f filters atomic input rows; it is not a post-aggregation predicate. : Target / predicate Behavior Column inside an atomic aggregate Filters the input to that aggregate Atomic measure + source-column predicate Supported; e.g. {sales: {+f: &quot;status = 1&quot;}} emits a conditional SUM Simple composite + source-column predicate Propagates to its atomic dependencies; verified for divide({sales}, {cnt}) . Use only when every dependency should receive the filter Atomic measure with +d and source-column +f Supported in the checked AVG rollup; inspect that filtering precedes the fine-grain aggregation Predicate referencing an aggregate measure, e.g. &quot;{cnt} &gt; 3&quot; Rejected with cannot use +f modifier with measure(s) ; use fine-grain filter examples A bare filtered atomic reference evaluates at the current query grain. Preserve +d and an explicit outer aggregate when the question concerns per-entity values (especially AVG, MIN, MAX, COUNT). Conditional SUM with no accepted input returns NULL; an explicit ELSE 0 is a different contract. Input gating can retain groups containing only rejected rows. Window function fn-macros generate rank() over (...) in compiled SQL. Use them in Model Query SQL as AGG(measure, &#39;rank&#39;) or AGG({measure: rank}) . See system_macros.dml for the full list and formulas. Filter modifier in .dml (column target): completed_sales: type: measure formula: &quot;{amount: {+f: {status: &#39;completed&#39;}}}&quot; Filter modifier in Model Query SQL: SELECT AGG(sales, &#39;+f&#39;, region = &#39;North&#39;) FROM MODEL Dimension modifiers Modifier Syntax Effect +d: dim {measure: {+d: dim}} Drill down: append dimension field dim to the target evaluation dimension and evaluate there. Does not automatically roll up to the query grain — that requires an explicit outer aggregate ( SUM / AVG /…). The target dimension comes from query GROUP BY plus the measure&#39;s dimensions: constraint (required fields must appear; ... allows additional query fields). Without a dimension modifier, a measure reference is already at its target evaluation dimension; wrapping it in SUM() fails with &quot;cannot aggregate a measure w/o +d modifier&quot;. +d is required for any SUM({measure}) , not just measures with dimensions: declarations. -d: dim {measure: {-d: dim}} Remove dimension field dim from the measure&#39;s evaluation dimension, producing a coarser grain. Use list form {-d: [dim1, dim2]} to remove multiple fields—repeating {-d: dim1, -d: dim2} causes YAML duplicate-key errors. If the measure declares closed allowed dimensions, one must permit the resulting evaluation/query dimension; omitting measure dimensions: permits any compatible dimension. Query filters: on the removed field are still pushed down to the dataset WHERE —this can corrupt ratio denominators (see dimension and filter removal below). -df: dim {measure: {-df: dim}} Combined -d + -f : removes a dimension field from the evaluation dimension ( -d ) and removes filters on that field from the measure&#39;s evaluation ( -f ). Query filters: that reference the removed field (or columns that depend on it including via pk() chains) are not pushed to the coarser-grain query&#39;s WHERE . Use list form {-df: [dim1, dim2]} . Equivalent to DAX CALCULATE(expr, REMOVEFILTERS(dim)) . -f: name {measure: {-f: name}} Remove filters referencing name from measure evaluation only . name can be a filter name (defined in query filters: ) or a column name (including via pk() chain dependencies). Use list form {-f: [name1, name2]} . Does not change grouping. Query filters still define the report row set — when a blocked filter is not an output grain column, constrain population (e.g. EXISTS ) rather than dropping the filter silently. dimension: [dims] {measure: {dimension: [dim1, dim2]}} Replace the entire evaluation dimension with the listed dimension fields. It shares the dimension: token with dataset/query/materialize properties, but here it modifies one measure reference rather than declaring dataset identity, query grouping, or a materialization key. Semantically close to -d , but generates different SQL: a separate aggregation CTE instead of a window OVER (PARTITION BY …) —see pitfalls P43 . Across time grains (e.g. {m:{dimension:[dt_quarter]}} on a dt_month query), bridge via the calendar and broadcast the coarse total onto fine rows — not a grand-total scalar. Quick glossary (avoid ambiguity) Writing Meaning {销量: {+d: 门店}} Evaluate at query grain ∪ {门店} ; stop there — no automatic roll-back SUM({销量: {+d: 门店}}) (or AVG / MAX /…) Fine-grain values, then outer aggregate rolls back to the current query grain {销量: {-d: 门店}} Drop 门店 from evaluation grain; filters on 门店 may still push down {销量: {-df: 门店}} Drop 门店 from grain and block 门店-related filters on that measure {销量: {-f: 门店}} Block 门店-related filters only; grouping unchanged Same-dimension modifier conflicts The language contract prohibits intersecting field sets in +d and either -d or -df within one modifier object. Different fields can coexist, as in SUM({销量: {+d: 门店, -d: 品牌}}) . The current checked build accepts the checked same-field conflicts and emits repeated regional totals followed by an outer sum. This is an implementation gap. See modifier composition . -df — combined -d and -f -df combines the semantics of two modifiers: -d (don&#39;t group by): removes a dimension field from the measure&#39;s evaluation dimension, producing a coarser-grain aggregation. -f (remove filter): removes filters on that dimension from the measure&#39;s evaluation — query filters: that reference the dimension (or columns that depend on it via pk() chains) are not pushed to the coarser-grain query&#39;s WHERE . -df is shorthand for {-d: dim, -f: dim} — both forms produce identical compiled SQL. Use -df for brevity. Use -df in ratio measures where the denominator must ignore both the grouping and the filtering of a dimension. Problem it solves : Without -df , query filters: are pushed to dataset WHERE before aggregation. When -d generates a coarser-grain temp table (ratio denominator), it computes from the filtered base — shrinking the denominator. -df blocks this pushdown for the specified dimension: the filter is applied only to temp tables whose grain includes that dimension, and omitted from temp tables where the dimension was removed. When to use -df vs -d : Scenario Modifier Why Ratio denominator must include all values of a dimension (e.g. brand share = brand sales / total sales across all brands) -df Filter on brand_id would shrink the denominator if pushed down Ratio denominator should reflect the filtered subset (e.g. share within selected brands only) -d Filter on brand_id should apply to both numerator and denominator No filter on the removed dimension field -d (or -df , equivalent) No filter to block Example: brand share within each store , retaining selected brands in the numerator but including all brands in the denominator: brand_share: type: measure formula: &#39;divide({sales} * 100.0, {sales: {-df: brand_id}})&#39; At query grain [store_id, brand_id] , a query filter on brand_id applies to the numerator. The denominator removes that field and its filter, retaining the store context. Dependency tracing : The compiler traces pk() chain dependencies. A filter on 客户标准名称 ( pk({store_id}).MDM_STORE(store_id).store_name ) is blocked when store_id is -df &#39;d, because the column depends on store_id . Equivalence with other systems : System Equivalent Syntax DAX (Power BI) CALCULATE(expr, REMOVEFILTERS(dim)) Removes grouping + filter context in one operation Tableau No direct equivalent Use FIXED LOD + Context Filter, or Table Calculation Filter Malloy No direct equivalent Use stage-level where: (post-aggregation) instead of source where: LookML No direct equivalent Use filtered measure ( filters: on measure) -- Product share within category SELECT category, product, AGG(sales) as product_sales, AGG({sales: {dimension: [category]}}) as category_total FROM MODEL GROUP BY category, product -- Remove time grouping while retaining the selected time filter SELECT region, AGG({sales: {-d: dim_dt}}) as selected_period_sales FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY region {-d:} on composite measures with FULL OUTER JOIN Independent measure datasets normally compose through FULL OUTER JOIN at aligned evaluation grains ( key-union ). Alignment ON uses plain = (not null-safe). Row-preserving LEFT / nullpk stay null-safe. Population, equality, uniqueness, and fill are separate contracts; see cross-dataset composition and join NULL equality . For primary-side population, use an unfilled presence measure guaranteed non-null for each real primary group and NULL for absent groups. For INNER population, require presence on both sides. An arbitrary business measure can be NULL on a valid group, and a filled measure can be non-null on an absent group. Neither is a reliable existence test. NULL arithmetic does not by itself remove secondary-only output rows. Combining modifiers Modifiers can be combined in a single reference, subject to same-dimension conflicts and modifier constraints : # Filter + dimension (outer SUM rolls +d back to query grain) formula: &#39;SUM({sales: {+d: customer, +f: {region: &quot;North&quot;}}})&#39; # Legal: +d and -d on DIFFERENT fields formula: &#39;SUM({sales: {+d: customer, -d: brand}})&#39; # Prohibited by the language contract, but accepted by the checked build: # formula: &#39;SUM({sales: {+d: store_id, -d: store_id}})&#39; # formula: &#39;{sales: {+d: store_id, -df: store_id}}&#39; # Time + dimension formula: &#39;{sales: {+d: customer}, yoy}&#39; # Dimension + rank formula: &#39;{sales: {-d: dim_dt, rank}}&#39; # Dimension + filter removal (ratio with deferred filters) formula: &#39;divide({sales: {-df: brand_id}} * 100.0, {sales: {-df: [store_id, brand_id]}})&#39; # Filter + absolute grain (e.g., ratio numerator/denominator at different grains) formula: &#39;DIVIDE({sales: {+f: &quot;status = 1&quot;, dimension: [prod_id]}}, {sales: {+f: &quot;status = 1&quot;, dimension: [prod_id, store_id]}})&#39; In Model Query SQL: SELECT product, AGG({sales: {-d: dt_date, rank}}) as all_time_rank FROM MODEL WHERE dt_month = date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) GROUP BY product Modifier stacking Multiple macros can be stacked in one expression. The compiler creates intermediate measures automatically, mirroring what a user would write manually. Syntax : {measure: {macro1, macro2, macro3}} or AGG(measure, &#39;macro1&#39;, &#39;macro2&#39;, &#39;macro3&#39;) For an ordinary sequential stack, the first macro transforms the original measure and the next macro applies to that intermediate. Time modifiers combined with multi-branch functions also transform their branches, as described below. A sequential example is: # Stacking: yoy creates intermediate &quot;sales_yoy&quot;, then rank applies to it # Equivalent to: # sales_yoy: # type: measure # formula: &#39;{sales: yoy}&#39; # ...then: {sales_yoy: rank} q_col: &#39;{sales: {yoy, rank}}&#39; Time modifiers and multi-path functions: the verified build pushes ytd into the current and comparison branches of yoy . Both {sales: {yoy, ytd}} and {sales: {ytd, yoy}} emitted identical SQL for the recorded monthly probe: YTD sales divided by prior-year YTD sales, minus one. This is not a sum of monthly growth rates. Do not sort modifiers by category or infer a different meaning solely from generated intermediate names. Grain-changing macros such as firstday have additional multi-path constraints. Compile and inspect the exact combination before relying on it. Quote authored +f expressions containing YAML indicators, for example {sales: {+f: &quot;amount &gt; 5&quot;, yoy}} . Intermediate measure reuse. Before creating a new intermediate, the compiler searches existing measures for one with the same formula (by expanded formula, shorthand, or map form). If a user-defined or previously generated measure matches, it is reused. This avoids redundant intermediates and lets users name their intermediates deliberately. Name collision handling. If the auto-generated intermediate name (e.g. sales_yoy ) collides with an existing measure that has a different formula, the compiler appends a numeric suffix: sales_yoy__2 , sales_yoy__3 , etc. Compatibility: macro prerequisites, time context, allowed dimensions, duplicate keys, and reaggregation determine whether a stack is valid. Compile the exact stack on the target build. Two range macros and repeated macro keys are not general composition mechanisms. If an error remains after expressing the stages explicitly, keep the diagnostic and reproducer; a manually named intermediate does not establish equivalence by itself. Time modifiers on composite ratios A time modifier can apply directly to a named composite measure. For example, define gross_margin_ytd with formula: &#39;{gross_margin: ytd}&#39; , where gross_margin divides gross profit by revenue. The cumulative context recalculates the dependencies and then the ratio; it does not add monthly percentages. See the complete profitability example . This verified ratio example is not a promise that arbitrary non-additive formulas can be rolled up without checking their semantics. Fixed-axis totals and output membership The fixed-total example (中文) uses an ordinary month field and a query grouped by region and month. With January selected, -d: month retains January in the denominator while -df: month includes both fixture months. Both queries still output only January rows. This fixture does not establish dynamic pivot axes, subtotal-level predicates or arbitrary dim_dt removal behavior."},{"url":"reference--query-yaml.html","title":"YAML query declarations","text":"YAML query declarations This page owns type: query , columns, filters, materialization selection, and after: . The query consumes the same model measures as Model Query SQL . Filename constraint: When compiling a .dml file with dmlc -c , the type: query block name must match the filename (without .dml ). For example, a file named my_report.dml must contain my_report: type: query , not some_other_name: type: query . The compiler rejects mismatches with: query name should be &lt;filename&gt; (the same as filename) instead of &lt;block_name&gt; . Basic structure my_report: type: query allows: ALL # optional: consider compatible materializations columns: dimension: - dt_month - product sales: # measure reference (no AGG needed) sales_yoy: formula: &#39;{sales: yoy}&#39; columns: is required. Its non-dimension keys name output columns; their values are formulas or direct measure references. An optional dataset: selects the dataset-query form and cannot be combined with columns.dimension . Query filters have these forms. Each key names a filter that measure-reference modifiers can address. Value form Meaning &lt;column&gt;: &lt;value&gt; Equality on the column with the same name &lt;column&gt;: [a, b] Membership in the listed values &lt;name&gt;: {column: &lt;column&gt;, value: &lt;value&gt;} Named equality filter on an explicit column &lt;name&gt;: {column: &lt;column&gt;, range: [lo, hi]} Named inclusive range on an explicit column &lt;name&gt;: {visibility: hidden, default: on} Hidden filter with its default enabled The filter placement rules define which stage receives each predicate. Detail vs aggregation Mode How to specify Constraints Aggregation No dataset: key Measures allowed; compiler aggregates Detail dataset: DATASET_NAME No measures; raw columns only # Detail query product_list: type: query dataset: DATASET_PRODUCTS columns: dimension: - product Auto-join of multi-dataset measures When an aggregate query references measures from different datasets , they compose through FULL OUTER JOIN semantics on the shared dimension fields: rows that exist in only one dataset still appear, with NULL for the other dataset&#39;s measures. The emitted join type follows the proven population contract. The compiler starts from FULL OUTER JOIN and reduces each join to the strongest type proven by query filters: &quot;{primary_measure} is not null&quot;: in filters: both prunes secondary-only rows and lowers the emitted join to LEFT JOIN ; presence guards on both sides yield INNER JOIN . The guard matches a golden SQL LEFT JOIN only when valid primary groups cannot have a NULL primary measure. Prove that NULL/empty-group contract and inspect generated join types and output; otherwise the filter also deletes valid groups. Keep the guard out of after: so it remains visible to lineage, review, and reuse. The formula-as-key shape is required: &quot;{expr}&quot;: with an empty value. name: {condition: …} is schema-invalid. Column references in DML format Column type Syntax Example Dimension field from same dataset bare name product: Dimension field from another dataset &quot;{DATASET}.column&quot; prod_id: &quot;{DATASET_PRODUCTS}.prod_id&quot; Measure bare name sales: Measure with modifier formula: sales_yoy: {sales: yoy} Pitfall : A bare column name in an aggregate query without dataset: will fail if the column lives on a specific dataset. Use &quot;{DATASET}.column&quot; form. Auto-aliasing and direct reference A query column with no formula can resolve to a measure or dataset column by name match — this is auto-aliasing . Three levels exist: Level Mechanism Example Result column (direct reference) Dataset columns: entry with no formula; DML name = table column name prod_amount: SUM(prod_amount) — name passed through as-is, no aliasing Query column → measure (auto-aliasing) Query column with no formula; column name = measure name revenue: (measure revenue exists) Compiler substitutes the measure&#39;s formula: expression Query column → dataset column (auto-aliasing) Query column with no formula; column name = dataset column name, derivable from the query dimension prod_name: (dataset column via pk({prod_id}) chain) Compiler resolves from dataset definition Key behaviors: dmlc -c does not validate dataset column existence against the table schema — a mismatched name compiles but produces runtime SQL errors. Use validateOnly (requires database connection) to catch mismatched names. When a query column name matches neither a measure nor a derivable dataset column, it errors as not defined, add formula? . When the query column name differs from the measure name, add an explicit formula ( display_name: &#39;{measure_name}&#39; ) or rename the measure with abbr: . A dataset column that is not derivable from the query dimension (e.g., a raw fact column with no dimension-field dependency) errors with cannot be derived from dimension [store_id] . A pass-through column (no formula: ) may carry metadata properties ( description: , visibility: ) in object form without requiring formula: . Example: revenue: { description: &#39;Total revenue&#39; } auto-aliases to measure revenue while attaching the description. visibility: hidden Hides a column from the final query output while keeping it available in intermediate temp tables. Aspect Behavior Final SELECT Hidden columns are normally absent, but a hidden column referenced by a filter/HAVING may be carried into final output; inspect generated SQL before claiming exact output Intermediate temp tables Hidden columns remain available for JOIN conditions and filters: filters: referencing hidden dimension fields Compiles, but can strengthen optional joins and can force helper materialization; verify generated SQL after: SQL referencing hidden dimension fields by name Breaks — query columns refer to nonexistence source column PK chain Intact — no PK warning fires from visibility: hidden alone Guidance: Remove visibility: hidden if after: references the dimension field by name. Keep it if after: only uses measure columns. For contracted outputs, compile and confirm that no hidden helper reaches the final result; extra columns are acceptable only when the output contract explicitly allows them. YAML forms that work: - dim: {visibility: hidden} or a nested map whose keys sit under the dim entry. Sibling visibility: at the same indent as the dim key (under-indent) is a schema lint error — write the inline or nested form instead. Always inspect compiled SELECT when using dmlc -c alone (schema lint runs in the editor / dmlc --lint ). Query dimension format columns: dimension: - dt_month - product # Correct: list dimension field names only Wrong : Don&#39;t put cross-dataset column mappings among the query dimension fields. Define those formulas as separate query columns: # Wrong columns: dimension: - f_tag_id: {DATASET_PT}.id # Correct columns: dimension: - f_tag_id f_tag_id: &quot;{DATASET_PT}.id&quot; Exception — measure-as-dimension: a query-dimension entry may bind a measure (usually with +d ) so the query grain groups by that measure value: columns: dimension: - first_purchase_month: &#39;{first_purchase_month: {+d: store_id}}&#39; sales: Declare the classification entity key explicitly with +d (preferred) or dimension: . Multiple independent fields are allowed when their complete keys match; ordinary output measures must safely roll up from Q ∪ K . Label-channel filters (formula filters: / Model Query HAVING ) apply after the label exists — see Measure-as-dimension . Do not use this exception for physical/ {DATASET}.col mappings. Filter placement hierarchy Filter placement is a semantic decision, not a syntax preference: Layer Where What goes here Query filters: Query block Report-specific slices (channel, brand, date range) Intermediate column / measure .dml dataset or top-level measure Metric-specific eligibility when rejected rows may remain in groups with NULL measures Dataset sql: Dataset definition Permanent source-population invariants or constructs DML cannot express declaratively Moving a source WHERE condition into a measure gate changes row existence unless rejected-only groups are impossible or contractually equivalent to present groups with NULL measures. Prove this explicitly; when it is false or unknown, keep a row-removing representation. Structured range semantics: range: [start, end] in query filters: includes both endpoints at the declared dt_&lt;grain&gt; . For 2025 daily data use [&#39;2025-01-01&#39;, &#39;2025-12-31&#39;] ; for monthly data use [&#39;2025-01-01&#39;, &#39;2025-12-01&#39;] . A dialect may implement this with an exclusive physical bound such as &lt; dateadd(day, 1, end) , but the DML input end remains the last included value. List vs range — a common footgun: A bare list value dim: [&#39;a&#39;, &#39;b&#39;] compiles to WHERE dim IN (&#39;a&#39;, &#39;b&#39;) (exact match of those two values), not an inclusive range. For date dimensions this is a silent data-loss trap: dt_date: [&#39;2024-01-01&#39;, &#39;2025-12-31&#39;] returns only rows on those two days, not the full 2-year span. Always use range: [start, end] when you mean an inclusive interval: # Wrong — only 2 days of data filters: dt_date: [&#39;2024-01-01&#39;, &#39;2025-12-31&#39;] # Right — full inclusive range filters: dt_date: range: [&#39;2024-01-01&#39;, &#39;2025-12-31&#39;] Common filter pitfalls : Symptom Fix [dataset …] [filters]: not supported Move to query filters: , measure formula, or sql: WHERE referred column {DATASET}.col is undefined in query filter Use string form: &quot;{DATASET}.col = value&quot;: Two-element list on a date dimension returns only 2 days Use range: [start, end] instead of bare list — dim: [a, b] compiles to IN (a, b) , not BETWEEN Complex EXISTS / PK chain in query filters: Use measure-input gating only for metric-specific eligibility. If the condition selects source rows, keep it at the source-row stage; moving it into a measure can change the report population. Inspect the emitted predicates and joins. pk chaining # Single PK lookup formula: &#39;pk(customer_id).{DATASET_CUSTOMERS}.tier&#39; # Composite PK — all columns required formula: &#39;pk(brand_id, tag_id).{DATASET_PRODUCTS}.state&#39; Arity rule : the number of keys inside pk(...) must match the number of dimension fields in the target dataset&#39;s dimension: . A mismatch produces expect N expr(s) to query dataset X using pk, but got M (see Troubleshooting index P65). Nested chains : a column derived from one pk() chain may serve as the key in another pk() chain. The compiler traces the derive chain and emits the corresponding nested LEFT JOIN s. This is the native way to model &quot;lookup an attribute, then use that attribute to look up another attribute&quot; without sql: or after: . # fact dataset columns: store_id: # First hop: store_id → Dim Customer → hq_id hq_id: pk({store_id}).{Dim Customer}.hq_id # Second hop: hq_id (itself a pk chain result) → Dim Customer → store_code hq_code: pk({hq_id}).{Dim Customer}.store_code # Third hop: hq_id → Dim Customer → org_id, then org_id → Dim Org → emp_name hq_org_id: pk({hq_id}).{Dim Customer}.org_id hq_emp_name: pk({hq_org_id}).{Dim Org}.emp_name Same-file forward references are supported in the checked build, including column dependencies and pk() targets. Keep dependency-first order for readability. Includes have a separate visibility boundary; see model composition . after: clause after: is optional. Without it, the compiler orders ascending by the compiler-resolved visible output columns that represent the query dimension fields, in resolved dimension-field order. A hidden dimension field may therefore be ordered through a visible query-column alias rather than its authored field name. With no effective output dimension, no row order is promised. An omitted NULL-order modifier uses the target dialect&#39;s default. Any after: block takes ownership of the final result order. In particular, select * from {QUERY} without its own ORDER BY removes the compiler&#39;s default order. Use after: only when a business-significant custom direction, NULL ordering, tie-break, measure/expression order, or TOP/LIMIT differs from the default. Don&#39;t re-aggregate: my_report: type: query columns: dimension: [product] sales: after: &#39;select * from {QUERY} ORDER BY sales DESC LIMIT 10&#39; When after: is present, {QUERY} is mandatory: it names the compiled query result consumed by the post-query statement. “Ordering only” means no business logic beyond this wrapper plus ORDER BY/TOP/LIMIT. The upstream compiler emits a redundant ORDER BY warning when an authored order matches the query&#39;s default output order. Follow its contextual hint: remove only the redundant ORDER BY clause when other important after: processing remains; remove the entire block only when nothing else remains. Wrong : Re-aggregating with group by in after: on a standard measure query. Let the query block aggregate measures. allows: ALL To permit the compiler to consider all compatible materializations: my_report: type: query allows: ALL columns: dimension: - dt_date sales: allows: controls eligible materializations. ALL permits candidates; NONE disables their use; explicit names constrain candidates. Eligibility still depends on grain and filter compatibility. It does not relax measure dimensions: or grant arbitrary query grain. See materialization declarations . YAML syntax pitfalls Quote formulas containing : , { , or - : formula: &#39;SUMIF(...)&#39; Dimension field under measures: : Wrong — declare under dimension: only dataset: on composite measures : Wrong — omit dataset: when formula references other measures Mismatched dimension field names across datasets : Align to one name (e.g. tag_id ) and map in columns:"},{"url":"reference--README.html","title":"DML and DMX language reference","text":"DML and DMX language reference Use the documentation index for concepts and usage guides, or the example catalog for worked patterns. Language definitions Topic Reference Evaluation context, grain compatibility and composition Evaluation semantics Datasets, columns and metadata Model structure Atomic and composite measures, windows and fill Measure declarations References, compound/effective-date lookups, functions and runtime variables DMX expressions Time, dimension and filter modifiers Measure modifiers FROM , AGG , filters and grouping Model Query SQL type: query , columns and filters YAML query declarations Stored grouping keys, attributes and measures Materialization declarations Includes, forward references and names Model composition Join equality, NULL keys and result membership Join NULL equality Diagnostics and built-in definitions Error codes — meanings and suggested fixes. Troubleshooting — symptoms and stable pitfall identifiers. System macros — built-in macro definitions used by the examples. For command-line flags, read dmlc --help and dmlc --advanced-help from your installed executable."},{"url":"reference--semantics.html","title":"DML evaluation semantics","text":"DML evaluation semantics This page owns evaluation context, grain compatibility, and composition contracts. Declaration syntax lives in model structure , measures , queries , and materialization . Scope and position What this spec covers This document specifies DML evaluation context, grain compatibility, and modifier constraints. Rules below describe language contracts. See Error codes for diagnostic meanings and suggested fixes. What this spec does not cover Compile and validate a query covers CLI commands and evidence collection. Build an analysis covers business analysis procedures. Core concepts explains the model, query, and expression layers. Three-layer architecture Layer Language What you write File Modeling DML Datasets, measures, column-macros, materialize, dt/fn-macros .dml (YAML) Querying Model Query (SQL or DML format) AGG(measure) , GROUP BY , modifiers .sql or .dml type: query Expressions DMX (Dato Measure eXpression) Formulas, references, modifiers, pk() Inside both layers DMX is the shared expression language. In .dml formulas: {column} , {measure: modifier} , pk(...) . In Model Query SQL: AGG(measure) , AGG({measure: modifier}) . Core building blocks Dataset — a table with a declared dimension and columns. Its dataset declared grain is determined by the declared dimension: list. Measure — a named business metric. Two types: Atomic : bound to a dataset ( dataset: field), uses SQL aggregation on columns. Composite : references other measures (no dataset: field). Dimension field — one named DML field. Dimension — a list containing one or more dimension fields. A dataset dimension: declares source-row identity; a query dimension declares grouping; an evaluation dimension controls a measure reference; and a materialization dimension declares an output key. Each inner list in measure dimensions: is one allowed dimension. A measure-as-dimension field is a query-dimension field whose value is produced by evaluating a measure (see Measure-as-dimension ). The modifier form {dimension: []} clears the evaluation dimension to request a grand-total context. The empty list is an operation, not a zero-field dimension. Column-macro — a derived column or cross-dataset lookup defined in .dml . Brings fields from other datasets into measure formulas without JOIN . Design principles Semantics must be executable. Measures compile to warehouse SQL — not just documentation. Govern semantics like code. Git, PR, LSP, dml_schema.yaml validation. Measures are not bound to a fixed result grain. A measure has a derived, optionally constrained measure valid/native context and is evaluated at an effective grain chosen by the query and modifiers; the compiler enforces compatibility. Complex derivations belong in the model. Time comparisons, ratios, multi-level aggregation — kept in .dml , not scattered to BI calculated fields or ad-hoc SQL. Evaluation context model Definition The evaluation context is the complete set of information the compiler uses to resolve a measure reference into executable SQL. The canonical product shorthand is Context = {grain, filters, time} . In the language model below, the time role is represented by the resolved dim_dt field. Context = { grain: [dim₁, dim₂, ...], // active grouping dimension fields filters: [predicate₁, ...], // active filter predicates dim_dt: TimeDim | null // resolved time dimension } Canonical grain roles Role Source Description Dataset declared grain Dataset dimension: What one dataset row represents Measure valid/native context Dataset source, referenced measures, formula/modifiers, and optional measure dimensions: constraint Contexts in which the measure definition is valid; not a fixed output grain Effective evaluation grain Query grain after applying +d , -d , dimension , and related modifiers Grain at which a particular measure reference is evaluated Query result grain GROUP BY or query columns.dimension What one output row represents. May include measure-as-dimension fields ( Measure-as-dimension ) alongside physical or mapped dimension fields. The broader query context combines query result grain with WHERE / filters: and resolved time information. “Measure native grain” and “target grain” in older material are aliases for measure valid/native context and effective evaluation grain respectively; new normative text should use the canonical terms above. Context transition Modifiers transform the evaluation context. The compiler applies modifiers to derive a new context for measure evaluation: apply_modifier(Context, Modifier) -&gt; Context&#39; Modifier Effect on grain Effect on filters +d: dim Append dim to grain (drill down) No change -d: dim Remove dim from grain (roll up) No change — filters on dim still pushed to dataset WHERE -df: dim Remove dim from grain Remove filters on dim (and derived columns) from measure evaluation dimension: [dims] Replace entire grain with dims No change +f: condition No change Add condition to filters -f: dim No change Remove filters referencing dim dt-macro (e.g. ya , pp , ytd ) No change Replace time range in dim_dt fn-macro (e.g. yoy , rank ) May change grain (e.g. grand → dimension: [] ) Depends on macro formula dim_dt — the time dimension dim_dt is the system variable representing the time dimension of the current query . Resolution priority : query dimension → query filter → null. Properties : {dim_dt} — resolves to the query&#39;s current time field (e.g. dt_month , dt_quarter ) {dim_dt.datetype} — type string: &#39;day&#39; , &#39;week&#39; , &#39;month&#39; , &#39;quarter&#39; , &#39;year&#39; {dim_dt.periods_per_year} — periods per year: 52 for weeks, 12 for months, 4 for quarters {dim_dt.datepart} — SQL datepart string Design goal : use dim_dt instead of concrete dt_date / dt_month / dt_quarter in measure formulas, so a measure works at any time grain without rewriting. Period conversion : dim_dt as quarter makes pp operate at quarter grain even if the query groups by year + quarter: SELECT dt_year, dt_quarter, AGG({sales: {dim_dt as quarter: pp}}) as last_quarter FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_year, dt_quarter Standard time dimension fields Field Grain Example value dt_date Day 2024-03-15 dt_week Week (starting Monday) 2024-03-11 dt_month Month 2024-03-01 dt_quarter Quarter 2024-01-01 dt_year Year 2024-01-01 All time fields use first-day format : the first day of the period. Automatic coarser-grain derivation A dataset that explicitly declares a standard time field also exposes the supported coarser members of the dt_&lt;grain&gt; series through the system calendar. For example, an explicit dt_date column makes dt_week , dt_month , dt_quarter , and dt_year valid dataset-scoped column references; model authors do not need to repeat those names under columns: merely to query or filter at a coarser time grain. The generated values follow the calendar and first-day rules above. An auto-derived time field is a virtual column identity , not a second explicit source definition. Language tooling must therefore: include supported derived fields in dataset-scoped resolution and completion; accept them anywhere the corresponding dataset column is legal, including as query dimension fields, filters, formulas, and modifier payloads; navigate a derived reference to the explicit source time column that supplies it ( dt_month derived from dt_date navigates to dt_date ); keep the virtual field name read-only for rename purposes unless an explicit definition with that name exists. If the same derived field can be reached from more than one explicit time source and no unique source can be selected, tooling must fail closed rather than choose an arbitrary definition. Calendar dataset The system provides a Dim Calendar dataset for cross-grain time lookups: Dim Calendar: type: dataset table: DML_CALENDAR dimension: [dt_date] columns: dt_date: dt_week: dt_month: source: pk({dt_date}).DML_CALENDAR(dt_date).dt_month dt_quarter: source: pk({dt_month}).DML_CALENDAR(dt_date).dt_quarter dt_year: source: pk({dt_quarter}).DML_CALENDAR(dt_date).dt_year Filter context and pushdown Filter placement depends on semantic scope: Layer Where What goes here Query filters: / WHERE Query block Report-specific slices (channel, brand, date range) Measure formula .dml measure Metric-specific eligibility when rejected rows may remain in output groups Dataset sql: Dataset definition Permanent source-population restrictions that current declarative DML cannot express A source WHERE and a NULL-producing measure gate are not generally interchangeable: rejected-only groups disappear in the former and may remain in the latter. Prove group existence, sibling metrics, distinct/join/window behavior, and NULL semantics before moving between these layers. Pushdown rule : query filters: are pushed to the dataset WHERE before aggregation. When -d generates a coarser-grain temp table, the filter is still applied — this can shrink ratio denominators. Use -df to block filter pushdown for a specific dimension field. Dependency tracing : the compiler traces column derive chain. A filter on store_name ( pk({store_id}).MDM_STORE(store_id).store_name ) is blocked when store_id is -df &#39;d, because the column purily derived from store_id . Grain system The dimension in each evaluation context determines its grain and compatibility with other contexts. The DML roles of each dimension declaration are summarized in dimension roles . Three grain types Grain type Declared by Meaning Dataset grain Dataset dimension: One row of the underlying table represents one value combination of the dimension fields in this dimension Measure grain Formula + modifiers + optional dimensions: constraint The grain at which the measure&#39;s formula is valid Query grain Query GROUP BY / columns.dimension: One query-result row represents one value combination of the dimension fields in this dimension Grain compatibility rule The query grain must be compatible with every measure&#39;s grain. For an atomic measure without dimensions: constraint: The measure can be evaluated at any compatible dimension composed from its dataset&#39;s dimension fields. The compiler chooses aggregation from the declared grain and requested context; at the declared unique row grain it may project raw values. For an atomic measure with dimensions: [[dims]] constraint: The measure can only be evaluated at the specified grain. Other formulas wanting to use it must explicitly {measure: {+d: dim}} to drill to that grain. The compiler rejects incompatible grains with: cannot set dimension [...] since only [...] are allowed . Dataset/materialize dimension: contains one dimension, while measure dimensions: contains alternative allowed dimensions—each inner list is one dimension. The latter is a constraint, not a declaration of dataset identity or query grouping. See the dimension-role contract in dimension roles . For a composite measure: The grain is the union of referenced measures&#39; grains (after modifier resolution). All referenced measures must be grain-compatible with each other. Multi-level aggregation via +d +d establishes a finer evaluation stage . It does not by itself roll the value back to the query grain. Writing Meaning {m: {+d: x}} Evaluate at query grain ∪ {x} ; stop there SUM / AVG / MAX / … ({m: {+d: x}}) Fine-grain value, then an explicit outer aggregate rolls back to the current query grain Query dim binding - lab: &#39;{m: {+d: K}}&#39; Measure-as-dimension ( Measure-as-dimension ): the result is a grouping key, not an ordinary rolled-up measure column The outer aggregation function ( SUM , AVG , MAX , etc.) is chosen by the author . The compiler does not invent it. With an outer aggregate, the compiler typically emits a CTE ( WITH ... AS (SELECT ... GROUP BY ...) SELECT ... ). Pattern : atomic measure → +d drill-down inside a composite that supplies the outer aggregate → query at any grain # Atomic measure (dataset grain: dt_date × store_id × prod_id) 销售量: type: measure dataset: o2o_pos formula: SUM(count_qty) # Distribution SKU count: how many products have sales &gt; 0? # {销售量: {+d: prod_id}} drills down to per-product grain, # then DISTINCTCOUNTIF counts products with positive sales. 分销SKU数: type: measure formula: |- DISTINCTCOUNTIF({prod_id}, {销售量: {+d: prod_id}} &gt; 0) # Distribution store count: how many stores have sales &gt; 0? 分销客户数: type: measure formula: |- DISTINCTCOUNTIF({store_id}, {销售量: {+d: store_id}} &gt; 0) # Average sales per store (selling power) 卖力: type: measure formula: |- divide({销售额}, {分销客户数}) -- Query at brand × quarter grain — the +d inside each measure -- generates intermediate CTEs at prod_id / store_id grain, then rolls up. SELECT dt_quarter, brand, AGG(销售额), AGG(分销SKU数), AGG(分销客户数), AGG(卖力) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_quarter, brand When +d appears on a query dimension binding rather than inside a measure formula with an explicit outer aggregate, that is measure-as-dimension ( Measure-as-dimension ), not the two-step pattern above. Cross-dataset auto-join Independent measure datasets normally aggregate separately and compose with FULL OUTER JOIN on aligned dimensions ( key-union ). Composition / densify FOJ ON uses plain = (SQL NULL≠NULL ); row-preserving LEFT and nullpk invent stay null-safe . Do not assume the same NULL equality for composition and for pk() / detail spines. Authoring detail: join NULL equality . Emitted join type. FULL OUTER JOIN is the composition semantics, not the emitted form. The intermediate template emits FULL OUTER JOIN (or densify UNION + LEFT with the same bare- = contract); the SQL compiler may then reduce each join to the strongest type proven by query predicates: a filter that proves the primary side present (e.g. {primary_presence_measure} is not null ) lowers the emitted join to LEFT JOIN ; filters proving both sides required lower it to INNER JOIN . Inspect the emitted SQL for the final join type — a LEFT / INNER result means the presence contract was proven by authored filters, not that composition semantics or ON equality changed. For a LEFT population, filter on a dedicated primary presence measure that is non-null on every valid primary group and NULL on absent groups. For an INNER population, require presence from both sides. Use unfilled source counts or another proven presence signal; a nullable business value can discard valid groups, while missing-row fill can retain absent groups. Validate shared key meaning, complete-key uniqueness, nullable-key acceptance (bare = vs null-safe site), unmatched rows, and missing-row fill separately. Independent preaggregation does not repair duplicate lookup keys or a wrong alignment contract. SQL inspection establishes the generated shape; database validation and result comparison require separate checks. Grain safety enforcement (existing compiler behavior) The compiler enforces grain safety at compile time: Measure dimension-constraint violation : if a measure with allowed dimension dimensions: [[customer_id]] is referenced at a query dimension that also includes dt_date , the compiler rejects with: cannot set dimension [dt_date, customer_id]+d: customer_id since only [[customer_id]] are allowed The compiler now includes a Hint explaining that ... allows additional dimension fields but listed fields remain required, and suggests using +d to add missing required fields. Use ... to permit additional dimension fields; the listed fields remain required. Measure aggregation requires +d : SUM({measure}) fails with &quot;cannot aggregate a measure w/o +d modifier&quot;—the compiler includes a Hint explaining that without +d the measure is already at its target evaluation dimension with nothing finer to aggregate. This applies to any measure reference wrapped in an aggregation function, regardless of dimensions: declarations. The target evaluation dimension comes from query GROUP BY plus the measure&#39;s allowed-dimension constraint. +d: dim appends a dimension field and evaluates at the resulting finer grain; the outer SUM / AVG /… rolls back to the target dimension. Use SUM({measure: {+d: dim}}) (not bare {measure: {+d: dim}} as a rolled-up column) when that two-step contract is intended. Missing time context : time modifiers require a resolvable dim_dt and their macro prerequisites. A grouped time field or a time filter can supply the context. Incompatible cross-dataset grains : if two measures from different datasets don&#39;t share enough dimensions for the query grain, the compiler reports an error. Measure-as-dimension rules : see Measure-as-dimension and the release caveats. Missing entity keys are rejected, but static checks do not catch every invalid lowering. Evidence boundary: compile success alone does not prove that generated SQL is valid or that its numbers are correct. Inspect the classification and aggregation stages, validate live keys, and compare results separately. Time grain rules Prefer one explicit time grain for a clear reporting contract. The checked build accepts dt_year, dt_month together; this does not establish every multi-time modifier combination. Use period-start values for coarser time fields. Equality selects one period; structured range: [start, end] includes both endpoints at that grain. WHERE predicate order is an authoring convention, not a grammar restriction. Use the macro&#39;s actual prerequisites and resolved dim_dt ; a time filter can supply the context without a visible time grouping field. Measure-as-dimension A measure-as-dimension field is a member of the query dimension whose value is the result of evaluating a measure (or a scalar expression over such a measure), not a physical dataset column. It remains a query-dimension role ( dimension roles ): it contributes to the query result grain . It does not declare dataset identity. Explanation: Measure-as-dimension . This section states the intended multi-pass contract. The explanation identifies checked shapes and the classification stages to inspect. The compiler models these query columns separately from physical dimension columns and ordinary output measures while reusing normal measure calculation behavior. Syntax on columns.dimension Authoring forms and capability boundaries (DML-format query): Form Example Meaning Named measure + explicit +d - first_purchase_month: &#39;{first_purchase_month: {+d: store_id}}&#39; Evaluate the measure with store_id appended to its evaluation dimension; use the result as the query-dimension field Bare measure name - first_purchase_month Invalid for a measure-derived dimension. The use site must declare an explicit complete +d key. Scalar wrap over {m: {+d: dim}} - payment_day: &#39;cast({authorization_ts: {+d: payment_id}} as date)&#39; Evaluate the scalar once per complete key and use the result as a query-dimension field. Named scalar measure + +d on the query measure formula: &#39;cast({authorization_ts} as date)&#39; , then - payment_day: &#39;{dt_payment_day: {+d: payment_id}}&#39; Equivalent preferred authoring shape when the scalar is reusable dimension: / rank label (no +d ) - brand_bucket: &#39;iif({sales: {dimension: [brand], rank}} &lt; 5, {brand}, &#39;&#39;others&#39;&#39;)&#39; Query-dimension formula that replaces evaluation dimension via dimension: / uses rank ; not the +d multi-pass attachment pattern Prefer type: query for authoring and validating these bindings. SQL shown in generated fixture headers is not proof that the corresponding Model Query SQL round trip is supported. Evaluation semantics (multi-pass) Let M be the measure-as-dimension expression, K its explicitly authored +d fields, and Q the ordinary query-dimension fields. Its evaluation and attachment grain is G = Q ∪ K , excluding measure-derived labels. Internal aggregation stages do not redefine K . Fine-grain evaluation. Evaluate every measure-as-dimension at G . Evaluate ordinary output measures at G as well, preserving the states or component measures required for later rollup. Attach on the complete classification key G . The classification relation must have one scalar value per complete key. If it is not co-located with consuming measures, attach it before grouping those measures by its value. Store-month classification requires store and month in the join, not just +d: store_id . Coarser attachment requires its complete key to be available or provably derivable in the consumer; a finer attachment requires an explicit drill-down and rollup. Preserve NULL-key and query-population semantics. Roll up to query result grain. Aggregate ordinary measures to { measure-as-dimension fields } ∪ Q . Fields in K are dropped unless also present in Q . If an ordinary measure cannot be safely rewritten to evaluation at G followed by this rollup, compilation fails. This is the same executable idea as multi-level aggregation via +d ( multi-level aggregation ), specialized so the drilled measure result becomes a query-dimension field rather than only an intermediate input to a composite formula. source → evaluate measure-as-dimension + other measures at finer evaluation dimension (+d) → attach on the complete classification key → roll up to query result grain (GROUP BY measure-as-dimension field[, ordinary query-dimension fields]) +d compatibility Use explicit entity keys on the classification and participating sibling drill-down references. Compatibility is determined by resolved evaluation dimensions and scope, not only the spelling of +d payloads. Shared +d . Every measure-as-dimension requires an explicit complete key. Several independent labels are allowed only when their keys are equal as sets; declaration order within the key is irrelevant. Bare references. Do not infer entity grain from sibling measures. Use an explicit +d or dimension: entity key; a bare measure binding is rejected. Both forms compile for the checked customer-count classifier; that observation does not establish every classification shape. Adding or removing an output metric must not change classification membership. Different keys. Independent labels with unequal keys are rejected. A label may not depend on another label in the same query. General bridging between different entity grains requires a separate modeled relation. Constraints Classification requires explicit entity keys ( +d preferred; dimension: is also recognized). Multiple-label compatibility and safe rollup are contract requirements, not blanket release guarantees; validate the exact query. General period-modifier combinations are unverified. test_82d exercises a mat rolling-year classifier; this does not establish yoy / pop correctness. Use only combinations with explicit classification/report time scope and result evidence. Scalar transformations may be inline or named. They must yield one determined value per complete classification key. Measure dimensions: allowlists and grain-safety rules ( grain safety ) apply to every measure reference inside the measure-as-dimension expression at its effective evaluation grain. Cross-dataset auto-join ( cross-dataset composition ) still aggregates each dataset’s measures to a common query grain; a measure-as-dimension field is part of that query grain after attachment and roll-up. Group by the same completed classification value that is displayed. One row per complete query key is required, including NULL keys. A classifier does not authorize summing nonadditive measures or inventing inactive entities. Ratios retain their component semantics; an explicit outer aggregate over entity values remains the author&#39;s choice. Source filters apply through existing modifier rules. -d changes grain without removing filters; -f and -df provide the existing scope controls. A label predicate belongs after classification, and a report aggregate threshold after aggregation; neither may alter the inputs of its own classification. History-to-cutoff, rolling-window and current-selection classifiers are distinct business definitions. Example first_purchase_month below specifically means the first positive monthly net-sales month within the evaluation scope. purchase_span explicitly evaluates both endpoints at the entity grain and surrounds the finer result with the final aggregation. first_purchase_month: type: measure formula: &#39;MINIF({dt_month}, {sales: {+d: dt_month}} &gt; 0)&#39; report: type: query allows: ALL columns: dimension: - first_purchase_month: &#39;{first_purchase_month: {+d: store_id}}&#39; sales: purchase_span: &#39;SUM(datediff(day, {first_buy: {+d: store_id}}, {last_buy: {+d: store_id}}))&#39; Query result grain: [first_purchase_month] . Fine-grain evaluation via {+d: store_id} on the measure-as-dimension and on purchase_span . Final GROUP BY uses first_purchase_month and drops store_id . Modifier schema constraints From dmx_variable_schema.yaml , the modifier object has these structural constraints: dim_dt variants : at most one dim_dt / dim_dt as date / dim_dt as month / etc. per modifier object. +d and dimension : at most one per modifier object (mutually exclusive). -d and dimension : at most one per modifier object (mutually exclusive). +d and -d / -df : may coexist only when their dimension-name sets are disjoint (drill one field, remove another). Same-name intersection is illegal — see modifier composition . Multiple modifiers can coexist in a single modifier object (see combining modifiers for composition examples), subject to the intersection rule above. Modifier composition Preserve authored modifier order. Several macros may create intermediate measures; time macros on multi-path functions may instead propagate into each branch. See stacking semantics and its release probes. Write one YAML key per modifier: {+f: {a: 1, b: 2}} , not repeated +f keys. -df combines dimension removal with filter removal. Same-dimension modifier conflicts: The language contract requires a compile error when the field set of +d intersects the field set of either -d or -df within one modifier object. The current build does not enforce this requirement in the checked cases. Do not algebraically cancel to bare {m} , and do not silently rewrite {+d: x, -df: x} into {-f: x} (when the output grain already contains x , -df and -f differ). Illegal Why {销量: {+d: 门店, -d: 门店}} Add and remove the same field — no unique silent reading; canceling to {销量} hides typos and diverges from AVG({销量: {+d: 门店}}) (equal weight per store) {销量: {+d: 门店, -df: 门店}} Must not become {-f: 门店} ; grain vs filter-only semantics diverge Authors who intend a clear reading must write one of: {销量} / SUM({销量: {+d: 门店}}) / {销量: {-d:…\\|-df:…\\|-f:…}} . Different dimensions may still coexist, e.g. SUM({销量: {+d: 门店, -d: 品牌}}) . The current checked build accepts the checked same-field dimension conflicts and repeats regional totals at customer grain before summing them. This is a known implementation gap, not an accepted authoring pattern. Express one evaluation stage explicitly and inspect its SQL."},{"url":"reference--troubleshooting--grain-and-joins.html","title":"Grain and join pitfalls","text":"Grain and join pitfalls These entries cover evaluation grain, classifier attachment, windows, source membership, and join behavior. Join NULL equality owns equality rules. Filter placement errors Symptoms : [filters]: not supported , cannot derive from dimension , referred column … is undefined Cause : Predicates left in the wrong layer after --to-dml — filters: on a dataset (the schema rejects filters: on datasets entirely), dim scoping duplicated in fact sql: , or query filter shorthand on cross-dataset attributes. Fix : Classify each predicate by semantic scope ( Filter placement hierarchy ): Symptom Move to [dataset …] [filters]: not supported Query filters: , measure formula, or sql: WHERE (datasets don&#39;t support filters: at all) Lifecycle/attribute predicate repeated in every fact branch Query filters: only when it is report-varying; keep a row-removing representation when it defines permanent population Numeric/link predicate in fact sql: WHERE Use an intermediate metric gate only after proving rejected-only groups, sibling metrics, joins/windows, and absent-vs-present-NULL semantics referred column {DATASET}.col is undefined in query filter String form: &quot;{DATASET}.col = value&quot;: Two-element list dt_date: [&#39;2024-01-01&#39;, &#39;2025-12-31&#39;] returns only 2 days Bare list compiles to IN (...) exact match, not a range. Use dt_date: { range: [&#39;2024-01-01&#39;, &#39;2025-12-31&#39;] } for inclusive intervals Branch-specific date window Declare dt_date on that dataset; query filters: dt_date: range: [...] Alternative lookup eligibility can strengthen joins An eligibility rule such as “matches the target dimension or , failing that, an indirect dimension” uses two optional relationships. If the derived flag is placed in query filters: , compiled SQL may make one lookup required ( INNER JOIN ) or lower the AND / OR expression in a way that no longer preserves fallback semantics. Prefer this shape: target_state: pk({snapshot_month}, {customer_id}).{TARGET_CUSTOMER}.state customer_is_eligible: |- iif({target_state} = 1, 1, iif(pk({customer_id}).{INDIRECT_CUSTOMER}.customer_id is not null, 1, 0)) included_amount: iif({customer_is_eligible} = 1, {amount}, null) measures: amount: SUM({included_amount}) Then inspect the generated SQL: both lookups must remain LEFT JOIN , the fallback must remain inside CASE , and any output-group guard should reuse a selected primary measure ( HAVING AGG(amount) is not null ) so it does not leak an extra hidden column into the result. Treat non-unique keys and target/indirect overlap as diagnostics; DML relationships should not silently reproduce accidental fanout. P43. {dimension: [...]} vs {-d: dim_dt} (different SQL shape) Wrong : Using {-d: dim_dt} when golden SQL uses a separate GROUP BY CTE at reduced grain. Fix : Match structure: {dimension: [cols]} forces a separate aggregation CTE; {-d: dim_dt} uses window OVER (PARTITION BY …) . See P44 and P45 . P44. Window share vs {-d: dim} Wrong : Stocking draft divide({m}, {m_minus_dim_X}) from --to-dml as final business DML. Fix : Use divide({m}*1.0, {m: {-d: dim}}) in query columns: or measures. See dimension and filter modifiers . Equivalent to Model Query SQL; YAML-specific pitfalls. Full rules: YAML query declarations . P62. Treating measure dimensions: as dataset identity or query grouping Symptom : dimensions: [[region]] is written on a measure expecting the query output to be grouped by region (like dataset dimension: ); the query instead compiles with no such grouping or fails with a different query dimension. Wrong mental model : measure dimensions: declares dataset row identity or drives query grouping. Correct mental model : measure dimensions: contains allowed dimensions . Each inner list is one dimension; the outer list contains alternatives. Dataset identity belongs to dataset dimension: ; grouping belongs to query GROUP BY / columns.dimension: . Fix : Remove measure dimensions: unless the metric truly requires those allowed dimensions; put result grouping in the query ( GROUP BY / columns.dimension: ). # Wrong: expecting dimensions: to produce GROUP BY region sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount}) dimensions: - [region] # Right: no dimensions: lock; grouping lives in the query sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount}) # SELECT region, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region See the dimension-role contract in dimension roles . P64. materialize: true on a measure Symptom : ERROR error during processing [ref null]: undefined modifier &#39;materialize&#39; at compile time; the measure is silently dropped ( 0 measure(s) processed ). Wrong : adding materialize: true to a measure definition, expecting the compiler to pre-aggregate it. There is no measure-level materialize flag (verified with dmlc -c , dmlc 0.3.16). Fix : declare materialization with a top-level type: materialize object ( parameters: + dimension: + measures: ), or dataset-level materialize: &lt;name&gt; — never on a measure. See Materialize definition ( type: materialize ) and materialization examples . # Wrong sales: type: measure dataset: transactions formula: SUM({amount}) materialize: true # ← undefined modifier &#39;materialize&#39; # Right DWS_SALES: type: materialize parameters: dt_date: date dimension: [dt_date, product] measures: [sales] P66. Measure-as-dimension without an explicit entity key Symptom : compilation requires an explicit +d or dimension: entity key. Prefer +d and read the release limitations before using the replacement-dimension form. Wrong : using a bare measure and expecting another output measure to supply or imply its entity grain. Fix : declare the complete entity key at the dimension use site. Inline scalar wrappers are valid when their measure inputs carry the explicit key. See Measure-as-dimension . # Wrong dimension: - payment_day # Right, inline scalar dimension: - payment_day: &#39;cast({authorization_ts: {+d: payment_id}} as date)&#39; P67. Incompatible or dependent measure-derived dimensions Symptom : compilation reports incompatible classification keys or a dependency between labels. Wrong : one label uses {+d: store_id} while another uses {+d: customer_id} , or one label refers to another label declared by the same query. Fix : make independent labels use the same complete entity key. Key order may differ. Model a separate relation or reusable business measure when labels truly live at different grains. P68. Output measure cannot be rolled up from the classification grain Symptom : compilation reports missing aggregation or that reaggregation is impossible. Wrong : expose {sales: {+d: store_id}} directly as an output, sum already-computed distinct counts, or average ratios without defining the intended weighting. Fix : surround a finer-grain reference with the intended aggregation, such as SUM({sales: {+d: store_id}}) . For ratios, roll up components and recompute. For distinct, median, percentile, and similar measures, preserve suitable inputs/states or define a separate supported measure; otherwise accept the compile error. P69. Fine-grain WHERE hung on coarse query filters: Symptom : CTE aggregates at (G) (e.g. product×region), filters ( cnt &gt; 3 ), then SUM s to (Q) (e.g. product). Draft/ --to-dml emits query dimension (Q) only with filters: &quot;cnt &gt; 3&quot; and SUM({total: {+d: region}}) . Compile/fusion may turn this into product-level HAVING COUNT(1) &gt; 3 — wrong population. Wrong : filters: &quot;cnt &gt; 3&quot;: # cnt is not defined at product-only grain columns: dimension: [product] grand_total: &#39;SUM({total: {+d: region}})&#39; Also wrong : SUM({total: {+d: region, +f: &#39;{cnt} &gt; 3&#39;}}) uses an aggregate measure in an input predicate. The diagnostic refers to the predicate, not a general ban on applying +f to measure references. See the capability table . Fix — filter inside the roll-up at the shared fine grain: grand_total: |- SUMIF({total: {+d: region}}, {cnt: {+d: region}} &gt; 3) # No accepted fine rows -&gt; NULL; ELSE 0 would change that result. Keep query filters: for report/ HAVING scope only. See Multi-level aggregation with +d a. P70. OVER on an atomic measure Symptom : window functions are not allowed in atomic measures , or (older binaries) exit 0 with illegal SUM(…) OVER (…) … GROUP BY missing partition columns. Wrong : measures: 净购进量: &#39;SUM({qty}) over (partition by {store_id}, {prod_id})&#39; Fix : window placement — detail OVER under columns: , then atomic agg: columns: net_qty: &#39;SUM({qty}) over (partition by {store_id}, {prod_id})&#39; measures: sum_net_qty: &#39;SUM({net_qty})&#39; Post- GROUP-BY rank/share stays a composite (no dataset: ) with {-d:} / {-df:} . Contract: Window placement . P71. Bare {col} in measure PARTITION BY Symptom : … reference is not allowed in PARTITION BY / Hint about {dimension:} or {-d:} . Wrong (on a measure): SUM({total}) over (partition by {product}) . Fix : Detail row window → move to columns: and keep bare {product} there. Post-aggregation window → partition by {-d: region} or {dimension: product} on a composite measure. Do not put {-d:} under dataset columns: for a detail window — that branch uses bare column refs. Verified on dmlc 0.3.16 (basket-affinity migration, dual product roles). These compile clean and silently change semantics — always inspect the emitted ON clauses. P72. Two grain fields aliasing one column mis-anchor cross-dataset rollups Symptom : strict compile passes, diagnostics empty, but a ratio is wrong — e.g. lift denominator is left_count * left_count instead of left_count * right_count . Wrong : a dataset exposes two fields backed by one physical column ( left_product_id: &#39;{product_id}&#39; , right_product_id: &#39;{product_id}&#39; ), and a second-role rollup {sold_baskets: {-df: [left_product_id]}} (or {dimension: [month, store, right_product_id]} ) is expected to join on right_product_id . The compiler re-aliases the reduced temp&#39;s key and joins it on the first role&#39;s key ( support.product = pair.left_product_id ). Fix : look up each role with pk() into a sql: dataset that groups the source at (shared dims, product_id) with a declared dimension: (P77); pk() keys are positional and explicit, so left and right cannot collapse. Or keep -df rollups only at grains with no alias collision. Verify in compiled SQL: two separate joins, … product_id = t.left_product_id and … product_id = t.right_product_id . P73. -df rollup composites emit partner-only rows — and can fabricate key values Symptom : a measure rolled up from another dataset compiles to a FULL OUTER JOIN composite; rows that exist only in the rolled-up partner emit output rows. The partner temp may project max(&lt;col&gt;) fillers for grain fields it lacks, so an orphan (month, store, product) row can surface as left = right = product — a fabricated self-pair, not just NULLs. Fix : keep a selected primary measure that is NULL on partner-only rows and guard the query with a formula-keyed filter: filters: &#39;{pair_basket_count} is not null&#39;: # formula as key, empty value This compiles to a join-back WHERE &lt;primary&gt; IS NOT NULL — and the proven presence also reduces the emitted FULL OUTER JOIN to LEFT JOIN . Judge the final join type in emitted SQL, not the composition template. Prove the contract first: a valid primary group must never have a NULL primary measure, and the guard only holds when partner-only rows lack it. (Complements Dimension modifiers b.) P74. Two table: datasets on one physical table — merge quirks and silent -df grain loss Symptom : warn during processing [dataset B]: similar dataset(s) [A] exist, consolidate? — then, if the two datasets&#39; column sets differ, skip dimension column &lt;f&gt; for measure &lt;m&gt; which does not exist in dataset &lt;A&gt; and the rollup silently aggregates at a coarser grain (e.g. pk(store_id) instead of pk(store_id, left_id) ). Verified (dmlc 0.3.16) : two table: datasets on one table do not crash per se — the minimal symmetric case compiles, and a role-playing second dataset even anchors -df correctly ( right_id = t1.right_id ). The datasets are merged into one #TEMP via self-join on the pk. But adding a column to only one side makes the merge drop the other dataset&#39;s alias fields from -df resolution. (An earlier &quot;duplicate table: → NPE&quot; report was mis-attributed; the crash was P75&#39;s filter shape.) Fix : prefer one table: dataset per physical table with alias columns. If you must role-play, keep the column sets identical and verify every rollup temp&#39;s pk(...) in emitted SQL — a warning here is a semantic error, not a nit. P75. Measure guard filters in query filters: — shape and failure mode Right : &#39;{measure} is not null&#39;: — the entire formula is the key, value empty. This is the way to prune FOJ orphan rows at the query grain. Wrong : my_measure: {condition: &#39;...&#39;} → schema error ( condition is not a filter property). my_measure: &#39;&lt;expr&gt;&#39; treats the key as a column name; when it resolves to a measure, Query.treat_filters can NPE instead of emitting a diagnostic. Fix : use the bare-formula key and make sure the referenced measure resolves at the query grain; an unresolvable measure reference fails with NPE, not a readable error. P76. -df / -d arguments are declared grain field names, not source columns Symptom : column(s) [x] has been specified as -d/-df, but not in dimension [...] after renaming a query dimension through a binding ( columns.dimension: [..., new_name: &#39;{col}&#39;] ). Cause : the binding synthesizes an internal field ( new_name__&lt;query&gt; ); modifiers remove fields by their grain names, and the underlying column name no longer exists in the grain. Fix : pass the declared grain field names to -df / -d / dimension: . If a field must stay removable by name, keep it on the dataset under that name. P77. pk() into an aggregate relation: sql: target + dataset: on the wrapper Symptom : dataset must be specified when aggregate non-measure column, did you mean &lt;SupportDataset&gt;? on MAX(pk(...).{Support}.col) . Rules : pk() targets datasets (or the raw TABLE(pk_cols) form) — a measure cannot be a pk() target . An aggregate lookup value (e.g. count(distinct …) at a coarser grain) therefore needs a sql: dataset with a declared dimension: ; it compiles to an indexed temp that joins like any dimension. The wrapping aggregate measure iterates rows of the consuming dataset — set dataset: to it ( dataset: BasketProductPair , not the support dataset). A golden INNER JOIN on a guaranteed-match support lowers to LEFT JOIN ; acceptable only when the lookup keys are produced by the same population — note the reasoning in the escape ledger. P79. Same-dimension +d with -d / -df Wrong : {销量: {+d: 门店, -d: 门店}} or {销量: {+d: 门店, -df: 门店}} accepted and rewritten to bare {销量} or {-f: 门店} . Why : no unique silent reading; canceling hides typos; for AVG the fine stage differs from a bare reference; when the output grain already contains the field, -df ≠ -f . Fix : express one intended evaluation stage. Use a bare reference, an explicit fine-grain reference with an outer aggregate, or a removal modifier. Different fields can still coexist. The current checked build accepts the checked conflicts without diagnostics and repeats regional totals before an outer sum. This violates the required rejection contract. See modifier composition . P80. -f / -df drops a non-grain query filter from the report row set Wrong : query filter {store_id}=1 while a measure uses {-f: store_id} and store_id is not in the output grain → filter silently omitted → FOJ partner rows from the unfiltered arm appear. Fix : -f only removes the filter from measure evaluation ; query filters still define population. When the blocked filter cannot reference an output column, constrain rows with a correlated EXISTS / semi-join. Do not change grouping via -f alone. P81. Cross-grain dimension: time replacement without calendar broadcast Wrong : {销量: {dimension: [dt_quarter]}} on a dt_month query becomes a single scalar attach or fails to resolve dt_quarter from a month CTE. Fix : evaluate at quarter grain (calendar bridge from month/date), then broadcast the quarter total onto each month row in that quarter ( nullpk(pk(dt_month).CAL.dt_quarter) pattern). Additive SUM may re-aggregate months; non-additive measures need fact-level coarse grain. P78. A sql: escape must bake its own population/window predicates Symptom : query dt_* range filters do not push inside a sql: block&#39;s text — they land on the outer WHERE of the compiled derived temp. Inside a self-join escape an unbounded inner leg silently widens the population (e.g. right-leg rows outside the report window form pairs). Fix : bake permanent population predicates ( line_status = &#39;SOLD&#39; ) and symmetric window bounds into the escape&#39;s inner query so every leg/branch sees identical eligibility; let query filters: handle report scope on the outer keys. And when the escape reads a table: -owned physical table, reference from {Dataset} (P22b) — never the raw table name."},{"url":"reference--troubleshooting--migration.html","title":"Model authoring pitfalls","text":"Model authoring pitfalls Keep reusable definitions in the model and presentation choices in the query or reporting layer. P1. Including presentation logic in DML Wrong : Putting FORMAT(...) , CONCAT(...) , CAST(... AS VARCHAR) in measure formulas. Fix : Remove formatting from .dml . Handle in dashboard/rendering layer. P2. Including query-only derived columns in DML Wrong : Creating measures for calculations that only make sense in one specific query. Fix : Put query-specific expressions in Model Query SQL, not .dml . P5. Not verifying compiled SQL matches golden SQL Wrong : Assuming -c output is correct just because it compiles. Fix : Run both the original SQL and compiled SQL; compare result sets. P6. Creating one-off measures for filters or query-dimension combinations Wrong : sales_north , sales_south , sales_east — or sales_north when WHERE region = &#39;North&#39; suffices. Fix : One generic measure sales ; use WHERE region = &#39;North&#39; in queries. P7. Duplicating aggregation formulas Wrong : revenue: SUM({amount}) and total_revenue: SUM({amount}) . Fix : One atomic measure; reference it everywhere. Category: Model Query SQL"},{"url":"reference--troubleshooting--models.html","title":"Model definition pitfalls","text":"Model definition pitfalls These entries cover definitions, names, lookups, and metadata. Model structure and measure declarations own declaration syntax. P21. AGG() in measure formulas Wrong : sales: AGG({amount}) in .dml Fix : sales: SUM({amount}) — use SQL aggregation functions in .dml P22. Cross-dataset references without lookup Wrong : profit: SUM({sales.amount} - {costs.amount}) across datasets. Fix : Use column-macros with {DATASET}.column or pk(...) lookups. P22b. Joining a table: -owned physical table in another dataset&#39;s sql: Wrong : Policy has table: policy , and another dataset&#39;s sql: does from policy / join policy . Fix : Use the dataset, not the physical table: from {Policy} / join {Policy} . For attributes without a SQL join, use pk(...).{Policy}.Col . {Policy} in sql: is a table-source substitution (compiler rewrites it to the dataset query name); it is not a column expression. See Dataset definition ( type: dataset ) . P23. Missing dataset: on atomic measures Wrong : sales: type: measure, formula: SUM({amount}) — no dataset. Fix : sales: type: measure, dataset: transactions, formula: SUM({amount}) P24. Overly specific measure names Wrong : sales_q1_2024_north , sales_john_doe . Fix : Generic measures + query filters. P25. Not documenting edge cases Wrong : Measure without description that &quot;excludes returns&quot; or &quot;includes zeros&quot;. Fix : Always add description: for non-obvious behavior. Document the dataset dimension and row meaning, scope flags, and misleading names— Write model metadata . P27. Mixing dimension-constrained measures with incompatible queries Wrong : Selecting sales_by_product (allowed dimension [product] ) and total_sales in a query whose query dimension is [dt_month] . Fix : Ensure every measure permits the query dimension and that their row meanings are grain-compatible, or use separate queries. P28. Using +d when GROUP BY suffices Wrong : Creating sales_by_region: SUM({sales: {+d: region}}) . Fix : sales: SUM({amount}) + query: SELECT region, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region P28b. Null contagion when a joined row is missing Wrong : profit: &#39;{revenue} - {costs}&#39; — if either side has no row for the slice (FOJ / sparse dt), that input is null and the difference becomes null. Fix — fill default when row missing ( missing-row fill ): Activate sparse inputs with outermost isnull / ifnull / coalesce(公式, 字面量) : revenue: &#39;isnull(SUM({amount}), 0)&#39; # or wrap the existing measure formula outermost costs: &#39;isnull(SUM({cost}), 0)&#39; profit: &#39;{revenue} - {costs}&#39; # use sites of revenue/costs → 0 If the result column itself must be non-null when its grain row is missing, activate on the result: profit: &#39;isnull({revenue} - {costs}, 0)&#39; Not enough / not activation: isnull({a}, {b}) , coalesce(a, b, 0) , bare {m: pp} , or expecting an unrelated outer composite to gain an automatic isnull wrap. Embedding an already-activated dependency in a composite still expands that dep’s fill at sparse FOJ use sites (see measures.md Fill default ). P28c. Null-safe ON on composition FOJ ( key-union ) Wrong : Expecting multi-arm FOJ / densify to treat NULL dimension keys as one shared “unknown” entity (Power Query–style null-safe merge), or rewriting composition ON to null-safe so both arms’ empty keys collapse into one row with both measures filled. Fix : Composition uses bare = ( join NULL equality ). Empty keys stay unmatched per arm. For a single unknown bucket, author an explicit sentinel ( coalesce(dim, &#39;…&#39;) ) into the grain. Keep null-safe for row-preserving LEFT / nullpk only. Population ≠ ON: use presence / {primary} is not null when the report should drop secondary-only keys. Category: Time Handling P37. Modifiers on columns Wrong : {customer_id: {+d: customer_id}} in a formula. Fix : Put drill-down on the measure being evaluated: {sales: {+d: customer_id}} . Other modifiers such as input +f also have column forms; this is not a ban on all column modifiers. P38. Unquoted YAML formulas Wrong : formula: SUMIF({sales: {+d: dt_month}}, {dt_month} = {first_month}) without quotes when the value contains : , { , or - . Fix : formula: &#39;SUMIF(...)&#39; P39. dataset: on composite measures Wrong : Composite measure that references other measures still has dataset: . Fix : Omit dataset: when the formula references other measures; keep dataset: on atomic measures only. P40. Measure cannot be redefined / duplicate formula Wrong : SELECT AGG(sales) AS sales or AGG(x) AS x when sales / x already exist as measures. Fix : Use SELECT AGG(sales) with no alias, or a neutral alias ( AS sales_value ). If the compiler says use existing measure &quot;X&quot; , use AGG(X) instead of re-expressing the formula. P41. DML {...} syntax in Model Query SQL Wrong : SELECT {dt_month}, AGG({sales}) FROM MODEL — braces are for .dml formulas only. Fix : SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL . Use AGG({sales: pop}) only for modifier form in queries. P42. Bare name vs {col} inside .dml formulas Wrong (meaning): Treating formula: calc_amt * 2 as “forgot braces” / a compiler hole. Bare calc_amt binds as a physical table column (SQL pass-through). Fix: Brace when the intent is the calculated column’s definition: formula: &#39;{calc_amt} * 2&#39; . Leave physical columns bare: net: amount - discount . See Dataset definition ( type: dataset ) ."},{"url":"reference--troubleshooting--queries.html","title":"Query syntax pitfalls","text":"Query syntax pitfalls These entries cover SQL and YAML queries. Model Query SQL and YAML queries own the supported syntax. P8. Forgetting AGG() in aggregation queries Wrong : SELECT product, sales FROM MODEL GROUP BY product Fix : SELECT product, AGG(sales) FROM MODEL GROUP BY product P9. Adding redundant alias on AGG() Wrong : SELECT AGG(sales) as sales Fix : SELECT AGG(sales) — auto-aliases to sales P10. Using subqueries Wrong : SELECT * FROM MODEL WHERE sales &gt; (SELECT AVG(sales) FROM MODEL) Fix : Use HAVING with a measure: HAVING AGG(sales) &gt; AGG(avg_sales) P11. Using JOINs Wrong : SELECT a.*, b.* FROM MODEL a JOIN MODEL b ON a.id = b.id Fix : Use cross-dataset column-macros or pk(...) lookups in .dml P12. Missing time context for time modifiers Wrong : SELECT product, AGG({sales: yoy}) FROM MODEL GROUP BY product (neither a grouped time field nor a time filter) Rule : Resolve the time context and satisfy macro prerequisites. An explicit grouped time field is clearest; a time filter can also supply the context. Prefer one time grain for readability. Time-context reference : Time modifiers . Correct (monthly YoY) : SELECT dt_month, AGG(sales), AGG({sales: yoy}) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_month Correct (YTD at day grain) : SELECT dt_date, AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_date = date(current_date) GROUP BY dt_date P13. Mixing time granularities Guidance : Prefer one time grain. The checked build accepts dt_year, dt_month together; acceptance of multiple fields is not a guarantee that every macro combination has the intended context. Inspect the resolved time grain. P14. Adding unnecessary null filters Wrong : WHERE customer is not null when not explicitly requested. Fix : Only exclude nulls when the question specifically asks for it. P15. Using BETWEEN for single periods Guidance : dt_month = &#39;2024-01-01&#39; clearly selects January. A date interval is valid when that interval is intended; use explicit bounds and check timestamp/end-day semantics. P16. Time-filter order is a style convention Both predicate orders compile. Predicate order is a readability choice, not an engine requirement. P17. Using ORDER BY without need Wrong : Adding ORDER BY to every query. Fix : Only use ORDER BY for Top N or when the question explicitly asks for ranking. P18. Manual growth rate calculation Wrong : DIVIDE(AGG(sales), AGG(sales, &#39;ya&#39;)) - 1 Fix : AGG(sales, &#39;yoy&#39;) — built-in modifier. P20. Using DISTINCT alone for existence checks Wrong : SELECT DISTINCT customer FROM MODEL.transactions for &quot;do we have any customers?&quot; Fix : SELECT customer, dt_date, transaction_id FROM MODEL.transactions LIMIT 1 — return specific record. Category: DML Model Design P45. Measure in a detail query Wrong : dataset: DATASET_PRODUCTS on a query that selects total_cost: (a measure). Fix : Remove dataset: for aggregate mode, or drop the measure from the detail query. P46. Bare column name in aggregate query Wrong : prod_id: with no dataset: when the column lives on DATASET_PRODUCTS . Fix : prod_id: &quot;{DATASET_PRODUCTS}.prod_id&quot; . P47. Mismatched dimension field names across datasets Wrong : DATASET_PT uses dimension id ; DATASET_MC uses tag_id . Fix : Align to one name (e.g. tag_id ) and map warehouse columns in columns: ( tag_id: id ). P48. Mapping used as a dimension field in dimension: Wrong : - f_tag_id: {DATASET_PT}.id under columns: dimension: . Fix : List - f_tag_id only; define f_tag_id: |- … formula separately. P49. Dimension field under measures: Wrong : tag_id: with a formula under dataset measures: . Fix : Declare grain under dataset dimension: ; use measures only for aggregations. P50. Complex filter in query filters: Wrong : EXISTS subquery or multi-field PK chain in query filters: . Fix : Move predicate into the fact dataset&#39;s sql: WHERE clause. P51. PK chaining parameter count mismatch Wrong : pk(1, tag_id).{DATASET_PRODUCTS}.state when DATASET_PRODUCTS has composite PK (brand_id, tag_id) . Fix : pk(brand_id, tag_id).{DATASET_PRODUCTS}.state with all PK columns. P52. Manual GROUP BY in after: Wrong : Re-aggregating with group by in after: on a standard measure query. Fix : Let the query block aggregate measures. Omit after: for automatic dimension ordering. When custom ordering/limit requires it, reference {QUERY} and keep the block to presentation ( select * from {QUERY} plus ORDER BY/TOP/LIMIT) unless unavoidable custom post-SQL is explicitly documented. For commands and result checks, see Compile and validate a query . P60. Subquery for MAX date in aggregation query Wrong : WHERE dt_month = (SELECT MAX(dt_month) FROM MODEL ...) Fix : Hardcode probe month or SELECT MAX(dt_month) FROM MODEL.&lt;dataset&gt; detail query. P61. Aggregating directly on dataset with GROUP BY Wrong : SELECT 购买方代码, 品牌, SUM(出厂价金额) FROM MODEL.DATASET_X GROUP BY 购买方代码, 品牌 Symptom : cannot aggregate directly on dataset MODEL.DATASET_X using GROUP BY Fix : Either (a) define measures in model.dml and use FROM MODEL with AGG() , or (b) use inline measures via /** dml ... **/ comment blocks for ad-hoc queries without editing .dml (see Inline measures ( /** dml ... **/ ) )"},{"url":"reference--troubleshooting--README.html","title":"Troubleshooting index","text":"Troubleshooting index Find a symptom or a stable pitfall ID below. Troubleshoot a query gives the diagnostic procedure. Error codes describes structured diagnostics. Error → fix quick reference Error / symptom Fix Pitfall UNION is not supported Separate queries or measures with different filters P10 aggregation is not possible with MODEL.DATASET_* FROM MODEL + AGG(measure) P8 , P45 subquery is not supported DML measure or +f / HAVING with AGG() P10 JOIN is not supported {DATASET}.column in column-macros P11 ref {...} not defined Define in model; in SQL use bare names P41 measure cannot be redefined Drop AS measure_name on AGG(measure_name) P40 use existing measure &quot;X&quot; instead of recreating… AGG(X) only P40 ... was referenced but not defined in sql Remove {...} from Model Query SQL (except modifier form inside AGG({…}) ) P41 file not found: SELECT ... (Windows) Replace / operator with DIVIDE() in inline CLI Model Query SQL P63 table … is owned by dataset … from {Dataset} / join {Dataset} , or pk(...).{Dataset}.Col P22b Measure-as-dimension: missing +d , incompatible label keys, or unsafe sibling rollup Add a complete explicit +d key to every measure-as-dimension; use compatible keys and aggregatable output measures P66 – P68 Same-object {+d:x,-d:x} / {+d:x,-df:x} silently becomes bare {m} or {-f:x} Illegal — must fail loudly; rewrite to an explicit intent P79 -f / -df arm drops a non-grain query filter → FOJ partner rows leak Keep report population constrained ( EXISTS / semi-join); -f does not change grouping P80 {m:{dimension:[dt_quarter]}} on a month query attaches as a scalar / wrong grain Calendar-bridge the coarse CTE and broadcast by quarter P81 Fine-grain CTE WHERE then outer SUM rewritten as coarse filters: &quot;cnt &gt; 3&quot; SUMIF / SUM(iif) with matching {+d: …} inside the measure P69 window functions are not allowed in atomic measures / illegal GROUP BY + OVER Detail OVER → columns: ; post- GROUP-BY → composite / {-d:} P70 – P71 … reference is not allowed in PARTITION BY Bare {col} on a measure window — move detail to columns: or use {-d:} / {dimension:} P71 Rollup ratio denominator collapses ( left² instead of left × right ), no diagnostic Two grain fields alias one column — -df / dimension: anchored the second role on the first key; use pk() into a declared-dimension sql: dataset P72 Extra rows (or fake left = right rows) from a rollup composite Cross-dataset -df emits FULL OUTER JOIN ; guard with &#39;{primary_measure} is not null&#39;: P73 -df rollup drops a grain field with only a &quot;skip dimension column&quot; warning Two table: datasets on one physical table with asymmetric column sets — keep column sets identical or avoid role-playing datasets P74 column(s) [x] … not in dimension on -df after a dimension rename -df args are declared grain field names, not source columns P76 dataset must be specified … did you mean X on MAX(pk(…)) Set dataset: to the consuming dataset P77 Find a pitfall by ID ID Topic P1 Including presentation logic in DML P2 Including query-only derived columns in DML P5 Not verifying compiled SQL matches golden SQL P6 Creating one-off measures for filters or query-dimension combinations P7 Duplicating aggregation formulas P8 Forgetting AGG() in aggregation queries P9 Adding redundant alias on AGG() P10 Using subqueries P11 Using JOINs P12 Missing time context for time modifiers P13 Mixing time granularities P14 Adding unnecessary null filters P15 Using BETWEEN for single periods P16 Time-filter order is a style convention P17 Using ORDER BY without need P18 Manual growth rate calculation P20 Using DISTINCT alone for existence checks P21 AGG() in measure formulas P22 Cross-dataset references without lookup P22b Joining a table: -owned physical table in another dataset&#39;s sql: P23 Missing dataset: on atomic measures P24 Overly specific measure names P25 Not documenting edge cases P27 Mixing dimension-constrained measures with incompatible queries P28 Using +d when GROUP BY suffices P28b Null contagion when a joined row is missing P28c Null-safe ON on composition FOJ ( key-union ) P29 Non-first-day time filters P30 Missing current day in past-N-days P31 Including current period in past-N-months P32 Using ytd modifier with full-period GROUP BY P33 Period-to-date with date range P34 Not running validateOnly before trusted use P35 Ignoring column descriptions P36 Mismatched dialect P37 Modifiers on columns P38 Unquoted YAML formulas P39 dataset: on composite measures P40 Measure cannot be redefined / duplicate formula P41 DML {...} syntax in Model Query SQL P42 Bare name vs {col} inside .dml formulas P43 {dimension: [...]} vs {-d: dim_dt} (different SQL shape) P44 Window share vs {-d: dim} P45 Measure in a detail query P46 Bare column name in aggregate query P47 Mismatched dimension field names across datasets P48 Mapping used as a dimension field in dimension: P49 Dimension field under measures: P50 Complex filter in query filters: P51 PK chaining parameter count mismatch P52 Manual GROUP BY in after: P53 PowerShell &amp;&amp; chaining P53b FROM name ≠ --model= file name P63 / operator in inline CLI Model Query SQL on Windows P54 CJK JSON via PowerShell pipe P59 YoY without column probe P60 Subquery for MAX date in aggregation query P61 Aggregating directly on dataset with GROUP BY P62 Treating measure dimensions: as dataset identity or query grouping P64 materialize: true on a measure P66 Measure-as-dimension without an explicit entity key P67 Incompatible or dependent measure-derived dimensions P68 Output measure cannot be rolled up from the classification grain P69 Fine-grain WHERE hung on coarse query filters: P70 OVER on an atomic measure P71 Bare {col} in measure PARTITION BY P72 Two grain fields aliasing one column mis-anchor cross-dataset rollups P73 -df rollup composites emit partner-only rows — and can fabricate key values P74 Two table: datasets on one physical table — merge quirks and silent -df grain loss P75 Measure guard filters in query filters: — shape and failure mode P76 -df / -d arguments are declared grain field names, not source columns P77 pk() into an aggregate relation: sql: target + dataset: on the wrapper P79 Same-dimension +d with -d / -df P80 -f / -df drops a non-grain query filter from the report row set P81 Cross-grain dimension: time replacement without calendar broadcast P78 A sql: escape must bake its own population/window predicates"},{"url":"reference--troubleshooting--time.html","title":"Time context pitfalls","text":"Time context pitfalls These entries cover period values, comparison context, and missing history. Evaluation semantics owns the time-context contract. P29. Non-first-day time filters Wrong : dt_month = &#39;2024-01-15&#39; or dt_month = &#39;Jan 2024&#39; . Fix : dt_month = &#39;2024-01-01&#39; — always first day. P30. Missing current day in past-N-days Wrong : dt_date BETWEEN date(current_date, &#39;-7 days&#39;) AND date(current_date, &#39;-1 day&#39;) . Fix : dt_date BETWEEN date(current_date, &#39;-6 days&#39;) AND date(current_date) — 7 days including today. P31. Including current period in past-N-months Wrong : dt_month BETWEEN date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;, &#39;-12 months&#39;) AND date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;) . Fix : dt_month BETWEEN date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;, &#39;-12 months&#39;) AND date(current_date, &#39;start of month&#39;, &#39;-1 month&#39;) — exclude current (incomplete) month. P32. Using ytd modifier with full-period GROUP BY Wrong : SELECT dt_year, AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;) FROM MODEL GROUP BY dt_year . Fix : ytd is cumulative. Use dt_date grain: SELECT dt_date, AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;) FROM MODEL WHERE dt_date = date(current_date) GROUP BY dt_date . P33. Period-to-date with date range Wrong : WHERE dt_date BETWEEN date(current_date, &#39;start of year&#39;) AND date(current_date) for YTD. Fix : WHERE dt_date = date(current_date) with AGG(sales, &#39;ytd&#39;) — modifier handles the range. Category: Compilation and Validation P59. YoY without column probe Wrong : AGG(销售额, &#39;yoy&#39;) when 去年同期出厂价金额 sums to zero. Fix : Detail query SUM(去年同期出厂价金额) before promising YoY narrative."},{"url":"reference--troubleshooting--tooling.html","title":"Compiler and tool pitfalls","text":"Compiler and tool pitfalls These entries cover validation, CLI capture, dialects and model filenames. Compile and validate owns the command workflow. P34. Not running validateOnly before trusted use Wrong : Writing a query and immediately running it without validation when database validation is available. Fix : Run --validate-only on the intended connection before trusted use. It catches compile errors, checks complete dataset dimensions reached by the query against observed data, and probes the main query with EXPLAIN; it does not prove result correctness or future uniqueness. Prerequisite : Validation requires an executable with --validate-only and a configured connection. Check --help and --advanced-help . If either prerequisite is missing, record database validation as not run. See live validation . P35. Ignoring column descriptions Wrong : Using customer_name when description says &quot;internal use only, use customer instead&quot;. Fix : Read and follow column descriptions. Replace internal fields with their public aliases. P36. Mismatched dialect Wrong : Assuming every SQLite date expression is accepted unchanged by MSSQL. Fix : DML lowers supported SQLite-style date expressions to the target dialect. Inspect generated SQL and test the exact expression; unsupported forms need a dialect-appropriate alternative. See date expressions . Category: Compiler, YAML, and naming P53. PowerShell &amp;&amp; chaining Wrong : cd path &amp;&amp; dmlc --model=model.dml query.sql Fix : cd path; dmlc --model=model.dml query.sql P53b. FROM name ≠ --model= file name Wrong : dmlc q.sql --model=acme.dml with SQL SELECT AGG(x) FROM MODEL → model file &quot;acme.dml&quot; does not match FROM &quot;MODEL&quot; (expected &quot;MODEL.dml&quot;) (exit 65). Fix : Change to FROM acme ; or use --model=model.dml / keep FROM MODEL ; or pass the directory that contains the right .dml ( --model=&lt;dir&gt; finds &lt;FROM&gt;.dml inside). Note : dmlc -e rejects directories — it needs a .dml file. See Model Query SQL §&quot;Query types&quot;. P63. / operator in inline CLI Model Query SQL on Windows Symptom : file not found: SELECT ... — the / in a / b makes the CLI treat the SQL string as a file path. Fix : use DIVIDE(a, b) in inline CLI invocations, or pass the query via a .sql file instead of a command-line argument. P54. CJK JSON via PowerShell pipe Wrong : dmlc ... | Out-File result.json or capturing stdout for Chinese field names. Fix : dmlc ... -o result.json --overwrite or Node execSync(..., { encoding: &#39;utf8&#39; }) ."},{"url":"support.html","title":"排错：先确定失败在哪一步","text":"排错：先确定失败在哪一步 先保存完整诊断，再缩小到一个指标。 编译通过不代表源键与数据一致；validate-only 通过也不代替业务结果核对。 现象 下一步 找不到模型 核对文件名与 FROM； 查询发现规则（英文） 编译报错 保留退出码和 JSON； 错误码参考（英文） DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE 修正键或数据后重测； 重复键验证 总数、占比或历史值不符 核对输入行、筛选、分组； 修饰符（英文） 物化结果过期 物化生命周期 准备可复现材料 保存 dmlc --version 、命令、退出码、完整诊断、最小模型/查询、脱敏输入和预期结果。删除密码、令牌、真实连接串及个人信息。通过已有产品联系人反馈；本站尚未列出公开支持渠道，不要把私密数据提交到无关仓库。 版本与已知限制 具体构建的观察只适用于所记录的构建和夹具。历史问题不是当前版本缺陷清单。遇到差异时，用当前编译器重现并保留生成 SQL。先看 销售练习 的验证状态；其他方言或未执行场景应标记“未验证”。"},{"url":"tutorials--first-query.html","title":"Compile your first model query","text":"Compile your first model query For a separately checked DuckDB execution path using the tutorial sales data, use the runnable sales exercise (中文) . The original tutorial records remain unchanged. We will build a sales model and compile a query that returns totals by region. We need an installed dmlc.exe and a writable working directory. This tutorial compiles SQL without a database connection. Identify the compiler Install dmlc.exe and add its directory to PATH. Open PowerShell and save the executable path before creating a working directory. $dmlc = (Get-Command dmlc.exe).Source &amp; $dmlc --version The output includes the version, build timestamp, and platform. The tutorial compiler record identifies the build used to check this tutorial. Now, create a new directory for our files. New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path first-query Set-Location first-query The prompt now ends in first-query . Define the model Save the following content as model.dml . FACT_SALES: type: dataset table: TABLE_SALES dimension: [id] columns: id: region: amount: measures: sales: SUM({amount}) cnt: COUNT(1) average_sale: type: measure formula: &#39;DIVIDE({sales}, {cnt})&#39; This model uses the checked default SQL dialect, mssql . For another target, declare dialect: at the model root before compiling; the connection does not choose the dialect. The dialect reference separates recognized targets from executed coverage. FACT_SALES declares one source row per id . sales and cnt aggregate those rows. average_sale divides their results. Now, lint the model. &amp; $dmlc --lint model.dml --output-format=json $LASTEXITCODE The process exits with 0 . If lint reports a finding, compare the saved file with the model above before continuing. Write the query Save the following content as query.sql beside model.dml . SELECT region, AGG(sales), AGG(average_sale) FROM MODEL GROUP BY region FROM MODEL selects model.dml . Each result row represents one region. Compile the query Now, compile the query in strict mode. &amp; $dmlc --compile --strict query.sql --model=model.dml --output-format=json --output-dir=gen $LASTEXITCODE The JSON reports status: passed , exitCode: 0 , and an empty diagnostics array. artifacts.sql contains SUM(amount) , COUNT(1) , and GROUP BY region . The final projection contains region , sales , and average_sale . The compiler may include a DATASET_GRAIN_UNVERIFIED note. We have compiled the declared model without checking a physical TABLE_SALES table. Inspect the result Open the SQL artifact under gen . Find the aggregate expressions and the grouping by region . We now have a complete model and consumer query, with generated SQL to inspect. To execute the query, use a connection with the declared source table. Follow Use the dmlc CLI for live key validation and execution. Core concepts explains how the model and query layers fit together."},{"url":"tutorials--interactive-journey.html","title":"Build a regional report with the interactive tutorial","text":"Build a regional report with the interactive tutorial For a separately checked DuckDB execution path using the tutorial sales data, use the runnable sales exercise (中文) . The original tutorial records remain unchanged. We will build a February sales report with completed sales, a previous-month comparison, average customer sales, and regional share. Open A small journey through DML in a browser. Keep the page and its sibling files together for offline use. Use the button beneath the introduction to start or resume at your next incomplete chapter. The tutorial calculates invented rows in your browser. It does not compile arbitrary DML or connect to a database. Recorded companion queries include real compiler output. We will download a complete project at the end. Choose an answer, then click Submit answer to submit it. Selecting an option alone does not complete a chapter. Dotted code names and result headings offer short hints on hover or keyboard focus; click or tap them to explore their definitions. 1. Identify each source row Read the four sales in chapter 1. Find the two North rows. Choose [id] as the source dimension and check your answer. The feedback explains why region is a grouping field rather than a unique source key. The progress indicator now shows one completed chapter. 2. Find an atomic measure Read the model declaration in chapter 2. Select the measure that sums a source column and check the answer. We now have reusable definitions for sales and row count. 3. Reuse measures in a formula Read DIVIDE({sales}, {cnt}) in chapter 3. For North, calculate 30 ÷ 2 = 15 and check your answer. The same formula gives 100 ÷ 4 = 25 for all four sales. A measure reference takes its value from the report&#39;s current grouping and filters. Expand the complete declaration to see how a composite measure reuses atomic measures without a dataset: property. 4. Change the grouping Select All sales , By region , and By sale in chapter 4. Watch the query, result table, and average calculation change together. All sales gives 100 and an average of 25. The regional view gives North 30 and South 70. Click average_sale in the query to see its model declaration. Under References , open sales and then amount to trace the source column, or open cnt to see the row-count formula. Click a grouping field to inspect its column mapping. Close the definition with × or Escape. These hints also work in later chapters&#39; formulas and result headings. Answer the question about the source dimension. It remains [id] throughout. At source-key grain the checked compiler reports an aggregation-simplification finding; that simplification relies on the declared key being unique. 5. Separate compilation from execution Follow the model-and-query diagram in chapter 5. Choose what a successful compile produces and check the answer. Then trace the separate checks: inspect compiled SQL, validate reached source keys and SQL planning on a connection, and compare executed results with an independent answer. 6. Compare report filters and measure filters Open the eight-row source table in chapter 6. Our original four sales now belong to February. Four January rows provide comparison history. Inspect the complete extended model. Select North as the report region. The result shows sales = 30 and completed_sales = 10 : one North sale is returned. Find +f in the completed_sales definition, then check the chapter exercise. In {sales: ...} , the name before the colon is the measure and the settings after it modify this reference&#39;s calculation. f means filter: +f adds a filter to this reference, keeping the original sales definition unchanged. Expand Two ways to write modifier settings to compare a named modifier with a configuration object. 7. Choose a denominator Select North in chapter 7. With -d: region , the denominator is 30 and the share is 100%. Switch to -df: region . The denominator becomes 100 and the share becomes 30%. Remember d = dimension and f = filter. -d removes a field from this reference&#39;s evaluation grouping; -df also removes its related filters. The month filter, source key and report grouping stay unchanged. Without a related region filter, both modifiers produce the same denominator in this example. Inspect the recorded compiler query and its strict findings. Check the exercise about retaining an all-region denominator. The report still selects North; the denominator reference has a different filter context. 8. Calculate at customer grain Select AVG in chapter 8. Find South customer C&#39;s total of 70 in stage 1. Stage 2 averages that one customer total, giving 70. Average transaction amount is 35 because it averages two source transactions. Switch the outer aggregate to SUM , then MAX . Observe both stages and the formula. Return to AVG and check the exercise. +d: customer adds customer to the evaluation dimension. The outer aggregate explicitly combines customer totals back to the query dimension. The source dimension still identifies source rows; the query dimension still groups result rows. 9. Compare adjacent months Read the two model definitions shown in chapter 9. previous_sales uses {sales: pp} for the previous month&#39;s value; sales_change uses {sales: pop} for growth. Select February 2025 . North has sales of 30, previous sales of 20, and growth of 50%. South has sales of 70, previous sales of 50, and growth of 40%. Read {sales: pp} as “sales, for the previous period.” The month context makes that the previous month. pop returns a decimal growth rate; the page displays 0.5 as 50%. Select January 2025 . Previous sales and growth are NULL because the example has no December facts. Check which period pp requests. Inspect the recorded companion query before treating its expected strict findings as resolved. 10. Break and repair a lookup key In chapter 10, enable Add an invalid second North directory row . Four facts become six joined rows. North&#39;s amounts now sum to 60. Disable the duplicate. Four facts again produce four joined rows and North totals 30. Check the exercise about source uniqueness. The compiler snapshot does not inspect these physical records. To check the keys on a database connection, expand Catch the duplicate key with dmlc in chapter 10. Save its manager lookup query as lookup.sql beside the downloaded model. The query must reach DIM_REGION for its dimension to be checked. Replace default with the connection containing your sample tables, then run: dmlc.exe --validate-only lookup.sql --model=model.dml --connection=default --output-format=json If that connection&#39;s TABLE_REGION contains both North entries, expect DIMENSION_NOT_UNIQUE for DIM_REGION . Correct the dataset dimension declaration or source data, then rerun validation. The browser toggle changes only this page&#39;s invented data. Validation reads the connected data, checks reached dataset dimensions, and probes the main query with EXPLAIN without executing its result rows. 11. Make missing-value behavior explicit In chapter 11, switch from Keep NULL to Use a literal zero fallback . The same January result groups remain. Only the prior-period value changes from NULL to 0. Read the filled measure formula and check what changes in January. The fallback supplies a value where that measure is used. It does not generate new calendar rows or decide the business meaning of missing history. 12. Build and download the final report Chapter 12&#39;s Try it exercise asks you to build February&#39;s report for both regions. Select February, group by region, and include all regions. Select completed sales, previous sales, average customer sales, and all-region share. Sales is already selected. Compare Model notebook · model.dml with Your question · query.sql below the controls. The notebook excerpt shows your selected measures and their dependencies. Expand the complete notebook or download model.dml to inspect the source dataset declarations. The result table follows both panes. Choose Submit answer beside the report controls. If a setting is missing, follow the feedback and try again, or choose Show explanation for guidance. Completing this exercise marks the twelfth stop complete. The completed configuration shows these expected example values: region sales completed_sales previous_sales average_customer_sales all_region_share North 30 10 20 15 0.3 South 70 70 50 70 0.7 The browser formats shares as percentages. The model and downloaded expected-result CSV contain decimal shares. Choose Download the checked project . Extract the project archive to a writable directory. Open its START-HERE.txt . The archive contains the complete model, final query, source CSVs, expected result, recorded compiler output, and optional SQL Server setup with a calendar table. To save a different report configuration, choose Download my query.sql . Only the recorded companion queries have the included compiler evidence. Other builder combinations still need compilation and SQL inspection. Compile the downloaded project With dmlc.exe available, open a terminal in the extracted directory and run these commands: dmlc.exe --lint model.dml --output-format=json dmlc.exe --compile --strict query.sql --model=model.dml --output-format=json --output-dir=gen Inspect the diagnostics and generated SQL. The recorded capstone query has a key-union join warning, status: findings , and exit 1. The join can include regions present only in the comparison month. Check whether those regions belong in your report, then inspect customer-grain aggregation and the final projection. SQL generation does not establish database result correctness. For optional database practice, use a new empty SQL Server database and review setup-mssql.sql . It creates the source tables and the calendar required by the time-comparison SQL. Validate the keys, execute the generated SQL, and compare every result value with expected.csv . This live database exercise has not been run as part of the documentation checks. For connection and validation commands, follow Compile and validate a query . The language reference covers more advanced windows, classifiers, materializations, and model composition after this beginner project. Resume or restart Use the globe and language selector in the fixed header to switch between English, 中文, 日本語, and Deutsch. Each language keeps its own name, so you can find it without reading the current page language. The twelve lessons, questions, explanations, navigation, and feedback change together. Your answers, report settings, and completed chapters stay in place. The browser remembers your language separately from your progress. Code, field names, source values, and the downloadable project retain their original identifiers. The fixed header shows your current chapter. Use its arrows to move between lessons, or open the chapter title to see all twelve chapters and your completed count. A check mark identifies completed chapters. At the final chapter, the return arrow opens the introduction. Open Symbol guide in that menu to revisit modifiers and the source, query and evaluation dimension roles. The guide follows chapter 12 and does not count as an additional chapter. The sample model uses FACT_SALES and DIM_REGION as logical dataset names, TABLE_SALES and TABLE_REGION as physical table names, and lower_snake_case for fields and measures. Correct exercise answers save progress in this browser when local storage is available. Choose Resume learning in the chapter menu to find the next incomplete chapter. Progress stays on this device; the page sends no learner data to a server. Choose Reset progress at the bottom of the chapter menu to clear completed exercises. If browser storage is unavailable, the lessons still work for the current page session."}];

const q=document.querySelector('#q'), results=document.querySelector('#results'), count=document.querySelector('#count');
const synonyms={'同比':'yoy year','上月':'pp previous','零分母':'divide zero','重复键':'dimension_not_unique','临时指标':'inline measure','物化':'materialization','缺失值':'null fill','复合键':'compound key','有效期':'range valid_from','累计':'running ytd','时区':'timezone','effective date':'valid_from range','range lookup':'valid_from pk','compound key':'region pk','running total':'running_sales window','parameters':'jdbc binding','udf':'scalar function','column types':'metadata type','多对多':'many-to-many bridge','many to many':'many-to-many bridge' };
function search(){const term=q.value.trim().toLowerCase(),extra=synonyms[term]||'',tokens=[term,...extra.split(' ').filter(Boolean)];results.replaceChildren();const found=term?documents.map(d=>({...d,score:tokens.reduce((n,t)=>n+(d.title.toLowerCase().includes(t)?5:0)+(d.text.toLowerCase().includes(t)?1:0),0)})).filter(d=>d.score>0).sort((a,b)=>b.score-a.score):[];count.textContent=term?found.length+' 个结果 / results':'输入关键词开始 / Enter a keyword';for(const d of found.slice(0,40)){const li=document.createElement('li'),a=document.createElement('a');a.href=d.url;a.textContent=d.title;li.append(a);results.append(li)}}
q.value=new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('q')||'';q.addEventListener('input',search);document.querySelector('form').addEventListener('submit',e=>{e.preventDefault();history.replaceState(null,'','?q='+encodeURIComponent(q.value));search()});search();